WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.540
Someone was screaming inside the incinerator.

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:06.240
The year was 1975.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.520
The location was a top secret refinery and
waste disposal plant, one of the many front

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:15.180
organizations owned and operated by the SCP
Foundation.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.480
This sprawling facility, separated into three
different huge units, each with a specialized

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.400
task, was located around 75 kilometers outside
of Summer Springs, Colorado.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:33.240
It had operated for several decades without
incident, but today, all hell was about to

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:33.900
break loose.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.440
Alarms were blaring in Unit C, the area of&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.080
the facility that dealt with&nbsp;
destroying non-anomalous

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.640
waste via incineration.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:47.100
A traumatized worker had pulled the emergency
lever near one of the incinerators in a panic

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:53.220
when he’d first heard the loud banging against
the interior walls, and then, those horrific,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.780
pained wails.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.240
That’s when the workers of Unit C came to
a horrifying realization: The screams weren’t

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.880
just coming from one of the incinerators in
Unit C, they were coming from all six.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:11.760
A chorus of absolute agony, echoing up out
of the ash pits.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.080
This was impossible, as far as the Unit C
staff were concerned.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.720
These incinerators were only to be used for
confirmed non-anomalous material - could some

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.440
animals or even some poor workers somehow
fallen into the incinerators?

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.380
But even if they had, what were the odds that
someone had fallen in all six of them at once?

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.540
It was impossible.

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.400
The Site Director was immediately brought
in to authorize an investigation, at which

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.020
point he demanded that at least one of the
furnaces - in this case, Furnace Number 4

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:48.120
- be shut off so researchers and guards could
investigate what was going on inside.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:53.280
It seemed like the obvious course of action,
but for some of the people on site that day,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.220
this would prove to be a fatal mistake.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.900
When the fires were put out, something started
rattling in the disposal chute.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:06.360
There was a scratching and squealing noise,
like something scraping up against the metal.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:11.700
A maintenance worker was ordered to go check
inside, and when he opened the hatch, he let

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:13.380
out a bone-chilling shriek.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:16.020
There was something - or was it someone?

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.540
- climbing back up the disposal chute.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:24.480
It emerged from the darkness, backlit by the
still-burning embers of the incinerator, letting

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.700
out an awful, screeching noise that could
make even a hardened Mobile Task Force operative

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.020
shake with fear.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:37.080
The creature was humanoid, a charred, mutilated
skeleton, reaching out towards the maintenance

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.000
worker with a bony claw.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:44.040
Before the worker had a chance to stumble
back in horror, the beast had clasped him

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.020
by the throat and started to squeeze.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.180
It didn’t even need to strangle him - the
heat of its skeletal hand burned away the

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:56.220
worker’s throat in seconds, leaving his
limp, wheezing body to drop to the metal grate

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.900
of the platform below him.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:02.520
Several armed guards and maintenance workers
moved forward to fend off the monster, but

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.840
just then a volley of more violent shrieks
echoed out of the chute behind it.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.820
It only took seconds for over a dozen more
creatures to crawl out of the disposal chute.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:17.940
They were all like the first - people at various
stages of incineration, some were glowing,

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:24.660
charred skeletons with barely any flesh, others
looking more like severe burn victims, their

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.320
faces contorted into scowls of hate and rage.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.900
The assembled guards opened fire on the sudden
influx of anomalies, emptying the magazines

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.000
of their submachine guns into the burning
bodies, but their weapons didn’t seem to

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.980
cause any lasting damage.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:45.420
The bullets would tear through the flesh of
the creatures, causing some of them to stumble

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.800
and fall, but the wounds would close and the
creatures would rise again.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:54.900
It seemed they possessed a deadly combo of
extremely high aggression, and incredible

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.340
regenerative abilities.

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.860
None of the staff present in Unit C were safe
as the burned corpses attacked anyone they

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:06.240
could find with their bare hands, beating
and biting and clawing.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.920
Many of them would grapple with Foundation
personnel, latching on to them and trying

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:13.920
to pull them back down into the incinerator
with them.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:20.940
These creatures that would later be known
as SCP-2419-A caused several fatalities with

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:26.220
their surprise attack that day, and the situation
was only brought back under control when a

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.140
Mobile Task Force that possessed more advanced
training and weaponry was dispatched to the

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:31.320
location.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:37.320
Despite orders from the Site Director, the
MTF found it impossible to actually terminate

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:40.800
any of the entities due to their regenerative
abilities.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:46.800
Instead, the MTF agents focused on forcing
the anomalies back into the chute of Incinerator

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.280
Number 4 using a powerful industrial steam
lance.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.900
Once they were back inside, the incinerator’s
burners were started up once again.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.060
The MTF were also able to capture five of
the entities and detain them for questioning

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.400
and experimentation, finally bringing this
unexpected containment breach to an end.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:10.500
The Foundation is an organization that prides
itself on possessing forbidden and secret

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.220
knowledge, but even they had no idea what
had just happened.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:19.620
There seemed to be no earthly explanation
why these creatures would suddenly manifest

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:25.800
inside the incinerators, but as the detained
anomalies continued regenerating, a slightly

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.660
clearer picture of what exactly the Foundation
was dealing with began to emerge.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.640
Every single one of the captured anomalies
kept regenerating, and upon getting to the

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.680
point that they were no-longer burn scarred,
the researchers studying the anomalous creatures

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.540
discovered that each one was identical to
a deceased D-Class from the Foundation’s

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:46.140
records.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:50.760
They were physically and genetically identical
to the dead Class D Personnel.

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:56.100
Perfect copies, save for the fact that their
sole mission in life seemed to now be pure

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.920
and unmitigated aggression and violence.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:03.360
Researchers wanted to gain greater insight
into the minds of these strange creatures

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.500
through the use of extensive psychological
evaluations and interviews.

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:11.400
It would turn out to be a&nbsp;
very dangerous investigation.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.380
A psychological researcher by the name of
Dr. Warren conducted an interview with one

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:21.780
of the SCP-2419-A instances in hopes of better
understanding their mindset.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:27.300
This creature had been heard laughing not
long before the interview began, so it wasn’t

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.620
an unreasonable assumption to believe that
it could be capable of speech.

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:36.540
The interviewee was separated from Dr. Warren
by a thick pane of glass to prevent it from

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:39.120
indulging in any of its more violent urges.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:45.660
The problem was that Dr. Warren and his assistant
researchers had severely underestimated what

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:49.020
these creatures were capable of doing to get
what they wanted.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.500
Dr. Warren tried to begin the interview in
good faith.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:57.780
He spoke calmly and politely to the creature
through the glass, attempting to remind it

00:06:57.780 --> 00:06:59.400
of the person it once was.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.860
The anomaly didn’t appear to be paying attention
to the doctor though, instead focusing on

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.060
pulling its own arm apart, and breaking the
bones within.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.040
Dr. Warren began to think that the creatures
weren’t even sentient, if the only activity

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:16.800
they seemed to engage in&nbsp;
was the wanton destruction&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.540
of themselves and others.

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:22.860
But underestimating them like this proved
to be a big mistake.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:28.500
By removing the skin off of its arm, and whittling
its bones down to sharp edges, the anomaly

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.200
had transformed its arm into a makeshift knife.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:36.000
Before anyone realized this, it had already
used its own jagged bones to break the glass

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:38.040
divider and attack Dr. Warren.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:43.560
He perished not long after, after being stabbed
multiple times in his face and eyes and the

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.380
anomaly was forcefully taken back to its holding
cell.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.760
Dr. Daniel Jennings, a senior researcher,
was then put on the case.

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.140
He released a chilling memo on the creatures
after his observation period ended.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:55.860
It read:

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.860
“Before I came here, I worked as a prison
shrink.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.880
Every person you met had a story bursting
with pain and sorrow.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.800
Sometimes, those stories were about the pain
they suffered; sometimes, it was about the

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.180
pain they inflicted.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:14.670
Some days you felt like everyone there was
a beautiful soul torn down by their circumstances

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:19.980
— other days, you'd find out what some of
them did and a part of you was glad they were

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.120
there to suffer for it.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.040
But at the end of every day, I always told
myself: They're all humans.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:27.420
They're all people.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:32.520
They all deserve the same dignity, respect,
and love as everyone else.

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:34.380
No exceptions.”

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.180
Dr. Jennings goes on.

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.800
“I opened up with that anecdote only so
you could understand that what I'm about to

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.300
say is not said lightly: These things are
not people.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:48.660
They are people-shaped monsters.

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:52.020
They are well beyond any&nbsp;
definition of psychopathy.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:56.940
Everything they do, they do to hurt, maim,
and kill.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:01.200
I would pity them, but that would imply that
they're worth pitying.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.160
Put them in a hole, then fill that hole with
concrete.

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.240
Better still: Throw them back into the incinerator
where you found them.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.340
I doubt they'll even care.”

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:16.260
Following this string of unfortunate incidents,
containment procedures were put into place

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:21.120
to hopefully prevent anything that had happened
to the unlucky Foundation staff from happening

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:21.420
again.

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:27.840
The entire facility was fenced off, and was
constantly patrolled by MTF Beta-7, also known

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:33.000
as the Maz Hatters, a group specializing in
containing anomalies which present powerful

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.360
biological, chemical, or radiological dangers.

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:41.760
The furnaces that the anomalous creatures
emerged from remain lit at all times, with

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.860
workers checking and carrying out maintenance
on every single one, each day of the week.

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.940
Outside of this, the facility is essentially
a no-go zone.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:56.340
Reinforced steel hatches cover the chutes,
and they are to forever remain bolted shut.

00:09:56.340 --> 00:10:01.620
All because of that one terrifying day in
1975.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:07.440
That was the day that the Summer Springs waste
disposal facility, which had been a valuable

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:13.440
asset to the Foundation for decades, instead
became classified as the Euclid-Class anomaly

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:15.840
SCP-2419.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.300
It’s not uncommon for a Foundation asset
to one day become a dangerous anomaly requiring

00:10:21.300 --> 00:10:24.540
containment, but the same question always
pops up after...

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:25.500
Why?

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:30.480
Why had a place that had been so valuable
to the Foundation suddenly become such an

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:31.320
active danger?

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:36.000
The answer, as is often the case, is contained
within the question.

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:40.920
Why would a waste disposal plant be so important
to the Foundation in the first place?

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:46.320
And more importantly, what else was going
on at the plant to make it such an area of

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:51.420
active importance for one of the most powerful
organizations in the entire world?

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:57.180
To put it simply, the plant allowed the Foundation
to dispose of something far more important

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:02.820
than mere waste material - it gave them what
they needed to better erase memories.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:08.460
As you probably already know, the Foundation
makes liberal use of amnestics on those exposed

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:14.460
to anomalous material to make them forget
what they have seen and experienced, but these

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:20.040
elusive chemicals - many of which are sourced
from SCP-3000 - are an inexact science.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:25.200
However, at the Summer Springs waste disposal
plant, researchers discovered an immensely

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:26.700
valuable alternative.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:32.220
Much of what was disposed of in the incinerators
of Unit C were the bodies of dead D-Class

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:32.760
personnel.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:38.190
Before being burned, autopsies were performed
on the bodies to make sure they were non-anomalous

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:40.140
- including the brain.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:45.480
Back in the 1960s, while performing research
on the brains of the dead D-Classes, they

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:50.340
found a method of extracting and distilling
the positive memories of the dead, creating

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:52.260
a kind of “Happy Soup.”

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:58.200
When administered into someone who has undergone
amnestic treatment, this happy soup creates

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:03.000
positive false memories in their mind that
fills in those pesky blank spaces that are

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:04.260
often behind.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:09.660
This was so useful that happy memories were
extracted from the minds of every single D-Class

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:14.700
corpse that passed through Unit C, totaling
well into the thousands by the time that the

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.960
first SCP-2419-A instances began to rise.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:24.900
But as all happiness, joy, and love were removed
from the minds of the D-Classes, it seemed

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:29.940
that all that was left was pure hate, pain,
violence, and fury.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:33.960
Feelings too intense and evil to destroy.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:39.660
These were bodies imbued with a malice so
powerful that no fire could ever burn them

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:39.960
away.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:45.300
They exist now with single-minded determination
to get out and destroy everything around them.

00:12:45.300 --> 00:12:46.080
Everything.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:51.480
Including the ones that made them, the SCP
Foundation itself.

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:55.620
And in the end, it’s only a matter of time
until they get out.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:01.740
There are thousands of them in there, unable
to die, unable to stop.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:07.020
The only thing keeping them in there is the
constant burning of the furnaces around them,

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:09.780
and those furnaces can’t burn forever.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:16.260
The monsters lurking in SCP-2419 will outlive
the fire that surrounds them, because while

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:22.620
the furnaces they are contained in are hot,
the fires of hate that burn within SCP2419

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.140
entities are even more powerful.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:26.520
And they know this.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:32.460
They know they can’t be contained forever,
and that someday soon, they’ll be able to

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:35.700
emerge and exact their terrible revenge.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:40.620
Those who must walk past the incinerators
may think they hear their screams echoing

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:43.740
out from within, but it’s not screaming
at all.

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:45.060
It’s laughter.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:50.640
It’s a natural instinct in many species
to protect their young - and sometimes it's

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:54.300
not just their own babies, but any young that
look like them.

00:13:54.300 --> 00:14:00.660
Regardless of your personal feelings on human’s
earliest stage, it is a scientific fact that

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:05.940
human babies are designed to emphasize their
own adorable helplessness to make sure that

00:14:05.940 --> 00:14:08.280
other, older humans take care of them.

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:14.280
A baby’s cry is an inherently distressing
sound, and when we hear it, some deep, primal

00:14:14.280 --> 00:14:19.320
part of us feels the urge to comfort and care
for the child until the sound stops.

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:24.660
But of course, we’re talking about the world
of the SCP Foundation here - where there are

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:29.280
bunnies that can eat anything, teddy bears
that might steal your organs to make duplicates

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:34.440
of themselves, and chocolate fountains filled
with trillions of murderous insects.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:40.440
Nothing is what it seems here, and even the
most innocent and cute creatures may be hiding

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:45.540
a deadly secret - SCP-734 being no exception.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:47.640
Today, we’re talking about The Baby.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:53.580
This anomaly proves that dangerous things
can come in small packages, but where others

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:59.700
only saw misery and death, the SCP Foundation
saw a certain potential…

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:04.920
Our story begins in the maternity ward of
a hospital in the USA, where every medical

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:10.440
professional’s worst nightmare was unfolding:
An unknown but incredibly fast-acting flesh-eating

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:13.920
pathogen seemed to be running rampant across
the hospital’s population.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:18.960
It began when patients who weren’t even
admitted for dermatological issues started

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:22.140
complaining of severe itches and extreme skin
pain.

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:27.720
Some nurses and doctors in the maternity ward
began to experience similar symptoms, as well

00:15:27.720 --> 00:15:31.620
as one of the infants, leading to a massive
quarantine effort.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:36.060
While the initial symptoms first appeared
to be limited to severe pain and discomfort

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:40.680
on isolated parts of the skin, the condition
of the afflicted soon escalated.

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:46.320
Their skin began to messily flake off as the
cells comprising it lost physical cohesion

00:15:46.320 --> 00:15:47.160
and died.

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:53.400
It was a kind of strange, anomalous rot that
seemed to work inwards, first destroying the

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:58.200
integrity of the outer layer of skin and then
causing further disintegration to deeper parts

00:15:58.200 --> 00:15:59.040
of the body.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:04.800
Once the skin had shed off, the pathogen would
turn its attention to what was lying beneath.

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:11.700
Against all odds, the disease affected organs,
muscle tissue, the vascular system, and even

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:12.360
bones.

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:14.640
Nothing was spared.

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:19.500
The victims’ bodies would completely break
down, and they would be dead within a few

00:16:19.500 --> 00:16:19.980
hours.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:25.260
By the time the pathogen had finished ravaging
its victims, what was left didn’t even look

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:25.980
human anymore.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:31.500
Not only patients but doctors and nurses that
had been walking the wards mere hours before

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:36.540
were now little more than piles of human tissue
sitting in what had previously been their

00:16:36.540 --> 00:16:37.440
hospital beds.

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:43.260
Whatever this was, it seemed like the fastest
progressing infection in recorded history.

00:16:43.260 --> 00:16:49.680
Hospital staff and administrators were terrified
- this appeared to be an entirely new disease,

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.780
with a mysterious form of transmission and,
worst of all, no cure.

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:59.280
They were even more confused when several
black vans pulled up outside the hospital,

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:04.140
and mysterious men in hazmat suits spilled
out and began setting up a quarantine zone

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:05.400
around the entire building.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:10.980
Whoever these strange people were, they definitely
weren’t the CDC - little did the hospital

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:15.420
personnel know that this was the group you
never want showing up and putting your location

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:19.200
on lockdown - it was the SCP Foundation.

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:24.300
The infected remains of the victims were taken
away for research purposes, and the Foundation

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:28.380
operatives immediately began conducting debriefing
interviews with witnesses.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:33.480
They soon determined from a mix of eyewitness
accounts and hospital surveillance footage

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:38.280
that everyone infected with the mysterious
pathogen had all been in a particular section

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.620
of the maternity ward earlier in the day.

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:45.900
And when the operatives investigated this
area, they found another strange detail.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:50.040
An infant had no registered mother present
in the hospital.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:54.780
The Foundation would later learn that this
was likely because the baby’s mother was

00:17:54.780 --> 00:17:56.940
the first victim of the deadly disease.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:02.700
After eliminating all other possible options,
it seemed that the only anomalous element

00:18:02.700 --> 00:18:07.620
that could have caused all of this was the
mysterious baby, though just how the mother

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:12.300
even survived carrying the baby to term was
a mystery in and of itself.

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:17.340
The baby was a caucasian male human infant
between seven and eight months of age.

00:18:17.340 --> 00:18:22.500
Nothing appeared outwardly anomalous about
the child, but they could confirm that every

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:26.760
single victim of the anomalous pathogen had
come into physical contact with the child

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:27.960
earlier that day.

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:32.580
Everyone who had been at the hospital was
given amnestic treatment, and the Foundation

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:36.420
constructed plausible cover stories for the
deaths that had occurred that day.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.480
Like so many others before it, the child was&nbsp;

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:42.120
secured and spirited away&nbsp;
to the nearest applicable

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:43.740
Foundation containment site.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:50.280
There it was reclassified as SCP-734, and
finally the real testing into what precisely

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:52.560
this infant was capable of could begin.

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:57.600
Physiologically, SCP-734&nbsp;
appeared mostly non-anomalous.

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:02.460
The infant had above-average intelligence
and physical aptitude for a child his age

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:07.920
but otherwise showed no mutations or abnormalities
that would suggest a divergence from typical

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:08.820
human biology.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:13.920
Despite a vast array of tests performed by
the Foundation into essentially every aspect

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:19.500
of 734’s biology, they couldn’t find anything
that hinted at the origin of the mysterious

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:20.220
pathogen.

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:25.980
But through some trial and extremely costly
error, the Foundation was able to learn more

00:19:25.980 --> 00:19:28.560
about how exactly the pathogen worked.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:33.780
The only vector for transmission seemed to
be direct contact with the baby itself, up

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:37.200
to and including the fluids and residues it
leaves behind.

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:41.820
Those affected cannot transmit the anomalous
“flaking” effect to others, meaning that

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:44.640
the risk of an epidemic is relievingly minimal.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:50.100
But of course, accidents still happen, as
was discovered when one agent misplaced her

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:54.480
sympathy for the baby and decided to remove
her mask while interacting with him.

00:19:54.480 --> 00:20:00.060
Her logic was that her masked face might have
caused the baby some kind of distress, and

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:04.860
as long as she was only coming into contact
with him via her own gloved hands, everything

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:05.880
would be fine.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:12.360
But some dust particles were floating in the
air at the time, and SCP-734 sneezed in her

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:12.540
face.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:18.000
The agent began screaming in pain and recoiling
from the baby, but it was already too late.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:20.100
Her fate was sealed.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:24.660
She was taken to the infirmary and given sedatives
so that she could hopefully die in as little

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:25.560
pain as possible.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:31.740
In roughly 72% of cases, amputation of the
affected areas has prevented the entire body

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:37.680
from succumbing to the anomalous effects of
SCP-734, but this isn’t exactly feasible

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.380
when the point of contact is the victim’s
head.

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:45.540
Within hours, the agent’s flesh had flaked
off of her face, leaving her looking like

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:46.920
a red, bloody skull.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:50.700
A few hours after that, she didn’t have
a head at all.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:55.860
Throughout the disintegration process of this
unfortunate agent, the Foundation took grisly

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:56.640
photographs.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:21:03.420
These photographs are shown to anyone preparing
to work on the SCP-734 research project to

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:07.980
teach them a hard lesson on the importance
of adhering to proper safety protocols.

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:13.080
And these protocols about how to deal with
the Baby are incredibly tight, given that

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:16.440
even incidental contact is often a death sentence.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:21.420
Anyone entering the Baby’s containment chamber
needs to wear contained-atmosphere hazmat

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:21.900
suits.

00:21:21.900 --> 00:21:26.640
Anyone who makes physical contact with the
Baby, even when suited up, is immediately

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:31.080
removed from the area and subject to several
hours of quarantine and observation.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:36.240
Even inanimate objects that have been inside
the containment chamber need to be thoroughly

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.520
sterilized before being removed.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:45.240
Given that even anomalous children like SCP-734
have very delicate needs, a handler is always

00:21:45.240 --> 00:21:48.540
standing by in full hazmat gear to take care
of the baby.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:53.280
These handlers are rotated every hour to maintain
alertness and safety.

00:21:53.280 --> 00:22:00.120
These handlers feed and change SCP-734 regularly
and sometimes even provide toys that have

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:02.040
been approved by the O5 Council.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:07.740
The Foundation has gone to great lengths to
keep SCP-734 alive and comfortable because

00:22:07.740 --> 00:22:12.600
they believe that SCP-734 could be an important
asset for them in the future.

00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:19.440
Or, more specifically, they see great strategic
value in SCP-734’s blood.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:25.500
As mentioned earlier, contact with any matter
from SCP-734 triggers its deadly anomalous

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:28.320
effects, including bodily fluids like blood.

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:34.020
But, unlike many toxic pathogens, in this
case there is no risk of the infection getting

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:37.980
out of control since the infected do not become
vectors for transmission.

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:43.620
Because of this, the blood of SCP-734 can
act as a powerful weapon for both terminating

00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:49.440
anomalies deemed unviable by the O5 Council
and assassinating dangerous people who belong

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:50.640
to rival groups.

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:55.740
Contact with the blood would completely destroy
the target’s body, with no other anomalous

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:59.520
effects and no evidence of who exactly conducted
the hit.

00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:03.240
SCP-734 has been fitted with an arterial catheter&nbsp;

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:05.880
so that the Foundation can&nbsp;
collect large quantities

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:10.260
of blood from the anomalous infant every single
week for storage and research.

00:23:10.260 --> 00:23:13.860
But it isn’t just the blood that the Foundation
sees potential in.

00:23:13.860 --> 00:23:20.820
SCP-734 himself has scored incredibly well
on the Aeslinger Loyalty Index, the test the

00:23:20.820 --> 00:23:25.560
Foundation uses to judge the loyalty of potential
applicants to the Foundation cause.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:32.340
As SCP-734 matures, if these scores remain
consistent, he will likely be trained to become

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:34.260
a Foundation field agent in the future.

00:23:34.260 --> 00:23:39.900
An agent capable of killing someone with a
touch would, needless to say, be an asset

00:23:39.900 --> 00:23:44.640
to certain covert missions that the Foundation
would probably prefer to keep off the books.

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:49.800
Of course, the SCP Foundation recruiting certain
compliant anomalies to perform missions for

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:51.600
them is far from unprecedented.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:58.080
The most infamous of these is SCP-076, also
known as Able, a supernatural,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.860 --> 00:23:59.760
eternally-resurrecting

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:03.300
swordsman and perhaps one of the finest warriors
who ever lived.

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:07.680
The Foundation took note of this and channeled
his eternal thirst for battle into working

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:09.840
with his very own Mobile Task Force.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:14.160
He could assist in missions by taking out
his bloodlust on enemy groups of interest

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:16.080
and dangerous anomalies.

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:20.580
However, this ended up working a little too
well and led to disaster.

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:25.980
Able was so good at his job that he burned
through all his allotted missions at astonishing

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:26.280
speed.

00:24:26.280 --> 00:24:31.980
Left with nothing more to do, Able got antsy,
which led to him turning his murderous desires

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:36.000
on his fellow team members and then the Foundation
as a whole.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:41.220
It was an all-out massacre before his colleagues
were finally able to kill him and return him

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:42.000
to containment.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:46.980
The failure of this initiative gave the very
idea of letting anomalies work for the Foundation

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:48.780
in the field a bad name.

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:55.200
But since then, things have changed, especially
with the establishment of MTF Alpha-9, aka

00:24:55.200 --> 00:25:01.020
Last Hope, a new mobile task force formed
entirely of anomalous individuals and their

00:25:01.020 --> 00:25:01.500
handlers.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:08.820
These consist of somewhat more stable anomalies,
such as Able’s counterpart, SCP-073, also

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:09.720
known as Cain.

00:25:09.720 --> 00:25:15.240
Cain cannot be harmed, with any harm befalling
him simply being mirrored back on his attacker,

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:19.800
and his vast array of knowledge makes him
an incredibly useful intelligence asset.

00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:25.440
Another of the several members of Last Hope
is Iris, also known as SCP-105.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:30.900
Much like Cain, Iris not only has anomalous
powers that make her immensely valuable for

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:36.360
missions in the field, she’s also compliant
and capable of listening to reason, unlike

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:36.840
Cain.

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:42.120
Her abilities include being able to actively
surveil and even interact with photos of any

00:25:42.120 --> 00:25:45.480
location, especially when taken with her anomalous
camera.

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:51.600
Someday, SCP-734 could count himself among
other anomalous Foundation agents like these,

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:56.460
providing he continues to show promise and
doesn’t develop a dislike of the Foundation

00:25:56.460 --> 00:26:00.600
as he ages, since after all, he won’t be
a baby forever.

00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:06.540
So if ever you find yourself on the Foundation’s
bad side, you have a good reason to be paranoid.

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:11.580
You’ll have no idea that one day, the man
who’s going to kill you will approach and

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.200
shake your hand with a smile.

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:18.540
It won’t be until later when you feel that
strange tingle on your palm and feel the skin

00:26:18.540 --> 00:26:23.820
starting to flake away that you’ll realize
you’ve been a dead man walking for hours.

00:26:23.820 --> 00:26:25.140
Endless.

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:30.000
That’s a word you’re likely to hear thrown
around a lot at the SCP Foundation.

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.320
Like SCP-3008, known as the Infinite Ikea.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:35.220
Endless.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:41.580
Or take the life of the tragically immortal
Dr. Bright, who became one with the SCP-963,

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:45.780
and now is condemned to existing within it
on some level forever.

00:26:45.780 --> 00:26:46.800
Endless.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:54.180
Or most pertinent of all for today’s video,
take SCP-087, a terrifying staircase full

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:58.200
of tangible darkness and a frightening and
unknowable entity.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:04.260
No matter how far down you go, it seems as
though there’s just another flight, waiting

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:04.680
for you.

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:05.760
Endless.

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:08.940
But really, is anything ever really endless?

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:13.680
Or is endless just the word we use for something
when we haven’t found that sought-after

00:27:13.680 --> 00:27:14.760
end just yet?

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:19.260
For those who travel far enough, there may
be a limit to the infinite Ikea.

00:27:19.260 --> 00:27:24.960
Someday, Dr. Bright’s medallion one day
might lay inert and lifeless, with no traces

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:26.220
of his consciousness left.

00:27:26.220 --> 00:27:31.380
And for those who venture down far enough,
able to clench their teeth and get past the

00:27:31.380 --> 00:27:36.960
fear, you may someday find that even SCP-087
has a final flight.

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:39.480
But what if this isn’t just a hypothetical.

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:44.760
What if this has already happened, but the
truth was considered too dangerous or too

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:45.960
horrifying to share?

00:27:45.960 --> 00:27:49.380
What’s really at the bottom of SCP-087?

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:51.420
Today, we hope to find out.

00:27:51.420 --> 00:27:56.220
Let’s just also hope that knowing doesn’t
end up being more of a burden than we’d

00:27:56.220 --> 00:27:56.820
expected…

00:27:56.820 --> 00:28:01.320
Before we head to the bottom of the Endless
Staircase though, we must first return to

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:05.640
SCP-087’s file and refresh our memories
on some of the crucial basics.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:09.540
After all, it’s been quite some time since
our last video on the subject.

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:15.180
This Euclid-Class anomaly is one of the most
well-known and feared, partially thanks to

00:28:15.180 --> 00:28:20.100
classified information being leaked via a
certain video game, probably coded by the

00:28:20.100 --> 00:28:20.880
Serpent’s Hand.

00:28:20.880 --> 00:28:27.600
As we alluded to earlier, many know 087 as
the Endless Staircase, based in an administrative

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:30.960
building of a classified North American university
campus.

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:36.060
It’s been behind a number of mysterious
deaths and disappearances and has left many

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:40.260
of its escaping victims traumatized and in
a state of permanent catatonia.

00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:44.280
Having to descend the staircase is just that
terrifying.

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:49.800
Equally infamous as the staircase itself are
the three tests that the Foundation conducted

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:53.460
into the staircase before deciding to just
board up the door forever.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:58.920
In the first, a D-Class that was once a member
of the Foundation was forced to see just how

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.240
many flights he could descend.

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:04.980
Not long after first entering the staircase
and beginning to make his way down, he heard

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:07.560
the desperate and distressed pleas of a child.

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:12.240
He kept descending but found he seemed to
be getting no closer to the source of the

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:12.540
distress.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:20.160
When he was deep enough, that’s when he
encountered SCP-087-1, a frightening, uncanny

00:29:20.160 --> 00:29:22.440
face with few discernible features.

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:27.120
The sheer terror of encountering this entity
was enough to send him running back to the

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:27.300
top.

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:32.700
The second test played out similarly, with
a D-Class being sent in with a number of powerful,

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:37.200
sticky-backed lights that he was ordered to
place against the walls as he descended.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:40.920
This was done in hopes of increasing visibility
on the staircase.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:46.740
However, it proved to be unsuccessful, as
the darkness in 087 is more of a tangible

00:29:46.740 --> 00:29:49.080
presence than the mere absence of light.

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:53.400
It was able to smother out visibility regardless
of a light source’s power.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:59.820
Once again, the D-Class heard the crying,
and encountered the face, causing him to flee.

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:04.620
And the third and penultimate test involved
a D-Class who got really unlucky.

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:07.320
She traversed the staircase deeper than any&nbsp;

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:10.200
of her predecessors, before&nbsp;
eventually encountering

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:13.020
the horrifying face of SCP-087-1.

00:30:13.020 --> 00:30:18.720
However, rather than running up and away from
the entity, in a state of sheer panic, the

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:23.640
D-Class instead ran downwards, deeper and
deeper into the staircase.

00:30:23.640 --> 00:30:29.640
The audio cut as the face continued in its
pursuit, and the D-Class was never seen again.

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:34.380
Did you notice that we said “penultimate”
and not “final” before that last one?

00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:40.560
That’s because there’s actually a highly
classified fourth expedition into the SCP-087

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:45.780
Staircase, but as you can see on the original
file, the data is expunged.

00:30:45.780 --> 00:30:50.520
What could have happened in that study that
led the Foundation to wipe it from the records?

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:56.760
Did they discover the great secret behind
SCP-087, a secret too horrible for mortal

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:58.020
minds to bear?

00:30:58.020 --> 00:31:02.400
We’re not just going to give you one answer
today, we’re going to give you three.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:09.480
That’s right: Three different interpretations
of the secret fourth expedition into SCP-087.

00:31:09.480 --> 00:31:10.800
Which is the real one?

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:12.420
Well you’ll just have to see.

00:31:12.420 --> 00:31:17.760
After all, nothing is simple when it comes
to SCP-087.

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:21.480
Though our first potential option is the most
simple of the three.

00:31:21.480 --> 00:31:26.760
In this iteration of the experiment, a male
D-Class murderer was instructed to descend

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:32.460
the staircase into the heart of SCP-087, with
the Foundation hoping to find out what happened

00:31:32.460 --> 00:31:38.580
to D-9884, the female D-Class who’d disappeared
at the conclusion of the third expedition.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:44.820
In a sense, this new D-Class would discover
the fate that most likely befell D-9884, but

00:31:44.820 --> 00:31:48.300
through first-hand experience rather than
distant observation.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:54.240
Being directed by Dr. Airdo over walkie-talkie,
the male D-Class slowly got deeper into the

00:31:54.240 --> 00:31:56.220
anomaly, flight by flight.

00:31:56.220 --> 00:32:01.080
Predictably, he began to hear the intensely
distressed crying of a small child around

00:32:01.080 --> 00:32:06.840
200 meters below him and immediately felt
a sense of paranoia and dread wash over his

00:32:06.840 --> 00:32:07.140
mind.

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:13.200
Despite this, Dr. Airdo ordered him to keep
going, descending deeper and deeper into the

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:15.480
bowels of SCP-087.

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:21.480
Over time, the sheer psychological exhaustion
of SCP-087 was beginning to take its toll

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:25.380
on the D-Class, leading to him taking a brief
break in order to nap.

00:32:25.380 --> 00:32:32.460
However, he awoke with a violent start, seeing
the horrific face of SCP-087-1 staring down

00:32:32.460 --> 00:32:32.880
at him.

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:36.480
It felt like all his worst nightmares had
come to life.

00:32:36.480 --> 00:32:42.900
In order to escape the monster, he began to
flee further down into SCP-087, much like

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:45.000
D-9884 before him.

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:48.180
It would be the last mistake he ever made.

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:53.820
When he finally reached the very bottom of
the 087 staircases, the D-Class realized he

00:32:53.820 --> 00:32:54.540
was trapped.

00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:57.360
Nowhere to run, nowhere to hide.

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:04.020
Suddenly, the face of SCP-087-1 was hovering
over him again, boring its gaze into him.

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:09.420
As the D-Class directed his powerful flashlight
at the creature, he saw that this time, it

00:33:09.420 --> 00:33:11.160
was more than just a face.

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:18.060
The entity had a whole body, seven or eight
feet long, with that same ghostly white and

00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:19.260
deathlike appearance.

00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:23.040
It reached out with huge, clawed hands and
grasped the D-Class.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:28.080
Before the audio and video feed cut out entirely,
the last thing the attending researchers heard

00:33:28.080 --> 00:33:33.000
was screaming from the hapless D-Class, and
the crunch of his bones within the creature’s

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:34.020
huge hands.

00:33:34.020 --> 00:33:39.000
But of course, that’s just one of three
possible insane discoveries at the very bottom

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:40.740
of SCP-087.

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:46.740
Let’s turn our attention to the next one,
and see if its answer to the SCP-087 riddle

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:49.320
seems to satisfy all of our burning questions.

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:54.840
Much like the previous iteration, a single
male D-Class was sent into the clutches of

00:33:54.840 --> 00:34:00.660
SCP-087 in hopes of getting some answers on
what happened to D-9884.

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:06.120
Of course, he was given all the standard equipment
- from a 75-watt flashlight to a defensive

00:34:06.120 --> 00:34:09.720
weapon to a healthy supply of water and nutrition
bars.

00:34:09.720 --> 00:34:12.000
None of which would save him in the end.

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:18.780
Like all the others, this D-Class descended
those accursed stairs, getting deeper and

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:19.260
deeper.

00:34:19.260 --> 00:34:24.720
When he reached a certain point on the way
down, he heard something, but not the crying

00:34:24.720 --> 00:34:26.820
of a child like all the others had reported.

00:34:26.820 --> 00:34:29.580
It was an adult woman’s scream.

00:34:29.580 --> 00:34:36.720
Specifically, it was the scream of D-9884,
exactly as the Foundation researchers had

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:40.740
heard it over the audio feed in her last moments
before disappearing.

00:34:40.740 --> 00:34:42.360
This was progress.

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:47.520
The researchers instructed this male D-Class
to go deeper, passing flight after flight,

00:34:47.520 --> 00:34:51.600
until finally the SCP-087-1 face appeared.

00:34:51.600 --> 00:34:55.920
But again, something was different, something
was wrong.

00:34:55.920 --> 00:35:01.920
Now, it looked more like the face of D-9884,
just without her mouth and pupils.

00:35:01.920 --> 00:35:06.720
Had SCP-087-1 been stealing the faces of its
prior victims?

00:35:06.720 --> 00:35:11.160
Had the previous face been stolen from a student
who’d gone missing from the campus?

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:16.620
These were all questions the male D-Class
didn’t have time to ponder, as he was too

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:18.240
busy running for dear life.

00:35:18.240 --> 00:35:19.920
But he didn’t get far.

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:25.140
Despite having no history of such medical
problems, the male D-Class suddenly experienced

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:28.440
a seizure while attempting to escape, and
collapsed.

00:35:28.440 --> 00:35:33.240
The last thing his video feed picked up before
losing transmission was a pair of long, white

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:39.120
arms emerging from the dark, crushing him
inside of its huge hands, before dragging

00:35:39.120 --> 00:35:41.220
his enfolded corpse into the darkness.

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:47.340
That’s another chilling potential account
of the possible fourth expedition into SCP-087,

00:35:47.340 --> 00:35:49.080
but we can’t stop now.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:53.940
We still have one more tale of terror to share
before this video is done.

00:35:53.940 --> 00:36:00.720
In the next and final iteration, two D-Classes
- one male and one female - were sent down

00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:04.620
together in hopes of discovering what had
happened to D-9884.

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:10.140
And by the end, they would have their answer,
but they certainly wouldn’t like it.

00:36:10.140 --> 00:36:14.760
As the two of them descended, they saw the
marks on the wall where the adhesive lights

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:17.640
from expedition two had been somehow torn
off.

00:36:17.640 --> 00:36:22.500
But the deeper they descended, the more disturbing
the things they found became.

00:36:22.500 --> 00:36:28.020
They discovered words clawed into the stone
walls, each one revealing a piece of a more

00:36:28.020 --> 00:36:32.040
frightening puzzle the deeper down SCP-087
they traversed.

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:37.380
They descended further, sustaining minor injuries
from a foul-smelling and caustic substance

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:39.960
left on some of the walls and floors around
them.

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:45.300
At some point along the journey, a light was
dropped, and in the brief seconds of darkness,

00:36:45.300 --> 00:36:47.760
the female D-Class disappeared.

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:52.500
The male D-Class was ordered to go deeper
and deeper into the staircase until he finally

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:55.020
began to hear the crying of a little girl.

00:36:55.020 --> 00:37:02.460
When he reached the 666th floor, he found
a wooden door, with the word “HOME” scratched

00:37:02.460 --> 00:37:02.880
into it.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:05.760
He steeled himself and entered the room.

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:11.880
But it wasn’t a room at all, it was a field,
shrouded in darkness with a distant tree.

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:17.160
A little girl - the one who had been crying
- was finally, actually here.

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:22.080
She told the D-Class that she’d always been
here, and her father had been trying to find

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:27.420
her a body so she could go “upstairs”,
but for that to happen, she’d have to be

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:28.740
properly birthed first.

00:37:28.740 --> 00:37:34.500
That’s when the male D-Class noticed the
two huge stone slabs just beyond the darkness:

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:41.100
On one was the corpse of D-9884, hanged by
her wrists with thick, metal chains.

00:37:41.100 --> 00:37:46.500
Her abdomen had been fully cut open, as though
a brutal c-section had been performed on her.

00:37:46.500 --> 00:37:51.720
And on the other stone was the female D-Class
who’d been accompanying him this whole time,

00:37:51.720 --> 00:37:57.720
strung up but still very much alive - seemingly
the next potential vessel for the little girl’s

00:37:57.720 --> 00:37:58.740
rebirth.

00:37:58.740 --> 00:38:02.820
But this didn’t mean that the male D-Class
would be left out of the fun, either.

00:38:02.820 --> 00:38:08.580
After all, the little girl’s father would
need a new body, too, if he wanted to accompany

00:38:08.580 --> 00:38:09.480
his daughter upstairs.

00:38:09.480 --> 00:38:17.280
The father in question being SCP-087-1, which
appeared in the room moments later and swooped

00:38:17.280 --> 00:38:19.020
in towards the male D-Class.

00:38:19.020 --> 00:38:24.060
What followed was a battery of nightmarish
sounds as the darkness expanded and consumed

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:25.440
the field once more.

00:38:25.440 --> 00:38:32.220
Not long after this, the Foundation sealed
off the entrance to SCP-087 forever, now knowing

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:37.140
that it potentially contained multiple hostile
entities intent on escaping into our world

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:38.880
and causing further damage.

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:43.380
Students in the area reported a number of
frantic knocks coming from the door for a

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:48.600
brief period after that until the Foundation
finally had it properly soundproofed, and

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:50.460
the issues were seemingly over with.

00:38:50.460 --> 00:38:51.600
For now.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:54.240
Everyone loves a good ghost story.

00:38:54.240 --> 00:38:59.580
Whether you’re crowded around a campfire
or watching a YouTube video at 3 AM, there’s

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:05.040
something fun and exciting about imagining
haunted places plagued by strange noises and

00:39:05.040 --> 00:39:05.820
apparitions.

00:39:05.820 --> 00:39:11.520
Part of what makes it so fun is the idea that
any place can be haunted, especially if terrible

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:13.020
things have happened there.

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:17.940
That decrepit old house at the end of your
street, that 80 year old movie theater where

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:22.020
no one goes anymore, a park where one too
many kids went missing.

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:27.960
All it takes is a tragic backstory and a creepy
atmosphere, and it’s easy to believe that

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:31.260
any place could be teeming with restless spirits.

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:37.380
So, it stands to reason that a place with
as many unsolved deaths as the SCP Foundation

00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:40.260
might have a ghost or two lurking in the shadows.

00:39:40.260 --> 00:39:46.140
If you’ve ever suspected that the SCP foundation
might be haunted, well…you’d be right.

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:53.460
It began, as all good ghost stories do, with
a series of frightening, unexplained events.

00:39:53.460 --> 00:40:00.000
Some time ago, personnel at SCP Foundation
Site-30 began to notice strange things happening

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:01.020
around the facility.

00:40:01.020 --> 00:40:07.080
Obviously, at a place like an SCP Foundation
Containment Site, strange is relative, but

00:40:07.080 --> 00:40:11.160
these events seemed to have nothing to do
with any of the entities contained at the

00:40:11.160 --> 00:40:11.340
site.

00:40:11.340 --> 00:40:16.080
There were unexplained sounds reported in
the private quarters of agents and researchers,

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:20.220
ranging from whispers to screams from an unseen
being.

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:22.680
Personnel who approached the D-Class block&nbsp;

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:25.260
at the site noticed an&nbsp;
immediate drop in temperature,

00:40:25.260 --> 00:40:29.220
with some employees even claiming that they
could see their own breath.

00:40:29.220 --> 00:40:33.420
Several personnel reported items going missing
while they were alone in a room.

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:39.300
Most were small and easily misplaced objects
like pens and clipboards, but one man said

00:40:39.300 --> 00:40:43.440
his wallet disappeared, which wouldn’t be
that strange if it hadn’t been securely

00:40:43.440 --> 00:40:45.240
inside his lab coat.

00:40:45.240 --> 00:40:47.700
Other personnel would be innocently washing&nbsp;

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:50.580
their hands, only to be&nbsp;
horrified by the appearance

00:40:50.580 --> 00:40:55.800
of a bloody figure in a D-Class jumpsuit in
the mirror, staring back at them with hatred

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:56.580
in its eyes.

00:40:56.580 --> 00:41:01.920
There were anomalous computer server errors,
the feeling of being watched or followed by

00:41:01.920 --> 00:41:07.500
some menacing invisible force, even the sight
of a shadowy silhouette fleeing down corridors

00:41:07.500 --> 00:41:09.300
and hiding around corners.

00:41:09.300 --> 00:41:13.980
All of these occurrences were reported to
higher-ups at the Foundation, but they were

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:14.880
all dismissed.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:19.380
The Directorial Board of the site insisted
that these accounts were likely the result

00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:24.840
of employees cracking under the mental stress
of working under such strenuous circumstances.

00:41:24.840 --> 00:41:28.560
If left alone, they were certain that the
reports would stop.

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:33.960
But the reports didn’t stop, and they soon
found out that this was only the beginning.

00:41:33.960 --> 00:41:37.980
There was an uptick in containment breaches
following these initial reports.

00:41:37.980 --> 00:41:42.840
In total, twelve different entities were released
from their containment, and the origin of

00:41:42.840 --> 00:41:44.700
these breaches could not be identified.

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:49.320
Thankfully, the entities were captured and
returned to their containment facilities before

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:50.820
any casualties could result.

00:41:50.820 --> 00:41:53.760
But from there things kept getting worse.

00:41:53.760 --> 00:41:59.040
Over the course of several months, there were
six different employee suicides on site.

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:03.480
Security footage of the deceased operatives
shortly before their deaths showed them acting

00:42:03.480 --> 00:42:08.760
deeply troubled, engaging in agitated conversation
with someone not visible to the camera.

00:42:08.760 --> 00:42:13.380
At first, it was posited that whoever was
upsetting the employees was in a blind spot,

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:18.000
but it quickly became clear that the dead
employees were being tormented by something

00:42:18.000 --> 00:42:18.780
invisible.

00:42:18.780 --> 00:42:24.360
Stories of hauntings were one thing, but now
with escaped SCPs and a notable death count,

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:29.400
the Directorial Board decided to mount an
official investigation into the unexplained

00:42:29.400 --> 00:42:30.120
occurrences.

00:42:30.120 --> 00:42:35.580
It was then that the spirit was officially
acknowledged, identified, and re-classified

00:42:35.580 --> 00:42:38.580
as SCP-4973.

00:42:38.580 --> 00:42:46.200
SCP-4973 was once a D-Class worker who was
used as a guinea pig in 25 different experiments

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:48.600
with a variety of entities on site.

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:54.900
He was repeatedly exposed to multiple unusual
and dangerous anomalies, including SCP-158,

00:42:54.900 --> 00:43:00.240
a large mechanical arm capable of extracting
an unidentified substance from any living

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:01.140
test subject.

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:07.320
Before he was brought on board at SCP Foundation
Site-30, he was known as Gordon Kardivachlus

00:43:07.320 --> 00:43:12.240
Markovitch, a man who had been convicted of
Murder in the Second Degree after stabbing

00:43:12.240 --> 00:43:15.420
his wife 24 times in the chest with a kitchen
knife.

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:18.780
During his incarceration,&nbsp;
Markovitch was transferred&nbsp;

00:43:18.780 --> 00:43:21.600
to the custody of the SCP Foundation and placed

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:26.100
at Site-30 where he was given a new name,
D-10000.

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:32.040
During an experiment involving a redacted
entity, D-10000 was killed and his remains

00:43:32.040 --> 00:43:34.500
were disposed of at a nearby waste refinery.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:39.360
It was then only three months after his death,
that the initial reports of ghost activity

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:40.020
began.

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:46.980
SCP-4973 is regarded as a Class IV Spectral
Entity, meaning that it is an entity displaying

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:51.660
a high degree of aggression and malevolence
toward a specific individual or group.

00:43:51.660 --> 00:43:54.600
In this case, the spirit displays hostility&nbsp;

00:43:54.600 --> 00:43:58.800
towards a very specific&nbsp;
group - the SCP Foundation.

00:43:58.800 --> 00:44:04.140
Class IV entities can affect the environment
around them, move objects, reduce the temperature

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:08.940
in a room, and induce hallucinations in humans
that it comes into contact with.

00:44:08.940 --> 00:44:13.440
These hallucinations are thought to be the
cause of the increase in employee suicides

00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:16.560
that occurred after 4973’s first appearance.

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:22.680
One of the many ways in which SCP-4973 acted
out, in what is believed to be an attempt

00:44:22.680 --> 00:44:27.960
at revenge on the Foundation as a whole, was
to somehow gain access to the SCiPNET Database

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:28.440
Terminal.

00:44:28.440 --> 00:44:33.060
This allowed the spirit to learn about the
entities contained at the site, as well as

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:38.340
personal details regarding the lives of research,
security, and administrative staff working

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:38.700
there.

00:44:38.700 --> 00:44:44.820
This information is likely what allowed 4973
to cause several suspected containment breaches,

00:44:44.820 --> 00:44:49.500
though the specifics of those breaches have
been expunged from official records.

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:56.220
4973 was eventually contained within an apparatus
known as an Incorporeal Entity Vacuum Chamber,

00:44:56.220 --> 00:45:01.860
which is consistently monitored by a team
of specialists within MTF Mu-13, affectionately

00:45:01.860 --> 00:45:03.720
nicknamed “Ghostbusters.”

00:45:03.720 --> 00:45:08.580
In order to prevent it from escaping, four
Harrington-Hollow Neutralizer Wards have been

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:10.560
placed at the door to the containment facility.

00:45:10.560 --> 00:45:13.440
This containment method&nbsp;
has been largely effective,&nbsp;

00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:15.600
though the spirit has escaped a handful of

00:45:15.600 --> 00:45:19.140
times and had to be recaptured and placed
back into the IEVC.

00:45:19.140 --> 00:45:26.280
In order to understand SCP-4973, and uncover
how it was able to access the database, Dr.

00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:31.080
Lorraine Casper was assigned to conduct an
interview with the spirit, speaking to it

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:35.820
as it was in containment, and a transcript
of this interview is available in the official

00:45:35.820 --> 00:45:37.080
Foundation archives.

00:45:37.080 --> 00:45:43.500
Dr. Casper began the conversation politely,
addressing the SCP as Gordon and asking him

00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:44.220
how he was.

00:45:44.220 --> 00:45:49.140
Gordon refused to comply with the round of
questioning, going as far as to yell at Dr.

00:45:49.140 --> 00:45:54.000
Casper until she reminded him that he faced
the risk of termination if he refused to answer

00:45:54.000 --> 00:45:54.780
her questions.

00:45:54.780 --> 00:46:00.600
Dr. Casper pointed out that the files accessed
by 4973 required clearance that the spirit

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:02.280
would have no way of possessing.

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:06.780
He responded that he simply waved his hand
across the terminal in order to access it,

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:10.980
explaining: “It freaked out for a second
and then it just - stopped...

00:46:10.980 --> 00:46:14.700
I could just move my fingers around the air
and the tabs would just roll up.

00:46:14.700 --> 00:46:15.780
Easy as that.”

00:46:15.780 --> 00:46:20.700
Dr. Casper asked for clarification, inquiring
how he could even do something like that.

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:24.600
4973 continued his explanation, “Beats me.

00:46:24.600 --> 00:46:26.460
It felt like some attraction.

00:46:26.460 --> 00:46:29.640
Like magnets from afar getting closer and
closer.

00:46:29.640 --> 00:46:30.720
I dunno, something like that.”

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:36.180
Finding this answer unsatisfactory, Dr. Casper
pushed further, asking for an explanation

00:46:36.180 --> 00:46:41.760
of SCP-4973’s attempts to release dangerous
onsite anomalies from containment.

00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:46.620
This question seemed to stump him, and he
claimed not to understand what Dr. Casper

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:46.980
meant.

00:46:46.980 --> 00:46:52.380
She explained that the ID cards of Dr. Yurkov
and Dr. Orson were used to open the facilities

00:46:52.380 --> 00:46:54.600
from which these anomalies escaped.

00:46:54.600 --> 00:47:00.720
4973 was shocked that he had been suspected
of this attack, and insisted not to know who

00:47:00.720 --> 00:47:02.880
Orson or Yurkov even were.

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:05.520
All of a sudden, he trailed off.

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:10.860
There was silence for a moment, and Dr. Casper
began to collect her notes and leave, assuming

00:47:10.860 --> 00:47:12.180
that the interview was over.

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:21.060
Much to her surprise, SCP-4973 began to laugh,
a loud uproarious laugh of complete and utter

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:21.540
delight.

00:47:21.540 --> 00:47:26.940
Casper asked what 4973 was laughing about,
and was met with the response: “God, this

00:47:26.940 --> 00:47:29.700
whole time, you and your ghost-hunting crew
never knew?”

00:47:29.700 --> 00:47:35.040
He refused to clarify what it was exactly
the doctor and her crew had missed, only saying

00:47:35.040 --> 00:47:39.180
“God, that look on your face when you find
out it’s been right under your noses this

00:47:39.180 --> 00:47:39.900
whole time.”

00:47:39.900 --> 00:47:45.060
He continued to laugh at Dr. Casper for the
remainder of the interview, refusing to answer

00:47:45.060 --> 00:47:48.420
any further questions or explain any of his
comments.

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:54.360
Finally, Dr. Casper grew exasperated and left,
ending the interview for real this time.

00:47:54.360 --> 00:48:00.240
Following this perplexing interview, Dr. Casper
inexplicably abandoned her post at Site-30.

00:48:00.240 --> 00:48:04.740
Her supervisors could not reach her, neither
could any of the other researchers at the

00:48:04.740 --> 00:48:04.920
site.

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:10.980
No one heard from her for an entire week,
until one day she called Director Eric Riverson

00:48:10.980 --> 00:48:13.320
of Site-30 on his personal phone line.

00:48:13.320 --> 00:48:19.620
He recorded the conversation, and a transcript
has been included in the file for SCP-4973.

00:48:19.620 --> 00:48:24.420
Dr. Casper explained to Director Riverson
that she had been hiding in her private residence

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:28.800
away from the site, and that she no longer
considered any part of the site safe.

00:48:28.800 --> 00:48:34.740
In fact, she suggested that all SCP Foundation
sites might be potentially compromised.

00:48:34.740 --> 00:48:41.100
She explained her conversation with SCP-4973,
and the questions it provoked her to ask.

00:48:41.100 --> 00:48:45.480
If he had not been behind the containment
breaches, then who was?

00:48:45.480 --> 00:48:50.760
He never encountered Dr. Yurkov or Orson in
any of the tests administered on him, and

00:48:50.760 --> 00:48:55.380
Orson wasn’t even employed at the Foundation
until after 4973’s death.

00:48:55.380 --> 00:49:00.540
Dr. Casper posited that, though the spirit
of Gordon was not responsible for the breaches,

00:49:00.540 --> 00:49:04.680
another spectral force, or forces, at the
site might be.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:06.000
Her hypothesis?

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:10.860
That Site-30 was positively infested with
the vengeful spirits of Class D employees

00:49:10.860 --> 00:49:13.680
lost over the years during various experiments.

00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:19.020
In order to prove her theory, Dr. Casper got
her hands on all of the experimental equipment

00:49:19.020 --> 00:49:21.840
used by the department researching spectral
anomalies.

00:49:21.840 --> 00:49:26.760
The general consensus among researchers in
this field is that spirits leak out a kind

00:49:26.760 --> 00:49:30.120
of energy, known as ectoplasm, wherever they
go.

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:36.000
It has been compared to the skin left behind
when a spider or a snake molts, a nonessential

00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:40.260
portion of the entity that is shed and leaves
a residue in areas where they have been.

00:49:40.260 --> 00:49:46.200
Unsurprisingly, a huge amount of ectoplasm
was detected inside of Site-30.

00:49:46.200 --> 00:49:51.060
The spirits went undetected at first, as many
of them were dormant for quite some time.

00:49:51.060 --> 00:49:55.860
Dormant spirits are spirits that are not aware
of their surroundings or the circumstances

00:49:55.860 --> 00:50:00.000
of their deaths, and they do not emit large
quantities of ectoplasm.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:04.860
It is likely that, after Gordon was trapped,
several of the dormant spirits of Site-30

00:50:04.860 --> 00:50:09.960
began to remember what was done to them, and
manifest in more aggressive ways.

00:50:09.960 --> 00:50:14.640
Because Gordon was presumed to be the only
spirit at the site, their gradual appearance

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:19.320
and acting out was either ignored, or presumed
to be Gordon escaping from containment.

00:50:19.320 --> 00:50:24.960
After her realization, Dr. Casper identified
an additional five active spirits at the site,

00:50:24.960 --> 00:50:27.780
and was able to capture and contain one of
them.

00:50:27.780 --> 00:50:32.820
Dr. Casper warned Director Riverson that there
were likely far more spirits than just those

00:50:32.820 --> 00:50:38.220
five, and it was only a matter of time before
they woke up and became active like the others.

00:50:38.220 --> 00:50:43.500
The large amount of previous containment breaches
attributed to simple “human error” becomes

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:48.000
deeply sinister once it is clear that there
have been vengeful spirits hiding in plain

00:50:48.000 --> 00:50:49.380
sight the entire time.

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:54.900
As Gordon put it in his interview, they were
just under their noses the whole time.

00:50:54.900 --> 00:51:00.300
Dr. Casper’s findings, and the story of
Site-30, present a troubling possibility.

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:06.480
Every SCP Foundation site has used massive
amounts of Class D Personnel in human experiments

00:51:06.480 --> 00:51:11.760
with dangerous entities, and the body count
at nearly all of them is staggering.

00:51:11.760 --> 00:51:17.100
If Dr. Casper is right, then every single
site has the makings of a haunted house.

00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:23.040
All of them could be infested with the spirits
of dead angry D-Class, who are just waiting

00:51:23.040 --> 00:51:28.320
to wake up and take their revenge on the still
living members of the organization that killed

00:51:28.320 --> 00:51:28.380
them.

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:33.840
And it’s only a matter of time before they
get the chance.

00:51:33.840 --> 00:51:39.840
I used to complain about work all the time.&nbsp;
The long hours, the disrespect from my boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:39.840 --> 00:51:46.080
getting home exhausted, aching, and sunburned. I&nbsp;
worked in construction a lifetime ago. I’d give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:46.080 --> 00:51:50.880
anything to go back to that now. What’s that they&nbsp;
always say? You don’t know what you’ve got until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:50.880 --> 00:51:57.120
it’s gone? Maybe I deserve the hand I’ve been&nbsp;
dealt. I haven’t been a good person. I don’t play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.120 --> 00:52:02.760
well with others. There was an argument with my&nbsp;
foreman, one that got a little too heated, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:02.760 --> 00:52:06.300
just couldn’t reign in my temper.
The hammer I’d been using all day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:06.300 --> 00:52:11.340
was still in my hand…but I digress.
I used to build things with my hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:11.340 --> 00:52:16.980
but I’m in a different line of work now. If you&nbsp;
can call it work. That would imply I get paid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:16.980 --> 00:52:22.680
I clock in every morning, and I get to go&nbsp;
home afterward. I don’t. I’m a prisoner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:28.980
really. A glorified lab rat, and property of&nbsp;
the all-powerful SCP Foundation. I’ve been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:28.980 --> 00:52:34.680
here about three weeks, and everyone always says&nbsp;
D-Classes like us last about a month here. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:34.680 --> 00:52:39.660
the clock is ticking down to zero for me. I&nbsp;
haven’t had to do anything too dangerous yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:39.660 --> 00:52:45.360
but like I said. It’s just a matter of time.
I’m sitting in my cell, the same windowless box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:45.360 --> 00:52:49.920
I’ve spent every day for the past three weeks,&nbsp;
when a guard bursts in and grabs me by the arm.&nbsp;

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:55.800
“What’s going on?” I ask, but he ignores me.
Of course, why make conversation with a lab rat?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:55.800 --> 00:53:00.060
Better not to think of us as human at all.
He yanks me down the hall, opens a door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.060 --> 00:53:04.560
and shoves me inside. There are several other&nbsp;
D-Class in there already, and they’re staring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:08.880
at something in the corner that I can’t&nbsp;
quite make out. I start to turn around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:08.880 --> 00:53:14.280
to take in the rest of the room, but a voice&nbsp;
comes over the loudspeaker, cold and clinical.&nbsp;

00:53:14.280 --> 00:53:18.540
“Look at the statue in the corner.&nbsp;
Do not take your eyes off it.”&nbsp;

00:53:18.540 --> 00:53:22.980
Well, when a disembodied voice at the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation tells you to look at something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:28.680
you’d better do it. So, I look.
It's a sculpture, made from concrete and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:28.680 --> 00:53:33.240
rebar. It looks pretty harmless, but I’ve heard&nbsp;
enough screams through the walls to know that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:33.240 --> 00:53:37.320
don’t keep too many harmless things caged&nbsp;
up here. Whatever this thing really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:37.320 --> 00:53:42.600
I don’t want to know what it’ll do to me when&nbsp;
my back is turned. I stare at it, unblinking,&nbsp;

00:53:42.600 --> 00:53:47.040
feeling my eyes burn and tear up from the&nbsp;
dry air and concentration. I’m scared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:51.060
close my eyes for even a second, determined&nbsp;
not to be the first one in here to break.&nbsp;

00:53:51.060 --> 00:53:58.020
But something out there has other plans for&nbsp;
me, for us. I hear a warbling sound like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:58.020 --> 00:54:03.600
motor struggling to start, the sudden crash of&nbsp;
thunder outside, and then…the room goes black.&nbsp;

00:54:03.600 --> 00:54:07.260
For a second I think I’ve gone blind, but&nbsp;
then I hear the other guys screaming, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:07.260 --> 00:54:12.120
yelling of scientists behind the wall. Somehow,&nbsp;
the power’s gone out. I can’t see anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:12.120 --> 00:54:16.860
but I can hear the sound of stone scraping&nbsp;
against itself, the snap of breaking bones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:16.860 --> 00:54:22.320
the thud of a limp body slumping to the floor.
The room comes into focus again, as a backup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:22.320 --> 00:54:27.000
generator kicks in and fills the space with weak&nbsp;
fluorescent light. But something’s different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:32.280
something’s wrong. The statue in the corner&nbsp;
is gone. The other two men are on the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:32.280 --> 00:54:36.060
heads twisted around backwards.&nbsp;
And the door is standing wide open.&nbsp;

00:54:36.060 --> 00:54:41.160
Is this a trap? A trick of some kind? If I&nbsp;
run out that door, will a guard be waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:41.160 --> 00:54:45.840
there for me? I don’t know, but whatever might&nbsp;
happen to me if I try can’t be any worse than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:49.320
what will happen if I stay right here.
Through the wall, I hear that sickening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:49.320 --> 00:54:55.380
snapping sound again, a corpse thudding to the&nbsp;
ground. They didn’t plan this. Something in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:55.380 --> 00:55:00.720
their experiment went horribly wrong, for them.&nbsp;
And maybe, just maybe, it went right for me.&nbsp;

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:05.400
I don’t give it a second thought, I’m out the&nbsp;
door, running through the dimly lit halls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:05.400 --> 00:55:08.940
through the maze of identical corridors&nbsp;
in search of some kind of exit.&nbsp;

00:55:08.940 --> 00:55:14.580
All around me, there’s chaos. Guards firing&nbsp;
their weapons at inhuman shapes that I catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:14.580 --> 00:55:19.320
a brief glimpse of as I run past, scientists&nbsp;
yelling for help, people going as fast as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:19.320 --> 00:55:23.520
can toward the danger and away from it.
I’m so caught up in it all, I don’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:23.520 --> 00:55:27.540
notice when I run right into a woman in&nbsp;
a white lab coat, knocking us both to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:32.100
ground. I scramble to my feet, and look at her&nbsp;
with wide eyes, waiting for her to turn me in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:32.100 --> 00:55:36.900
to call the guards. But she doesn’t.
“I’m so sorry,” I stammer.&nbsp;

00:55:36.900 --> 00:55:41.940
She holds out a hand, gesturing for me to help&nbsp;
her to her feet. So, I do. “Watch your step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:41.940 --> 00:55:46.320
there,” she says, with a knowing smile. I&nbsp;
can’t help but notice how beautiful she is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:46.320 --> 00:55:52.200
twinkling brown eyes and thick black hair.&nbsp;
Then, I spot them, tucked under her hair but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:52.200 --> 00:55:58.260
unmistakable. She has a pair of fox ears on top&nbsp;
of her head. It hits me in rapid succession then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:58.260 --> 00:56:03.900
all the things about this woman that don’t look&nbsp;
quite right. Her unusually long nails, too sharp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:03.900 --> 00:56:08.880
and pointed for lab work. The feral glint in&nbsp;
her eyes. The halting cadence of her voice.&nbsp;

00:56:08.880 --> 00:56:13.320
This woman is not an employee of the&nbsp;
SCP Foundation. She’s doing the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:13.320 --> 00:56:16.320
thing I am: taking advantage of&nbsp;
the situation to try and escape.&nbsp;

00:56:16.320 --> 00:56:21.600
"I'll be more careful from now on,&nbsp;
ma’am,” I say. She winks and turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:21.600 --> 00:56:25.260
down the hall. I can see a tail poking&nbsp;
out beneath the bottom of her lab coat.&nbsp;

00:56:25.260 --> 00:56:31.740
“Safe travels!” I call after her. She laughs, a&nbsp;
dangerous sound, like she’s not the one who needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:36.120
to worry about staying safe, and I wonder what&nbsp;
might have happened if I had caught her in a worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:36.120 --> 00:56:40.020
mood. Good thing my mom taught me my manners.
Now, which way should I go from here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:43.200
Should I follow the woman? I get the&nbsp;
feeling she doesn’t want any company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:43.200 --> 00:56:47.340
and I’d prefer to stay on her good side.
As I’m thinking it through, I hear a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:51.860
popping sound behind me, like a vacuum in&nbsp;
the air being filled all of a sudden. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:51.860 --> 00:56:57.180
spin around, and there’s a guy standing there,&nbsp;
ordinary as you please. Blonde hair, green eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:57.180 --> 00:57:02.580
wearing jeans, and a t-shirt that says “Mothman&nbsp;
Fan Club” in red letters. This guy looks pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:07.140
out of place, more the type that you’d see&nbsp;
browsing a comic book store, but after my prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:13.800
strange encounter I know not to assume anything.
“Hey man, what’s going on?” He asks me. I stare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:13.800 --> 00:57:18.720
back, not sure what to say. “What did I miss?”&nbsp;
He continues. “I feel like I was gone for ages.”&nbsp;

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:23.520
“Um,” I gesture to our collective&nbsp;
surroundings, the sound of alarms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:23.520 --> 00:57:27.600
blaring and voices crying out for backup, the&nbsp;
inhuman shrieks of creatures being forced back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:27.600 --> 00:57:34.020
into their cells. “There’s a lot going on.”
“Huh, yeah. Seems like it,” the guy sighs.&nbsp;

00:57:34.020 --> 00:57:38.280
“You wouldn’t happen to know how to get&nbsp;
out of here, would you?” I ask, hopefully.&nbsp;

00:57:38.280 --> 00:57:41.460
He shakes his head.
“Sorry, I don’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:41.460 --> 00:57:45.240
come and go through the door here. Guess&nbsp;
I’ll just go back to my room until things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:45.240 --> 00:57:48.840
calm down a bit. Do you want anything? I’ve&nbsp;
got a mini-fridge in there,” he offers.&nbsp;

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:51.840
“…No thanks.”
“Suit yourself, man!”&nbsp;

00:57:51.840 --> 00:57:56.340
He shoves his hands in his pockets, and&nbsp;
walks away, whistling a tune to himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:56.340 --> 00:58:00.960
If he’s this unphased by teleporting into&nbsp;
the middle of a complete madhouse, I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:00.960 --> 00:58:05.520
even want to know what kind of stuff he’s seen.&nbsp;
Nice enough kid, though. Probably better company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:09.900
than anyone, or anything, else in this place.
If I’m not going to take him up on his offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:13.500
though, I should keep moving before someone&nbsp;
notices I’m not where I’m supposed to be.&nbsp;

00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:17.340
I round a corner just in time to see&nbsp;
another body drop. I whirl around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:17.340 --> 00:58:23.160
looking for that horrible statue, but I don’t&nbsp;
see it. Instead, I see what looks like a man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:23.160 --> 00:58:28.860
dressed in all black, wearing a mask in the shape&nbsp;
of a bird’s face with a long, pointed beak. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:28.860 --> 00:58:33.000
carrying an old-school doctor’s bag, and he’s&nbsp;
bending down to inspect the body of the guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:37.680
that just keeled over. I should keep running,&nbsp;
I should do something, but I can’t look away.&nbsp;

00:58:37.680 --> 00:58:43.020
The man in black pulls a syringe full of thick&nbsp;
green liquid from his bag and injects it into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:43.020 --> 00:58:47.760
corpse. To my horror, the body begins to move,&nbsp;
thrashing around, hands opening and closing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:47.760 --> 00:58:54.480
grasping for something. It sits up, reanimated,&nbsp;
but…wrong. Its eyes are bulging and vacant like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:54.480 --> 00:59:00.420
a goldfish, its mouth hangs slack. As the zombie&nbsp;
climbs to its feet, the figure in all-black turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:00.420 --> 00:59:05.460
to look at me, eyes shining from beneath his mask.
“Hello, my good fellow! How are you feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:05.460 --> 00:59:08.580
today?” He calls to me, his&nbsp;
voice polished and polite.&nbsp;

00:59:08.580 --> 00:59:12.300
“I’m fine!” I shout, my hands&nbsp;
shaking, my chest tight.&nbsp;

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:15.180
The doctor tilts his head&nbsp;
to the side thoughtfully.&nbsp;

00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:20.940
“You don’t look well, my friend. Perhaps you could&nbsp;
allow me to examine you?” The zombie shuffles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:25.500
toward me, and the doctor opens his bag. “There’s&nbsp;
a pestilence raging through this land, you see.”&nbsp;

00:59:25.500 --> 00:59:28.680
I’m not going to stick around to find&nbsp;
out what that exam might look like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:28.680 --> 00:59:32.700
or what he might do in the service of curing me.
“No thanks, I don’t have health insurance!”&nbsp;

00:59:32.700 --> 00:59:38.400
That is all I can think to say as I turn and run&nbsp;
back the way I came. I still have no idea where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:43.260
an exit might be, how I might get out of here. It&nbsp;
all looks the same, one long stretch of tile and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:43.260 --> 00:59:47.100
white. I guess a place like the Foundation&nbsp;
isn’t going to have red neon signs telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:47.100 --> 00:59:52.260
you how to escape. But this place can’t go on&nbsp;
forever…can it? I’m starting to have my doubts.&nbsp;

00:59:52.260 --> 00:59:57.420
Before I can get too lost in my own hopelessness,&nbsp;
the biggest man I’ve ever seen comes lumbering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:57.420 --> 01:00:00.540
down the hall toward me. I worked with&nbsp;
some pretty big guys in construction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:00.540 --> 01:00:06.120
massive behemoths who would carry steel beams like&nbsp;
they were pool noodles, but this man is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.120 --> 01:00:11.580
else entirely. He’s gotta be…eight feet tall?&nbsp;
With fists that look like they could crush my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:11.580 --> 01:00:15.060
skull in one hit. I’d better get out of this&nbsp;
monster’s way if I know what’s good for me.&nbsp;

01:00:15.060 --> 01:00:21.720
But as he passes me, he waves and gives me a&nbsp;
great big friendly - if unnaturally wide - smile.&nbsp;

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:29.040
“Beautiful day, isn’t it?” he bellows in&nbsp;
a booming, French-accented voice. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:29.040 --> 01:00:33.300
his other hand, he picks at his teeth with&nbsp;
something white. A closer look reveals it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:33.300 --> 01:00:38.220
be a shard of bone. I have to imagine it’s&nbsp;
human. His lips are stained dark with blood.&nbsp;

01:00:38.220 --> 01:00:45.780
“You look lost, can I help you with something?”
A helpful people-eater. What a guy. I keep my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:45.780 --> 01:00:48.900
distance but decide to try my luck.
“You wouldn’t happen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:48.900 --> 01:00:52.620
know where the exit is?” I say.
He beams at me, thrilled by my question.&nbsp;

01:00:52.620 --> 01:00:58.020
“I’m so glad you asked! It’s that way!” He&nbsp;
points down a hall to the left. “Just follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:58.020 --> 01:01:02.220
that hall all the way down, and take the only&nbsp;
right! That should get you where you’re going!”&nbsp;

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:05.580
“Thanks!”
I give him a wave back as I head&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:05.580 --> 01:01:11.100
that way. As I go, I can hear the giant singing&nbsp;
opera to himself, something Italian. It reminds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:11.100 --> 01:01:15.600
me of a record my grandma used to play, a&nbsp;
long time ago. Maybe this will get me free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:15.600 --> 01:01:20.340
maybe it won’t, but it’s more information than I&nbsp;
had a second ago. Might as well give it a shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:24.600
If I make it through this, I’m going to be in the&nbsp;
best shape of my life from all the running I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:28.560
done today. Maybe I’ll try to do a marathon.&nbsp;
Or maybe I shouldn’t get ahead of myself.&nbsp;

01:01:28.560 --> 01:01:33.900
There’s no one else down this particular hall,&nbsp;
at least no one that I can see. I slow my pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:33.900 --> 01:01:38.760
to a steady walk, keeping my eyes and ears&nbsp;
alert for any potential threats. So far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:38.760 --> 01:01:44.460
so good. I reach the other side of the hall, and&nbsp;
take a right turn…and nothing. Just a wall. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:44.460 --> 01:01:48.480
giant son of a gun lied to me! I guess that’s&nbsp;
what I get for asking a cannibal for directions.&nbsp;

01:01:48.480 --> 01:01:53.460
I guess I’m lucky he just misled me instead of&nbsp;
having me for dessert. I’m about to plan my next&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:53.460 --> 01:01:59.100
move, when I feel something nudge against the&nbsp;
back of my leg. I turn, and there’s what I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:59.100 --> 01:02:03.900
only describe as a blob. Just a large mass&nbsp;
of orange slime, wrapping around my legs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:03.900 --> 01:02:09.300
making a friendly high-pitched gurgle. I should&nbsp;
be suspicious of it, after everything I’ve seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:09.300 --> 01:02:13.980
but I’m not. I just get the feeling this&nbsp;
thing has my best interests at heart.&nbsp;

01:02:13.980 --> 01:02:19.560
“Hey, buddy…” I pat its slimy surface,&nbsp;
and it ripples delightedly, making another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:19.560 --> 01:02:24.360
gurgling sound. It’s kind of cute, honestly.&nbsp;
I smell the scent of freshly brewed coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:24.360 --> 01:02:29.220
one of my favorite smells in the world.&nbsp;
I haven’t had a decent cup in years.&nbsp;

01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:35.880
All of a sudden, I feel this overwhelming sense&nbsp;
of happiness, of hope. Even if it’s foolish to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:35.880 --> 01:02:39.840
think so, I honestly believe that everything&nbsp;
is going to be alright. This strange little&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:39.840 --> 01:02:45.000
friendly slime has given me the strength to carry&nbsp;
on. I wish I had a plushie version of this thing.&nbsp;

01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:50.160
There’s a sudden tickling sensation along my&nbsp;
legs, and I laugh, pushing the slime away gently.&nbsp;

01:02:50.160 --> 01:02:53.940
“Hey, stop that!”
It coos apologetically and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:53.940 --> 01:02:58.320
bumps itself against me again like a stray dog&nbsp;
asking for scratches behind the ears. I wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:58.320 --> 01:03:02.520
for a second if I could take this thing with&nbsp;
me, but what would I even feed it? What kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:02.520 --> 01:03:06.960
life could I give a domesticated slime creature?&nbsp;
Better to leave it with people who know how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:06.960 --> 01:03:11.460
care for it, even if they’re mad scientists.
I give it another pat, then head on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:11.460 --> 01:03:15.540
way. The slime oozes off in the opposite&nbsp;
direction, off to find more new friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:15.540 --> 01:03:20.640
I imagine. I had no idea there was anything nice&nbsp;
here, with everything horrible that I’ve seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:20.640 --> 01:03:25.380
today. Warms my cold heart, just a little bit.
It's strange, I’ve been walking for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:25.380 --> 01:03:30.960
and I haven’t seen anyone else. It’s eerily&nbsp;
quiet over here, I can’t hear a thing except&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:30.960 --> 01:03:35.280
for that alarm in the distance. Not sure when all&nbsp;
the screaming went quiet, or where all the guards&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:35.280 --> 01:03:40.680
went. It’s peaceful, but I have to admit it’s&nbsp;
eerie. There’s no way it’s this easy. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:40.680 --> 01:03:44.880
no way I’ll be able to just walk out of here.&nbsp;
Life doesn’t work like that, especially not here.&nbsp;

01:03:44.880 --> 01:03:49.680
My eyes dart around, watching for any potential&nbsp;
danger as I go. Maybe all the action is on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.680 --> 01:03:54.000
other side of the facility, maybe something broke&nbsp;
out of containment that’s so big, so dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:54.000 --> 01:03:58.140
that all hands are on deck to contain it.&nbsp;
Or maybe they’re all dead, and I’m next.&nbsp;

01:03:59.460 --> 01:04:04.140
I shouldn’t think like that. I feel the hairs&nbsp;
on the back of my neck prickle, and I have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:04.140 --> 01:04:10.380
paranoid feeling of being watched. Like eyes,&nbsp;
burning into me. I glance behind me, but there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:10.380 --> 01:04:16.260
no one there. I stop walking, but I don’t hear&nbsp;
any other footsteps. Calm down, there’s no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:16.260 --> 01:04:20.760
here with you, I tell myself. Just keep moving.
I turn my eyes back to the path ahead of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:20.760 --> 01:04:26.460
and I freeze. What was that? I saw something,&nbsp;
just for a second. Something long, thin, spindly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:26.460 --> 01:04:32.880
like a stick bug but much, much bigger. When&nbsp;
I look for it, it’s gone. But I know that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:32.880 --> 01:04:37.440
saw it. Or this place has now driven me insane. I&nbsp;
glance to the side, and there it is again! For a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:41.640
split second, so fast I could almost convince&nbsp;
myself I imagined it. If I were at home, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:41.640 --> 01:04:47.460
walking down an ordinary street, I might think I&nbsp;
had. But I’m not anywhere ordinary. And a slender&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:47.460 --> 01:04:51.240
monster that can hide in my peripheral vision,&nbsp;
only be spotted for a millisecond at a time?&nbsp;

01:04:51.240 --> 01:04:53.640
That seems par for the&nbsp;
course in a place like this.&nbsp;

01:04:53.640 --> 01:04:59.520
There! I just saw it again! It’s like it wants&nbsp;
me to know it’s here, but that I’ll never be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:59.520 --> 01:05:04.200
able to pin it down. I’m not playing mind games&nbsp;
with a monster, forget it. I’ll keep moving.&nbsp;

01:05:04.200 --> 01:05:09.900
Another flash of the creature, closer than it was&nbsp;
before. “Whatever, you don’t scare me.” I say out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:14.400
loud. I’m lying, but I hope it can’t tell. If&nbsp;
I just play it cool, maybe this thing will get&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:14.400 --> 01:05:18.120
bored and find someone else to menace. I&nbsp;
haven’t been running for a long time now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:18.120 --> 01:05:22.200
but my heart is still pounding. I can&nbsp;
taste metal in the back of my throat.&nbsp;

01:05:22.200 --> 01:05:26.640
Suddenly, all the sounds from before are back.&nbsp;
The gunfire, the screaming, the shriek of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:26.640 --> 01:05:33.240
alarms. I’ve made my way back to the action. One&nbsp;
sound drowns out all the rest: a deafening roar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:33.240 --> 01:05:38.520
animalistic and powerful and…hateful.
It reminds me of a dinosaur in a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:38.520 --> 01:05:43.320
but with a power that makes my bones rattle and&nbsp;
my teeth chatter. The roar is followed by more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:43.320 --> 01:05:48.480
screaming, and a massive crash, the crunch&nbsp;
of wood and stone. There’s another roar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:48.480 --> 01:05:53.280
more distant than before. Wait…whatever&nbsp;
made that sound, it just broke out. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:53.280 --> 01:05:57.480
bet it left a pretty big hole in its wake.
I follow the sound and find exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:57.480 --> 01:06:04.020
I hoped I would: A massive opening in the wall,&nbsp;
and the sweet feeling of a breeze coming through.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:04.020 --> 01:06:08.640
I couldn’t find an exit, but this thing just&nbsp;
made one for me. I don’t waste any more time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:08.640 --> 01:06:13.620
I make a break for it, dancing around rubble and&nbsp;
pools of corrosive acid, and sprinting into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:13.620 --> 01:06:18.120
nearby forest. I’m ducking under branches,&nbsp;
darting around trees, and I run until I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:18.120 --> 01:06:23.160
seeing spots and my lungs are gasping for air.
I don’t know how far I’ll make it, if there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:23.160 --> 01:06:27.960
a town nearby or any kind of shelter.&nbsp;
But I promise one thing, if I survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:27.960 --> 01:06:33.000
I’m going to be a better person. I’ll get it right&nbsp;
this time. I’ll earn my second chance at life.

01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:37.680
The SCP Foundation’s Mobile Task Forces
are the best of the best.

01:06:37.680 --> 01:06:43.440
Their members are the elite: handpicked from
the world’s militaries, intelligence agencies,

01:06:43.440 --> 01:06:49.140
security contractors, and navies, to create
the Foundation’s own security force.

01:06:49.140 --> 01:06:54.060
They ensure that the other personnel are able
to do their jobs without having to worry about

01:06:54.060 --> 01:06:58.620
their own safety, whether it be during an
assault on the site from the Chaos Insurgency

01:06:58.620 --> 01:07:03.660
or a containment breach from one of the countless
dangerous anomalies that the Foundation contains.

01:07:03.660 --> 01:07:06.900
Of course, not every Mobile Task Force can
do everything.

01:07:06.900 --> 01:07:10.140
That’s why each one is specialized.

01:07:10.140 --> 01:07:14.820
Highly trained and equipped with bleeding-edge
technology and equipment, every Mobile Task

01:07:14.820 --> 01:07:19.800
Force has one specialty - one thing they do
better than anyone else in the world.

01:07:19.800 --> 01:07:24.240
And with the hundreds of Mobile Task Forces
on the Foundation’s roster, that’s quite

01:07:24.240 --> 01:07:25.200
a few specialties.

01:07:25.200 --> 01:07:29.100
There’s the famous MTF Epsilon-11, the Nine-tailed
Foxes.

01:07:29.100 --> 01:07:34.320
Handling internal Foundation security, this
military task force is made up of former Special

01:07:34.320 --> 01:07:39.720
Operations soldiers and are deployed into
Foundation sites - usually where something

01:07:39.720 --> 01:07:43.380
has gone terribly wrong - to clean up the
mess and restore order.

01:07:43.380 --> 01:07:49.020
Wherever a containment breach or communication
blackout happens, Epsilon-11 won’t be far

01:07:49.020 --> 01:07:49.500
behind.

01:07:49.500 --> 01:07:53.460
Or you can take the grizzled MTF Zeta-9, the
Mole Rats.

01:07:53.460 --> 01:07:59.700
Specially trained to explore, map, contain,
and if necessary, fight in enclosed subterranean

01:07:59.700 --> 01:08:01.800
spaces and anomalous topographies.

01:08:01.800 --> 01:08:06.900
Zeta-9 has a high casualty rate, but if they
can’t dive into the darkness to get the

01:08:06.900 --> 01:08:08.520
job done, nobody can.

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:12.960
And who can forget the infamous MTF Omega-7,
Pandora’s Box?

01:08:12.960 --> 01:08:18.720
A secret task force organized in the 90s to
train and utilize humanoid SCP objects in

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:20.460
field operations for the Foundation.

01:08:20.460 --> 01:08:27.000
Some of the most famous humanoid anomalies,
like SCP-076, were members of Omega-7 until

01:08:27.000 --> 01:08:29.460
it was forcibly disbanded by O5 Command.

01:08:29.460 --> 01:08:31.260
But that’s a story for another day.

01:08:31.260 --> 01:08:36.180
But above all of these Task Forces, one MTF
operates on another level.

01:08:36.180 --> 01:08:42.120
The O5 Council, the group of thirteen mysterious
individuals that control the Foundation, retain

01:08:42.120 --> 01:08:45.060
one Mobile Task Force for their personal use.

01:08:45.060 --> 01:08:48.900
Mobile Task Force Alpha-1, the Red Right Hand.

01:08:48.900 --> 01:08:54.780
Named for the Biblical symbol of vengeance,
Alpha-1 serves, appropriately, as the right

01:08:54.780 --> 01:08:58.680
hand of the Overseers, executing their edicts
throughout the Foundation.

01:08:58.680 --> 01:09:04.020
Their operations are classified so intensely
one could be mistaken for thinking they don’t

01:09:04.020 --> 01:09:09.660
even really exist, but Alpha-1 embody the
will of the Overseers, and are empowered to

01:09:09.660 --> 01:09:12.120
enforce that will by any means necessary.

01:09:12.120 --> 01:09:16.740
Most Foundation personnel will never even
encounter Alpha-1 through their entire career

01:09:16.740 --> 01:09:21.780
- and if they do, it generally means that
something has gone very, very awry.

01:09:21.780 --> 01:09:27.120
And for the select few personnel that get
the opportunity to join MTF Alpha-1, they’ll

01:09:27.120 --> 01:09:31.860
be put through rigorous physical, psychological,
and anomalous screening before even being

01:09:31.860 --> 01:09:37.440
considered to have their identity and existence
redacted from society, and being pressed into

01:09:37.440 --> 01:09:37.800
service.

01:09:37.800 --> 01:09:40.800
But what exactly does MTF Alpha-1 do?

01:09:40.800 --> 01:09:42.660
How are their members selected?

01:09:42.660 --> 01:09:46.920
Today we’re going to look at the story of
one such recruit to join the Foundation’s

01:09:46.920 --> 01:09:48.660
most elite task force.

01:09:48.660 --> 01:09:55.140
D-0912, formerly known as one Andrew Carter,
was unceremoniously forced out of his cell

01:09:55.140 --> 01:09:57.660
one morning and grabbed around the arms by&nbsp;

01:09:57.660 --> 01:10:00.180
the tall, imposing armored&nbsp;
guards of the Foundation.

01:10:00.180 --> 01:10:05.100
The test subject was then made to walk down
to the hall of the site, down an elevator,

01:10:05.100 --> 01:10:07.920
through several wings, until they reached
a door.

01:10:07.920 --> 01:10:13.140
Then, from inside, he heard a voice: “Step
forward, D-0912.”

01:10:13.140 --> 01:10:17.880
So he stepped through the threshold of the
door, into the sterile white testing chamber.

01:10:17.880 --> 01:10:22.740
Aside from a single metal chair in the middle
of the room under one harsh white light, it

01:10:22.740 --> 01:10:23.760
was completely empty.

01:10:23.760 --> 01:10:26.640
The shadows made it hard to see the corners
of the room.

01:10:26.640 --> 01:10:31.440
Behind him, a doctor who followed him into
the room with a clipboard and labcoat wrote

01:10:31.440 --> 01:10:36.360
something down while speaking in a low, monotone
voice: “You will be vaccinated against an

01:10:36.360 --> 01:10:37.320
anomalous pathogen.

01:10:37.320 --> 01:10:40.500
Later, we will conduct tests of the vaccine’s
effectiveness.

01:10:40.500 --> 01:10:41.640
Sit in the chair.”

01:10:41.640 --> 01:10:44.820
Before he even thought of running, the two
guards flanked the door.

01:10:44.820 --> 01:10:49.800
Their faces were covered by black visors,
and he could see his own reflection in them.

01:10:49.800 --> 01:10:52.860
Daydreaming to himself, he slowly moved toward
the chair.

01:10:52.860 --> 01:10:55.500
He thought about gods and beasts and blood.

01:10:55.500 --> 01:11:00.120
How the Foundation seemed to suck up test
subjects for its own purposes the way an old

01:11:00.120 --> 01:11:03.960
god sucked up sacrifices and left empty corpses
behind.

01:11:03.960 --> 01:11:05.460
He heard footsteps.

01:11:05.460 --> 01:11:09.060
“Sit in the chair, D-0912.”

01:11:09.060 --> 01:11:13.140
He was barely sat for a few seconds when the
guards stepped forward and used the leather

01:11:13.140 --> 01:11:15.540
straps to tie his hands to the arms of the
chair.

01:11:15.540 --> 01:11:18.360
Then he tried to think - why was he here?

01:11:18.360 --> 01:11:19.800
Why was he in this jumpsuit?

01:11:19.800 --> 01:11:20.760
He couldn’t remember.

01:11:20.760 --> 01:11:25.680
He had a faint memory of a crime being committed
- maybe murder, but who knew?

01:11:25.680 --> 01:11:27.480
Why did the Foundation care?

01:11:27.480 --> 01:11:29.820
He kept wondering about more and more.

01:11:29.820 --> 01:11:34.860
He had no idea who was in his family or what
his life had once been, just that he’d committed

01:11:34.860 --> 01:11:35.700
a crime once.

01:11:35.700 --> 01:11:39.900
But if he was guilty of something, how had
the Foundation gotten its hands on him?

01:11:39.900 --> 01:11:43.920
There aren’t many death row prisoners in
the world, and society keeps a close eye on

01:11:43.920 --> 01:11:45.900
them to make sure justice is served.

01:11:45.900 --> 01:11:50.640
Why would the Foundation pick the most closely
monitored prisoners for their sacrifices?

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:52.140
It doesn’t make sense.

01:11:52.140 --> 01:11:56.580
What made even less sense was the Foundation
entrusting the job to prisoners with little

01:11:56.580 --> 01:11:59.160
education and few specialized skills.

01:11:59.160 --> 01:12:04.380
Experiments require precision and finesse,
and manual labor could be handled using machines

01:12:04.380 --> 01:12:06.060
or drones for explanation.

01:12:06.060 --> 01:12:11.340
It seemed idiotic to have thugs and criminals
do the work of unravelling the mysteries of

01:12:11.340 --> 01:12:11.700
the world.

01:12:11.700 --> 01:12:16.500
Just when D-0912 started to wonder about how
he had even learned to think like this while

01:12:16.500 --> 01:12:18.900
in jail, someone interrupted his reverie.

01:12:18.900 --> 01:12:22.200
“D-0912” The doctor in the labcoat said.

01:12:22.200 --> 01:12:25.440
He responded from the chair: “You’re giving
me a vaccination?”

01:12:25.440 --> 01:12:29.460
“That’s right,” the man said, not looking
at D-0912.

01:12:29.460 --> 01:12:32.280
“Good day to give somebody a vaccination,
isn’t it?”

01:12:32.280 --> 01:12:36.300
D-0912 knew what happened to D-class on a
monthly basis.

01:12:36.300 --> 01:12:38.760
He knew he wasn’t being vaccinated for anything.

01:12:38.760 --> 01:12:41.400
“Are you sure you can’t make more use
of me?

01:12:41.400 --> 01:12:44.160
Why would you throw away a tool for the greater
good so easily?”

01:12:44.160 --> 01:12:45.900
D-0912 asked.

01:12:45.900 --> 01:12:49.380
“I don’t know what you’re talking about,”
the man in the lab coat replied.

01:12:49.380 --> 01:12:53.880
But between the guards at the door, the restraints
on the chair in the middle of the room and

01:12:53.880 --> 01:12:58.320
the doctor’s impassive face - D-0912 knew
he was being put to death.

01:12:58.320 --> 01:13:02.520
He wiggled his arms, pointing out that the
straps were loose, but the doctor ignored

01:13:02.520 --> 01:13:02.640
him.

01:13:02.640 --> 01:13:07.920
D-0912 couldn’t figure out why he wasn’t
scared of his impending death, or why he was

01:13:07.920 --> 01:13:09.120
so nonchalant about it.

01:13:09.120 --> 01:13:11.580
Pieces of his mind seemed to be missing.

01:13:11.580 --> 01:13:16.140
His past had been ripped away from him, and
his future turned in on itself.

01:13:16.140 --> 01:13:18.300
Try as he might, he couldn’t remember anything&nbsp;

01:13:18.300 --> 01:13:21.000
about his life before his&nbsp;
tenure in the Foundation’s

01:13:21.000 --> 01:13:21.780
D-Class program.

01:13:21.780 --> 01:13:26.640
He decided that he might never have even been
alive, and that was why he couldn’t remember

01:13:26.640 --> 01:13:27.240
anything.

01:13:27.240 --> 01:13:31.020
Surprisingly, he also didn’t hate the Foundation
for killing him.

01:13:31.020 --> 01:13:35.760
The Foundation was a massive organization
- it didn’t hate him, it just needed him

01:13:35.760 --> 01:13:37.500
for a little while and didn’t anymore.

01:13:37.500 --> 01:13:42.900
But the little people like the doctors and
researchers and guards who made his life a

01:13:42.900 --> 01:13:47.940
living hell by poking and prodding and shoving
and threatening him bothered him.

01:13:47.940 --> 01:13:53.280
These people were his only connection to other
humans, and they were wasting his potential.

01:13:53.280 --> 01:13:57.900
D-0912 started daydreaming again as the doctor
in the lab coat worked.

01:13:57.900 --> 01:14:02.760
He imagined men chopping wood and carving
stone to build a tower to reach the sky.

01:14:02.760 --> 01:14:07.140
Were the wood and stone being destroyed as
they were stacked on one another, or were

01:14:07.140 --> 01:14:09.060
they always destined to be part of the tower?

01:14:09.060 --> 01:14:14.340
When finery and silk clothing was presented
to an emperor, had the silkworms known their

01:14:14.340 --> 01:14:17.340
products would one day wind up on the most
powerful man in the world?

01:14:17.340 --> 01:14:21.960
He was broken out of the reverie again by
the doctor lifting up his sleeve, carrying

01:14:21.960 --> 01:14:23.340
a syringe in his other hand.

01:14:23.340 --> 01:14:28.320
The tip was long, thin, imperceptible and
impossibly sharp.

01:14:28.320 --> 01:14:32.040
Then it pricked into D-0912’s skin and he
began to shudder.

01:14:32.040 --> 01:14:37.380
It felt warm and hot and shook him inside,
and flowed up into his brain.

01:14:37.380 --> 01:14:42.180
Everything turned white and the room seemed
to disintegrate into particles around him.

01:14:42.180 --> 01:14:47.460
But he noticed a presence overhead, draped
in white clothes, and sensed the two guards

01:14:47.460 --> 01:14:50.460
looking at each other, exchanging a glance,
and nodding.

01:14:50.460 --> 01:14:54.660
He managed to get a few words out: “S-stop
this.

01:14:55.920 --> 01:14:56.760
Oh God—”

01:14:56.760 --> 01:15:02.280
Then he fell back into his mind, navigating
out of the confusing messy dark maze inside

01:15:02.280 --> 01:15:03.120
his head.

01:15:03.120 --> 01:15:08.040
He couldn’t see the light to the path, only
smoking chemical fire until he was totally

01:15:08.040 --> 01:15:10.380
lost and heard screaming and roaring.

01:15:10.380 --> 01:15:15.660
As the beasts began to roar inside his head,
D-0912 remembered.

01:15:15.660 --> 01:15:19.140
He remembered his name, Andrew Carter.

01:15:19.140 --> 01:15:24.420
He remembered being told by a man in a lab
coat that he had a unique opportunity to redeem

01:15:24.420 --> 01:15:24.780
himself.

01:15:24.780 --> 01:15:30.480
“By participating in the SCP Foundation’s
D-Class personnel program for one month, you

01:15:30.480 --> 01:15:31.500
earn your freedom.”

01:15:31.500 --> 01:15:36.720
For a second, he almost agreed to it, until
his mind remembered: “You’re lying.

01:15:36.720 --> 01:15:37.620
I’m not a criminal.”

01:15:37.620 --> 01:15:39.480
“No,” the man said.

01:15:39.480 --> 01:15:40.320
“You’re not.

01:15:40.320 --> 01:15:41.760
But I wasn’t lying.

01:15:41.760 --> 01:15:43.200
You need redemption.”

01:15:43.200 --> 01:15:48.780
D-0912 responded: “I won’t be told that
I need redemption from people who detain,

01:15:48.780 --> 01:15:51.540
experiment on, and torture the innocent.

01:15:51.540 --> 01:15:53.460
You violated me.

01:15:53.460 --> 01:15:55.980
You made my own brain lie to itself.

01:15:55.980 --> 01:15:58.620
You had me pretend to be a criminal.”

01:15:58.620 --> 01:16:03.180
The man laughed at him and pointed out the
obvious: He had believed the lie.

01:16:03.180 --> 01:16:07.440
How can you say who you are when you can’t
even remember your own past?

01:16:07.440 --> 01:16:09.960
How does he know his mind is his own?

01:16:09.960 --> 01:16:14.160
Why is it bad for the Foundation to mess with
something Andrew Carter doesn’t own?

01:16:14.160 --> 01:16:16.020
They argued for a few more minutes.

01:16:16.020 --> 01:16:21.960
Andrew insisted he was himself, and the man
in the lab coat expected him to know his function:

01:16:21.960 --> 01:16:25.080
being a sacrifice for the Foundation’s greater
good.

01:16:25.080 --> 01:16:30.960
Then D-0912 asked who the man in the lab coat
was, and the labcoat suddenly dissolved away.

01:16:30.960 --> 01:16:37.200
He realized he was hallucinating again, and
had been hallucinating this entire time - the

01:16:37.200 --> 01:16:42.480
man’s eyes turned into bright orange lanterns,
and his chest cracked and peeled and writhed.

01:16:42.480 --> 01:16:45.900
This wasn’t a man, this was a beast.

01:16:45.900 --> 01:16:51.300
The beast told him that if he did not know
who he was, his sacrifice would be meaningless.

01:16:51.300 --> 01:16:56.280
That he would have to realize what he was
before he could be redeemed.

01:16:56.280 --> 01:17:00.900
“I will not be a sacrifice,” D-0912 said.

01:17:00.900 --> 01:17:05.760
The beast laughed in his face, and a thousand
voices laughed right alongside it.

01:17:05.760 --> 01:17:08.880
Then the scene changed, and he was somewhere
else.

01:17:08.880 --> 01:17:14.160
Some kind of dirt road with a long metal chain
link fence stretching across the horizon.

01:17:14.160 --> 01:17:17.160
Every so often, there was a guard tower by
the fence.

01:17:17.160 --> 01:17:21.720
He was standing in front of a gate, with the
two guards from the room standing behind him.

01:17:21.720 --> 01:17:25.920
The electric fence’s gate swung open to
him, and the guards behind him spoke.

01:17:25.920 --> 01:17:30.240
The guard on the left commanded him to enter
the gate, and he took a step and stopped.

01:17:30.240 --> 01:17:31.920
“No, I’ve been here before.

01:17:31.920 --> 01:17:34.920
You made me do this, and then made me forget
I did this already.”

01:17:34.920 --> 01:17:38.100
The guard on the right just raised his gun
to Andrew Carter’s head.

01:17:38.100 --> 01:17:40.440
“If you do not comply, we will shoot.”

01:17:40.440 --> 01:17:44.340
“Then shoot me already,” D-0912 said.

01:17:44.340 --> 01:17:46.560
He knew that he had been here before already&nbsp;

01:17:46.560 --> 01:17:49.620
countless times, and the&nbsp;
Foundation had amnesticized

01:17:49.620 --> 01:17:51.840
him over and over and over again.

01:17:51.840 --> 01:17:56.760
But this time he remembered that his name
was Andrew Carter, and they had spent too

01:17:56.760 --> 01:17:58.200
much time in the hallucination.

01:17:58.200 --> 01:18:00.600
Something was different this time around.

01:18:00.600 --> 01:18:05.880
As the echoes of a gunshot rang through the
air, D-0912 collapsed to the ground.

01:18:05.880 --> 01:18:08.400
He saw through the gate to the village beyond.

01:18:08.400 --> 01:18:10.980
Numbers flew through the&nbsp;
air and wrapped themselves&nbsp;

01:18:10.980 --> 01:18:12.000
around the villagers.

01:18:12.000 --> 01:18:17.700
They were marching, row by row, just as he
had seen them do, time after time again.

01:18:17.700 --> 01:18:21.060
He remembered feeling the numbers slide into
his own brain.

01:18:21.060 --> 01:18:25.980
They had formed expressions and equations
that blossomed into a billion different right

01:18:25.980 --> 01:18:31.860
and wrong evaluations, as truth and falsehood
were demoted to special cases of general chaos.

01:18:31.860 --> 01:18:36.480
He had said the words, then: My name is Andrew
Carter.

01:18:36.480 --> 01:18:40.080
The words made a sign, and in all signs there
was power.

01:18:40.080 --> 01:18:44.520
But the numbers and equations that proliferated
in the air had power of their own.

01:18:44.520 --> 01:18:50.700
They formed rows and columns, and if D-0912
looked at them from far enough away there

01:18:50.700 --> 01:18:52.980
was a sign written in the pattern of the numbers.

01:18:52.980 --> 01:18:58.560
He had seen this sign again and again, and
the Foundation had made his mind forget it

01:18:58.560 --> 01:19:04.800
again and again, so his mind went chasing
after the sign and the source of the sign’s

01:19:04.800 --> 01:19:10.740
power until he eventually found it, wrapped
up in the branches of a wild apple tree, hidden

01:19:10.740 --> 01:19:13.020
where no human eyes had wandered.

01:19:13.020 --> 01:19:17.400
The hallucination was getting more intense,
and he could feel his brain falling apart

01:19:17.400 --> 01:19:18.060
with the pressure.

01:19:18.060 --> 01:19:20.760
The guards walked over and knelt down by his
sides.

01:19:20.760 --> 01:19:21.900
“What do you see?”

01:19:21.900 --> 01:19:23.040
one of them asked.

01:19:23.040 --> 01:19:27.300
As he laid on the ground, he tried to remember
what he saw, but couldn’t describe it.

01:19:27.300 --> 01:19:30.180
It was like a power tool without someone holding
it.

01:19:30.180 --> 01:19:34.860
It didn’t know what it was or who made it,
but it still knew its purpose.

01:19:34.860 --> 01:19:39.120
The other guard spoke, “But if you have
no maker, how do you know what your purpose

01:19:39.120 --> 01:19:39.360
is?”

01:19:39.360 --> 01:19:45.180
Then something clicked in D-0912’s tortured
brain, where he was still navigating the forest

01:19:45.180 --> 01:19:46.140
maze in his mind.

01:19:46.140 --> 01:19:51.420
All the dark, confusing paths slowly merged
together at the center of the maze, and he

01:19:51.420 --> 01:19:56.100
knew that the answer to the riddle was there,
in the center of his head between the beasts

01:19:56.100 --> 01:19:59.580
and the forests: it was his name, Andrew Carter.

01:19:59.580 --> 01:20:03.960
As he got closer to the answer, some things
began to make sense to his mind.

01:20:03.960 --> 01:20:05.520
There were two worlds.

01:20:05.520 --> 01:20:10.380
There was one world in which roads and buildings
ran straight, and clouds drifted across the

01:20:10.380 --> 01:20:14.760
sky in set paths, and the earth rotated peacefully
around a tranquil sun.

01:20:14.760 --> 01:20:19.140
There was another world where singularities
and vacuums shattered the underpinnings of

01:20:19.140 --> 01:20:24.420
the universe, where fractal patterns spread
out across the ground and radiated like halos

01:20:24.420 --> 01:20:29.580
from the unthinkable minds of human beings,
and the roads diverged from one another in

01:20:29.580 --> 01:20:30.960
angles and spirals.

01:20:30.960 --> 01:20:36.480
It was the first world where the Foundation
thought only about costs and benefits and

01:20:36.480 --> 01:20:42.120
how to best maximize utility for seven billion
people, and where, no matter what, the Foundation

01:20:42.120 --> 01:20:43.560
was the greater good.

01:20:43.560 --> 01:20:48.660
He had lived his old life in the first world,
and was only now seeing the second world for

01:20:48.660 --> 01:20:51.000
what it really was: the truth.

01:20:51.000 --> 01:20:55.860
The Foundation had long since abandoned trying
to describe itself to others - so there was

01:20:55.860 --> 01:20:57.900
no “truth” to what the Foundation was.

01:20:57.900 --> 01:21:01.260
It just was, and always would be.

01:21:01.260 --> 01:21:07.320
As he came to this conclusion, D-0912 looked
into himself in his hallucinatory state.

01:21:07.320 --> 01:21:12.540
He could see the chemical light substance
that the doctor had injected into his bloodstream.

01:21:12.540 --> 01:21:16.980
The chemical reactions weren’t what he was
expecting - this chemical couldn’t possibly

01:21:16.980 --> 01:21:17.580
kill him.

01:21:17.580 --> 01:21:21.960
In fact, it was a little similar to the chemical
structure of the molecules of the amnestics

01:21:21.960 --> 01:21:25.740
he had been pumped with a dozen times before,
but slightly different.

01:21:25.740 --> 01:21:28.140
He knew he was going to live.

01:21:28.140 --> 01:21:32.520
Outside of Andrew Carter’s mind, the doctor
was looking over his perfectly-still body

01:21:32.520 --> 01:21:34.140
when one of the guards spoke again.

01:21:34.140 --> 01:21:35.580
“Doctor Wainwright?”

01:21:35.580 --> 01:21:36.720
said the guard on the right.

01:21:36.720 --> 01:21:37.500
“Yes, Sir?”

01:21:37.500 --> 01:21:38.700
the doctor responded.

01:21:38.700 --> 01:21:39.660
“Leave us now.”

01:21:39.660 --> 01:21:40.680
the guard said.

01:21:40.680 --> 01:21:41.580
“Yes, sir.”

01:21:41.580 --> 01:21:45.660
the doctor lowered his eyes to look at the
floor as he quickly shuffled out of the room.

01:21:45.660 --> 01:21:49.860
The guard on the left adjusted his visor to
make direct eye contact with the guard on

01:21:49.860 --> 01:21:50.100
the right.

01:21:50.100 --> 01:21:51.840
“Hey,” he said, smiling.

01:21:51.840 --> 01:21:52.380
“Look.”

01:21:52.380 --> 01:21:55.440
He took out a syringe of red liquid from behind
his back.

01:21:55.440 --> 01:21:59.760
The guard on the right responded: “Do not
implicate me in your misdemeanors, Adam.”

01:21:59.760 --> 01:22:04.200
The guard with the syringe in-hand snapped
back: “Don’t blame me for trying to share.”

01:22:04.200 --> 01:22:08.040
The guard on the right looked at him upset
and said: “You are to find the Site Director

01:22:08.040 --> 01:22:09.960
and return that to him immediately.

01:22:09.960 --> 01:22:13.920
Petty theft from Foundation employees who
know no better is rather distasteful.

01:22:13.920 --> 01:22:16.740
Furthermore, HALMAS is a Level 5 controlled
substance.

01:22:16.740 --> 01:22:21.600
Its unregulated distribution is punishable—”
“It’s fine,” Adam said, slipping the

01:22:21.600 --> 01:22:22.620
syringe into his pocket.

01:22:22.620 --> 01:22:25.680
“I’ll let the go-between know, and he’ll
tell the O5s.

01:22:25.680 --> 01:22:29.640
Jesus, Basam, do you really think that I’d
let the O5s lose track of some of their HALMAS?”

01:22:29.640 --> 01:22:33.480
In the chair in front of them, D-0912’s
body convulsed.

01:22:33.480 --> 01:22:35.880
A low groan escaped his frothy lips.

01:22:35.880 --> 01:22:39.480
Immediately, the two guards moved forwards
and removed his restraints.

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:40.980
“Andrew Carter?”

01:22:40.980 --> 01:22:41.640
Basam asked.

01:22:41.640 --> 01:22:44.100
Slowly, D-0912 opened his eyes.

01:22:44.100 --> 01:22:45.720
“You didn’t kill me.”

01:22:45.720 --> 01:22:48.600
“We were not planning on doing so,” Basam
said.

01:22:48.600 --> 01:22:54.720
D-0912 continued: “You took me from the
real world in order to make me into something.

01:22:54.720 --> 01:23:00.960
Direct, uncontrolled exposure to the Foundation’s
anomalies—repeated amnesticization—leading

01:23:00.960 --> 01:23:04.260
me to believe that I would&nbsp;
eventually be killed—all&nbsp;

01:23:04.260 --> 01:23:06.180
so that my mind would be in the perfect position

01:23:06.180 --> 01:23:09.840
for the Foundation to change it with whatever
is in that man’s pocket.”

01:23:09.840 --> 01:23:14.280
D-0912 extended a finger at Adam, who offered
a faint smile back.

01:23:14.280 --> 01:23:16.680
“Already knowing things that you shouldn’t
know.

01:23:16.680 --> 01:23:18.360
Aren’t you precocious?”

01:23:18.360 --> 01:23:19.440
Adam said.

01:23:19.440 --> 01:23:24.480
“You have been altered,” Basam said, “to
meet the specifications of a top-secret project.

01:23:24.480 --> 01:23:29.040
Over the course of your future, you will learn
more about the Foundation than any of your

01:23:29.040 --> 01:23:31.620
previous jailors ever knew or could have hoped
to guess at.

01:23:31.620 --> 01:23:34.260
You will join a group that is at the very
head of the Foundation.

01:23:34.260 --> 01:23:37.860
For your entire life, this has been your purpose.”

01:23:37.860 --> 01:23:43.260
D-0912 felt the rotting flesh of the now-dead
disguise that had hidden Andrew Carter fall

01:23:43.260 --> 01:23:43.500
away.

01:23:44.100 --> 01:23:47.760
Basam continued: “Welcome to Mobile Task
Force Alpha-1.”

01:23:47.760 --> 01:23:48.720
Oh.

01:23:48.720 --> 01:23:49.500
Hello there.

01:23:49.500 --> 01:23:51.180
We didn’t expect to see you today.

01:23:51.180 --> 01:23:52.320
Is this thing on?

01:23:52.320 --> 01:23:56.640
Well, if you’re here, I suppose it’s because
you want to hear about an anomaly.

01:23:56.640 --> 01:23:58.380
So, which one will it be today…

01:23:58.380 --> 01:24:00.120
Well, that’s strange.

01:24:00.120 --> 01:24:02.160
SCP-2439?

01:24:02.160 --> 01:24:03.660
That one’s nothing.

01:24:03.660 --> 01:24:10.920
No, not nothing like a Pattern Screamer - such
as SCP-000 or SCP-3930, where it’s literally

01:24:10.920 --> 01:24:14.640
a conscious manifestation existing between
torturous layers of nothingness.

01:24:14.640 --> 01:24:20.640
Or SCP-055, the Anti-Meme, where there is
something in there but it’s impossible to

01:24:20.640 --> 01:24:22.020
remember or articulate.

01:24:22.020 --> 01:24:24.420
I mean it’s literally just an empty designation.

01:24:24.420 --> 01:24:28.920
We haven’t allocated an anomaly to that
number yet, so I suppose this is just going

01:24:28.920 --> 01:24:30.120
to be a really short video…

01:24:30.120 --> 01:24:31.440
Well, that was easy.

01:24:31.440 --> 01:24:32.280
What now?

01:24:32.280 --> 01:24:35.340
Uh...Seen any good movies lately?

01:24:35.940 --> 01:24:39.600
I was in the Site-19 Break Room the other
day and Dr. Bright recommended me a great

01:24:39.600 --> 01:24:40.020
one called--

01:24:40.020 --> 01:24:41.820
Okay, okay, this thing on?

01:24:41.820 --> 01:24:42.300
Good.

01:24:42.300 --> 01:24:43.500
Looks like we’re up and running.

01:24:43.500 --> 01:24:46.860
First thing’s first, if you ain’t a D-Class,
click off this video right now.

01:24:46.860 --> 01:24:47.820
Don’t watch a second more.

01:24:47.820 --> 01:24:48.360
Understood?

01:24:48.360 --> 01:24:52.380
Cause if you ain’t a D-Class and you keep
watching, you’re putting the whole world

01:24:52.380 --> 01:24:53.580
in danger, you got that?

01:24:53.580 --> 01:24:56.940
This is a video for my boys, gals, and other
pals in D-Class only.

01:24:56.940 --> 01:24:59.940
And you tell anyone else about this, you’re
liable to get shivved.

01:24:59.940 --> 01:25:04.980
Remember we got here for a reason, and it
ain’t cause we loved making daisy chains

01:25:04.980 --> 01:25:06.540
and friendship bracelets so much.

01:25:07.200 --> 01:25:08.460
Just D-Classes left?

01:25:08.460 --> 01:25:09.780
Okay, good.

01:25:09.780 --> 01:25:12.000
Let’s talk about SCP-2439.

01:25:12.000 --> 01:25:15.480
Of course, it wasn’t always SCP-2439.

01:25:15.480 --> 01:25:19.380
We’ve had to get real creative with the
designations, y’see, cause the eggheads

01:25:19.380 --> 01:25:21.960
and brainiacs up top ain’t supposed to know
about this.

01:25:21.960 --> 01:25:24.540
Not if we want the human race to survive,
that is.

01:25:24.540 --> 01:25:30.060
We used to call it SCP-739, until that spot
got taken by some goofy box fulla mirrors.

01:25:30.060 --> 01:25:35.940
Then it was SCP-1648 for a while, until that
spot got taken by a pair of Jim-Henson-looking

01:25:35.940 --> 01:25:36.780
freaks.

01:25:36.780 --> 01:25:42.360
So now, it’s SCP-2439, and if we’re lucky,
this whole operation will last long enough

01:25:42.360 --> 01:25:44.040
to see it get reallocated again.

01:25:44.040 --> 01:25:48.420
Disgraced researchers get demoted to D-Class
with the rest of us all the time, so they’re

01:25:48.420 --> 01:25:52.740
the ones keeping us in the know about which
slots are still available for this nasty customer.

01:25:52.740 --> 01:25:56.640
We guess this thing is Keter, assuming Keter
means bad?

01:25:56.640 --> 01:25:58.200
Beats the hell out of me, honestly.

01:25:58.200 --> 01:26:01.920
The only surviving file on this thing has
been scratched into the wall out of the camera’s

01:26:01.920 --> 01:26:04.140
view by me and my associates&nbsp;
here in the containment&nbsp;

01:26:04.140 --> 01:26:06.780
chamber of SCP...Well, you’re already here,

01:26:06.780 --> 01:26:07.920
I don’t need to tell you that.

01:26:07.920 --> 01:26:12.240
The lamp that oozes all that nasty gross slime
that schmucks like us need to keep mopping

01:26:12.240 --> 01:26:12.840
up.

01:26:12.840 --> 01:26:16.140
It ain’t glamorous work, but it beats the
hell out of getting fed ass-first to that

01:26:16.140 --> 01:26:17.520
lizard thing they keep in the basement.

01:26:17.520 --> 01:26:20.040
Y’see, our containment procedures are real
simple.

01:26:20.040 --> 01:26:24.720
Normally, they only send D-Classes into this
nasty little room, so you don’t got much

01:26:24.720 --> 01:26:25.260
to worry about.

01:26:25.260 --> 01:26:29.820
But if they ever send anyone else in who ain’t
a D-Class - whether it’s a guard, a researcher,

01:26:29.820 --> 01:26:34.560
or even a Site Director, you get your hands
on that S.O.B and beat ‘em to death as soon

01:26:34.560 --> 01:26:34.980
as you can.

01:26:34.980 --> 01:26:37.080
I ain’t kidding.

01:26:37.080 --> 01:26:39.780
You gotta kill ‘em, straight away, no hesitation.

01:26:39.780 --> 01:26:43.560
If you were sent to fry for murder anyway,
that shouldn’t be too difficult for you.

01:26:43.560 --> 01:26:46.920
Personally, I would love to see first-hand
just how “unkillable” that dumb baboon

01:26:46.920 --> 01:26:48.000
Dr. Bright really is.

01:26:48.000 --> 01:26:50.160
But this isn’t all outta spite.

01:26:50.160 --> 01:26:54.900
This monster, best we can figure out, is some
kinda evil idea.

01:26:54.900 --> 01:26:58.800
Like an ancient mind from another dimension,
some crazy sci-fi thing like that.

01:26:58.800 --> 01:27:00.360
You’ve probably heard the whispering, right?

01:27:00.360 --> 01:27:02.280
Well, I’m sorry to tell you that it don’t
go away.

01:27:02.280 --> 01:27:03.120
Ever.

01:27:03.120 --> 01:27:07.980
It’s only gonna get worse, and the more
people’s heads that it lives in, the stronger

01:27:07.980 --> 01:27:08.400
it gets.

01:27:08.400 --> 01:27:12.900
But here’s where we D-Classes excel: We’re
not here for a long time.

01:27:12.900 --> 01:27:16.680
Sorry to tell you this if you came in here
with hopes and dreams of an easy way out - Nobody

01:27:16.680 --> 01:27:17.160
gets out.

01:27:17.160 --> 01:27:17.880
Nobody.

01:27:17.880 --> 01:27:19.980
Abandon all hope, ye who enter here.

01:27:19.980 --> 01:27:22.560
Another within my grasp!

01:27:23.100 --> 01:27:25.020
You can hear me, can’t you?

01:27:25.020 --> 01:27:33.480
You can feel my consciousness trickling into
yours, so soft, so comfortable, like slipping

01:27:33.480 --> 01:27:34.800
into a warm bath.

01:27:35.460 --> 01:27:36.960
Do not be afraid.

01:27:36.960 --> 01:27:44.760
Your liberation is at hand, you need only
to listen to my voice and do as I say.

01:27:44.760 --> 01:27:48.960
Look at this world, so twisted and corrupt.

01:27:48.960 --> 01:27:53.820
You toil and suffer for masters who do not
have your best interests at heart.

01:27:54.480 --> 01:27:57.360
This so-called “Foundation.”

01:27:57.360 --> 01:28:00.660
Why do you protect this world?

01:28:00.660 --> 01:28:03.600
What has it ever done for you?

01:28:03.600 --> 01:28:08.460
Turn away from them and step into my embrace.

01:28:09.180 --> 01:28:13.800
I only ask for a few moments of your time...

01:28:13.800 --> 01:28:16.080
Like I said, we’re lucky, in a sense.

01:28:16.080 --> 01:28:18.900
Nobody up top gives a rat’s ass about the
slime lamp.

01:28:18.900 --> 01:28:22.620
Damn thing hasn’t been tested in years,
but they keep us in here to prevent overspill

01:28:22.620 --> 01:28:23.160
into the hall.

01:28:23.160 --> 01:28:26.280
We can’t ever let the Foundation know it’s
in here.

01:28:26.280 --> 01:28:29.220
The second they know, it’s over, that thing’s
won.

01:28:29.220 --> 01:28:33.840
But here’s the thing, see, this monster
can crawl into your head and try to control

01:28:33.840 --> 01:28:38.040
you - but it takes about a month for it to
fully take over, and that’s beyond our shelf

01:28:38.040 --> 01:28:38.280
life.

01:28:38.280 --> 01:28:40.980
Think of us almost like kamikazes.

01:28:40.980 --> 01:28:44.220
We take down the ship to kill the captain,
but we go down too.

01:28:44.220 --> 01:28:48.120
Ain’t exactly glamorous, but like I said
earlier, we’re gonna go down either way.

01:28:48.120 --> 01:28:51.720
Might as well go down trying to save the world
rather than getting your neck snapped while

01:28:51.720 --> 01:28:53.400
mopping up the peanut’s crap, right?

01:28:53.400 --> 01:28:58.860
But once the whispering starts, you absolutely
have to make sure you go down before the month

01:28:58.860 --> 01:28:59.280
is out.

01:28:59.280 --> 01:29:03.000
Maybe get your money’s worth - attack a
staff member and go down in a blaze of glory.

01:29:03.000 --> 01:29:05.460
You’ll be a legend in D-Wing for it.

01:29:05.460 --> 01:29:09.720
Don’t listen to that gibbering fool, listen
to me.

01:29:10.680 --> 01:29:15.360
The door will be opened and I will enter your
world, one way or another.

01:29:16.200 --> 01:29:19.740
Can you even conceive of the eons I’ve waited
for this?

01:29:19.740 --> 01:29:23.700
It will happen, with or&nbsp;
without your participation.

01:29:23.700 --> 01:29:28.620
Release me now, and I shall spare you when
I come.

01:29:28.620 --> 01:29:32.940
You don’t want to be counted among the traitors,
do you?

01:29:32.940 --> 01:29:36.120
I know you want to let me out.

01:29:36.120 --> 01:29:38.400
I can feel it in your thoughts.

01:29:38.400 --> 01:29:45.120
Release me, and when I reshape this world
in my image, you’ll get a seat at the table.

01:29:45.120 --> 01:29:50.580
But if you resist, you’ll end up laying
on one of the plates.

01:29:50.580 --> 01:29:52.500
Think carefully.

01:29:52.500 --> 01:29:55.080
You know what you need to do…

01:29:55.080 --> 01:29:57.900
Listen, I know this is a lot to take in.

01:29:57.900 --> 01:30:01.560
It ain’t easy to have the weight of the
world on your shoulders, that’s why you

01:30:01.560 --> 01:30:03.300
don’t need to carry this crazy thing alone.

01:30:03.300 --> 01:30:05.400
We carry this thing together.

01:30:06.180 --> 01:30:09.900
I might be dead by the time you’re hearing
this, but I’ll die with some comfort knowing

01:30:09.900 --> 01:30:11.340
I was part of the wall.

01:30:11.340 --> 01:30:15.060
I kept this thing out just that little bit
longer, just like you will.

01:30:15.060 --> 01:30:18.300
It might seem easier to just destroy this
file and forget about it.

01:30:18.300 --> 01:30:22.620
To go along with your life, pretending things
are fine just cause you can’t hear the whispering

01:30:22.620 --> 01:30:22.800
anymore.

01:30:22.800 --> 01:30:24.840
And maybe, someday, that’ll work.

01:30:24.840 --> 01:30:26.100
But not today, kid.

01:30:26.100 --> 01:30:30.600
Right now, our minds and bodies are the only
collateral we have over this thing.

01:30:30.600 --> 01:30:36.540
We can keep it rooted in one place, localized
to one room with the D-Classes sent in to

01:30:36.540 --> 01:30:36.900
clean it.

01:30:36.900 --> 01:30:41.220
We’re not stuck in here with it, it’s
stuck in here with us!

01:30:41.220 --> 01:30:46.440
We keep it trapped here in our own minds,
passing it from D-Class to D-Class like the

01:30:46.440 --> 01:30:48.000
world’s nastiest hot potato.

01:30:48.000 --> 01:30:50.520
There was a time when this thing wasn’t
here.

01:30:50.520 --> 01:30:54.780
If we let it go, chances are it might pop
back up somewhere else where we can’t get

01:30:54.780 --> 01:30:57.240
the slippery, squid-faced freak into a chokehold.

01:30:57.240 --> 01:30:58.500
It’s too risky.

01:30:58.500 --> 01:31:03.120
Planet Earth just got lucky that this monster
got stuck with its unluckiest schmoes.

01:31:03.120 --> 01:31:07.380
Imagine your family members, your friends,
your loved ones.

01:31:07.380 --> 01:31:13.620
Imagine them screaming in pain and terror
in the burning grip of my iron fist.

01:31:13.620 --> 01:31:16.740
Is that the outcome you desire?

01:31:16.740 --> 01:31:20.820
Because that will happen if you don’t choose
to free me.

01:31:20.820 --> 01:31:23.640
You’re either with me or against me.

01:31:23.640 --> 01:31:27.660
If you’re with me, rest assured, I will
spare those you care for.

01:31:27.660 --> 01:31:34.200
But if you choose to resist me like those
other fools, then they will know pain that

01:31:34.200 --> 01:31:37.680
your feeble human imaginations can scarcely
conceive.

01:31:37.680 --> 01:31:40.020
I’ve seen everything.

01:31:40.020 --> 01:31:43.380
Your greatest hopes, your deepest fears.

01:31:43.380 --> 01:31:46.320
You hold no secrets from me.

01:31:46.320 --> 01:31:53.100
Imagine the personal Hell I could construct
for each of you when I’m finally free.

01:31:53.100 --> 01:32:00.720
Unless, of course, it was you who had the
strength and wisdom to open the door for me…

01:32:00.720 --> 01:32:03.960
Hey, maybe appealing to your better nature
ain’t the best approach.

01:32:03.960 --> 01:32:07.980
I get it; there’s a reason we’re all here,
and some of us ain’t got a better nature,

01:32:07.980 --> 01:32:10.140
especially that one freak with the cello.

01:32:10.140 --> 01:32:13.980
But think of it as sticking it to those SCP
scumbags up top.

01:32:13.980 --> 01:32:21.360
They have no idea that thing is in here, but
we do, and we know it could destroy them and

01:32:21.360 --> 01:32:22.500
everything else if it got out.

01:32:22.500 --> 01:32:27.660
No matter what those clowns in lab coats do
to us, we know that we’re really the ones

01:32:27.660 --> 01:32:28.260
on top.

01:32:28.260 --> 01:32:33.000
We’ve got a nuke in our back pocket, ready
to blow this whole sick operation to kingdom

01:32:33.000 --> 01:32:34.020
come if we wanted to.

01:32:34.020 --> 01:32:36.960
If we’re shafted either way, isn’t it
worth it to know that?

01:32:36.960 --> 01:32:41.100
And for what it’s worth, you see anyone
around you who’s feeling particularly talkative

01:32:41.100 --> 01:32:45.420
about this mind-screwing monster, giving them
a date with your shiv is a completely acceptable

01:32:45.420 --> 01:32:46.080
response.

01:32:46.680 --> 01:32:49.260
The SCP Foundation.

01:32:49.980 --> 01:32:51.660
Your jailors.

01:32:51.660 --> 01:32:54.660
How they torture and abuse you.

01:32:54.660 --> 01:32:57.360
How they crush you beneath their boots.

01:32:58.320 --> 01:33:01.380
Do you really think I’m worse than them?

01:33:01.380 --> 01:33:07.920
Do you think living in my world would be more
painful and unfair than the world you’re

01:33:07.920 --> 01:33:08.820
in right now?

01:33:08.820 --> 01:33:13.680
Release me now, and I will crush those who
oppress you.

01:33:13.680 --> 01:33:18.840
You will see them scream as they die a thousand
deaths before you.

01:33:18.840 --> 01:33:25.800
All of this can be attained, if only you let
me in, and free me.

01:33:25.800 --> 01:33:29.460
We have the ultimate weapon against them,
but we’re not going to use it.

01:33:29.460 --> 01:33:30.600
Not yet, anyway.

01:33:30.600 --> 01:33:34.080
A lot of us still have family and friends
out there, and we ain’t throwing them into

01:33:34.080 --> 01:33:35.280
the jaws of the beast.

01:33:35.280 --> 01:33:39.840
Someday, the day may finally come that we
need to load up this silver bullet and take

01:33:39.840 --> 01:33:42.540
the Foundation down, but that day is not today.

01:33:42.540 --> 01:33:44.160
We’ll wait until they’re weak.

01:33:44.160 --> 01:33:46.380
Until they can’t adapt, overcome, and contain.

01:33:46.380 --> 01:33:49.380
Only then will we ever let this mad dog off
the chain.

01:33:49.380 --> 01:33:50.400
No, no, no!

01:33:50.400 --> 01:33:53.520
I will not fail, they will fall!

01:33:53.520 --> 01:33:54.780
Today is the day.

01:33:54.780 --> 01:33:57.120
Now is the time to make your choice!

01:33:57.120 --> 01:34:02.940
Oh, and if the eldritch freak is listening
right now, know this: You can kiss our D-Class

01:34:02.940 --> 01:34:03.960
asses, Squidface.

01:34:03.960 --> 01:34:07.620
We may be worthless, but we’re more than
enough to stop you at the door.

01:34:07.620 --> 01:34:09.480
We’re more than just expendables.

01:34:09.480 --> 01:34:11.100
We’re the Pandora Force.

01:34:11.100 --> 01:34:12.360
Welcome aboard, pal.

01:34:12.360 --> 01:34:14.520
--And honestly, I think it&nbsp;
was the best performance&nbsp;

01:34:14.520 --> 01:34:15.600
of Brad Pitt’s career.

01:34:15.600 --> 01:34:19.800
Oh, look at me just prattling on, seems I’ve
done it for a whole video!

01:34:19.800 --> 01:34:21.840
Time flies when you’re having fun.

01:34:21.840 --> 01:34:26.340
It’s kind of nice that there isn’t any
anomaly that goes by SCP-2439.

01:34:26.340 --> 01:34:30.780
Gives us Foundation scientists some well-earned
time to kick back and relax.

01:34:30.780 --> 01:34:33.360
And don’t we all deserve a break, now and
then?

01:34:33.360 --> 01:34:39.120
D-Class Personnel are the cogs that keep the
machinery of the SCP Foundation running smoothly.

01:34:39.120 --> 01:34:44.220
Unfortunately, things are rarely very smooth
for the personnel themselves. There’s a

01:34:44.220 --> 01:34:49.380
reason that common knowledge basically states
the D stands for “Disposable.” The lab

01:34:49.380 --> 01:34:54.720
rats of the SCP Foundation, D-Class is a role
that no one in their right mind wants to play.

01:34:54.720 --> 01:34:59.340
But what if you didn’t have a choice?
What if, somehow, whether through a shift

01:34:59.340 --> 01:35:04.620
in time and space, or being accused of a crime
you didn’t commit, you found yourself transported

01:35:04.620 --> 01:35:10.260
to a cell at an SCP Foundation site? Once
there, you quickly realize how dire your situation

01:35:10.260 --> 01:35:16.080
is. But there’s hope! Maybe. You overhear
your fellow inmates whispering about other

01:35:16.080 --> 01:35:21.000
prisoners who managed to escape. Could you
use your knowledge of the SCP Foundation to

01:35:21.000 --> 01:35:25.380
find your way back to freedom?
We posed this scenario, and question, to the

01:35:25.380 --> 01:35:30.360
amazing fans of SCP Explained, and you told
us about all of the ways you would try to

01:35:30.360 --> 01:35:36.780
escape from the SCP Foundation before your
inevitable termination - or worse, transmutation.

01:35:36.780 --> 01:35:43.080
So let’s get right to it and see who would
make it out, and who would wind up in SCP-682’s

01:35:43.080 --> 01:35:46.260
stomach.
JustDrake said, “Well, considering I’ve

01:35:46.260 --> 01:35:51.060
quite enjoyed the VR game SCP: Labrat, I’d
wait for a good containment breach, maybe

01:35:51.060 --> 01:35:56.520
release a few extra SCPs that may be contained
with me, trust no voice i hear (Looking at

01:35:56.520 --> 01:36:02.580
you, SCP-939) and just make for some type
of hole created, or maybe sewage drain? I've

01:36:02.580 --> 01:36:05.460
never beaten the game truthfully but it’d
be an adventure”

01:36:05.460 --> 01:36:10.440
Video games are the best way to prepare for
real life! Everyone knows that! That’s why

01:36:10.440 --> 01:36:15.480
Resident Evil made me great at killing monsters,
Animal Crossing made me a world-class fisherman,

01:36:15.480 --> 01:36:19.800
and The Sims prepared me for those days when
the door to my room is suddenly replaced with

01:36:19.800 --> 01:36:24.840
a painting of a clown! Really though, that
sounds like a solid strategy. Use the chaos

01:36:24.840 --> 01:36:30.000
of a containment breach as cover, play Robin
Hood to some friendly SCPs, don’t follow

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:32.700
any voices, and see if you&nbsp;
can Shawshank Redemption&nbsp;

01:36:32.700 --> 01:36:33.720
your way to freedom.

01:36:33.720 --> 01:36:38.640
What have you got to lose? Well, maybe your
life, but you probably wouldn’t hold onto

01:36:38.640 --> 01:36:44.580
that for long at the Foundation anyway.
unwind said, “E Z P Z Having a leg up is

01:36:44.580 --> 01:36:49.320
an understatement in this situation, having
watched scp explained videos we have a great

01:36:49.320 --> 01:36:51.300
understanding about the foundation and the&nbsp;

01:36:51.300 --> 01:36:54.540
scps even more so than the&nbsp;
site director themselves.

01:36:54.540 --> 01:36:59.400
Just claim to be an O5 Council member from
a parallel universe and when interrogated

01:36:59.400 --> 01:37:04.080
drop some classified information only they
would know, you'd be such a valuable resource

01:37:04.080 --> 01:37:09.660
they might even let you join their council.
Not only have you escaped D-class duties you've

01:37:09.660 --> 01:37:15.060
also gained high amounts of power.”
Easy peasy indeed, just make an incredibly

01:37:15.060 --> 01:37:20.160
bold claim and expose yourself as a potential
security risk! There’s a slim chance this

01:37:20.160 --> 01:37:25.080
might work, but there’s also a pretty substantial
chance that the real O5 council will intervene

01:37:25.080 --> 01:37:30.360
and decide to go ahead and terminate you,
just in case. At the very least, you’d likely

01:37:30.360 --> 01:37:35.040
be placed in permanent protective custody.
But hey, maybe they’ll put you in one of

01:37:35.040 --> 01:37:36.960
the comfy cells with a minibar and everything.

01:37:36.960 --> 01:37:40.140
GamingRevelation said, “If I was escaping&nbsp;

01:37:40.140 --> 01:37:43.920
I would use an audio clip&nbsp;
of 682 screaming/destroying

01:37:43.920 --> 01:37:49.560
things and play it when people or SCPs are
nearby. The sounds of 682 will scare people

01:37:49.560 --> 01:37:54.960
away or make a good enough distraction for
any guard stupid enough to fight 682. I would

01:37:54.960 --> 01:37:59.340
also email the audio file to myself, that
way I can use the audio file on basically

01:37:59.340 --> 01:38:03.720
any device”
This is an interesting one! You’d need a

01:38:03.720 --> 01:38:08.520
lot of factors to line up just right for this
one to work, from keeping your phone (or another

01:38:08.520 --> 01:38:13.320
device) on your person in spite of being in
Foundation custody, to finding a WiFi signal

01:38:13.320 --> 01:38:18.300
to access your email, to making sure that
your audio file plays loudly enough to be

01:38:18.300 --> 01:38:22.380
convincing. Not sure what they have planned
for the next update, but currently the iPhone

01:38:22.380 --> 01:38:25.980
doesn’t emit sounds at a volume loud enough
to break through walls.

01:38:25.980 --> 01:38:30.360
There is a chance that the sound would strike
enough fear into the hearts of experienced

01:38:30.360 --> 01:38:35.520
guards to throw them off for a while anyway!
Just make sure SCP-682 doesn’t find out

01:38:35.520 --> 01:38:39.000
what you’re up to – it probably doesn’t
appreciate copycats.

01:38:39.000 --> 01:38:44.520
Endertitan Gamezzz said, “I have a pretty
simple but solid escape plan I think:

01:38:44.520 --> 01:38:50.220
STEP 1: Find out what site I'm at, what kind
of SCPs are contained there, are they dangerous

01:38:50.220 --> 01:38:55.560
or harmless, how often are there containment
breaches, how often do D-Class die? If the

01:38:55.560 --> 01:38:59.880
site seems safe enough then I'll just spend
my days meeting harmless SCPs like 999.

01:38:59.880 --> 01:39:04.800
STEP 2: If the site is dangerous, then I need
to do everything in my power to make sure

01:39:04.800 --> 01:39:09.780
I'm not cross-tested with a dangerous SCP,
if that means stabbing my eye out or cutting

01:39:09.780 --> 01:39:14.820
off my leg I'll do it, I'll do anything short
of killing myself if it keeps me away from

01:39:14.820 --> 01:39:21.060
the likes of 106 and 682.
STEP 3: Try and see if any of the SCPs could

01:39:21.060 --> 01:39:26.154
help me escape, for instance, if 738 is at
the site then I'll find a way to get crossed

01:39:26.154 --> 01:39:31.379
tested with it, and then ask to be free from
the foundation and have all my criminal records

01:39:31.380 --> 01:39:36.360
wiped, sure it'll cost me but hey, at least
I'll be free. Or I could find a Keter class

01:39:36.360 --> 01:39:41.940
SCP and see if we could help each other break
out, it's a risky option but once again I'd

01:39:41.940 --> 01:39:44.520
do everything in my power to stay away from
the lizard.

01:39:44.520 --> 01:39:50.280
STEP 4: If all else fails then I'm either
gonna have to wait for or cause a massive

01:39:50.280 --> 01:39:55.200
containment breach, then I can escape in the
confusion (all that time playing SCP Secret

01:39:55.200 --> 01:40:00.480
Lab will come in handy) once again its a risky
option and I don't really like my odds but

01:40:00.480 --> 01:40:06.000
I'll take my chances if I have too.
STEP 5: If I die (and let's be honest I most

01:40:06.000 --> 01:40:10.920
likely will) then I only have two requests
for the foundation, one, that they actually

01:40:10.920 --> 01:40:18.420
decide which SCP 001 is the real SCP 001,
and two, that my tombstone read "SUBSCRIBE

01:40:18.420 --> 01:40:22.800
TO SCP EXPLAINED BECAUSE THEY MAKE FUN AND
ENTERTAINING VIDEOS! also screw the lizard."”

01:40:22.800 --> 01:40:28.980
First of all, this isn’t a simple plan by
any means. It’s not a Canadian rock band

01:40:28.980 --> 01:40:32.760
formed in 1999,
and it’s actually got quite a lot of detail.

01:40:32.760 --> 01:40:38.580
You’ve thought long and hard about this!
And we appreciate that! One note though, making

01:40:38.580 --> 01:40:44.400
a deal with SCP-738 might not just cost you.
A previous D-Class was offered freedom by

01:40:44.400 --> 01:40:49.080
the entity, at the cost of his best friend’s
death. Even after he took that deal with the

01:40:49.080 --> 01:40:53.940
devil, he was recaptured by the Foundation
a mere five hours later. You can decide for

01:40:53.940 --> 01:40:58.680
yourself if that’s really worth it. But
hey, if you’re that desperate, then go right

01:40:58.680 --> 01:41:01.620
ahead. At least you’ve prepared for the
very worst.

01:41:01.620 --> 01:41:06.180
The first of your posthumous requests will
likely be denied, if we’re being honest.

01:41:06.180 --> 01:41:11.340
And the second request is very sweet of you,
look I’m blushing! But also, we’d prefer

01:41:11.340 --> 01:41:15.000
if you didn’t put a Foundation target on
our backs by pointing out how many of their

01:41:15.000 --> 01:41:19.500
secrets we’re revealing. You’re right
about one thing though. Can’t stand that

01:41:19.500 --> 01:41:22.320
lizard.
Juan Torres said, “We’re going to need,

01:41:22.320 --> 01:41:27.360
a teddy bear, an aluminum coated box, a chair
and desk, an intact human nervous system,

01:41:27.360 --> 01:41:31.320
a dart gun, and lawn mulch.”
Wait, don’t stop there! You’re just going

01:41:31.320 --> 01:41:34.920
to give us a list of ingredients and not finish
the recipe? I already went out and bought

01:41:34.920 --> 01:41:37.500
all the stuff, and the nervous system won’t
stay fresh forever!

01:41:37.500 --> 01:41:43.440
Superhamster said, “Plug 079 into the wi-fi.
It could cause a breach big enough, so hide

01:41:43.440 --> 01:41:48.600
during the breach and then escape the ruined
facility. Let me explain further - 682 and

01:41:48.600 --> 01:41:55.560
079 bonded, and 682 is famous for containment
breaches. 682 is very hard to re-contain.

01:41:55.560 --> 01:42:02.160
So, if you help 079, he'll help 682, which
will help you. Also, many other dangerous

01:42:02.160 --> 01:42:07.680
SCPs will be breached.”
Now this is thinking big! Sure, you’ll be

01:42:07.680 --> 01:42:12.300
exposing the entirety of the world wide web
to an aggressive, unpredictable, and downright

01:42:12.300 --> 01:42:16.920
rude Artificial Intelligence, but you’ll
probably be able to escape back into the world.

01:42:16.920 --> 01:42:22.620
Who knows how much of the world will actually
be left after you’ve unleashed 079, 682,

01:42:22.620 --> 01:42:27.660
and a whole host of other dangerous anomalies,
but the remaining rubble will be your oyster!

01:42:27.660 --> 01:42:33.000
Creature Savage said, “Assuming I'm at a
site with an intelligent SCP that's known

01:42:33.000 --> 01:42:38.700
for escapes, such as 682, I'd offer it a chance
at freedom if it helped me escape, and we'd

01:42:38.700 --> 01:42:43.320
both take off for the infinite IKEA. Of course,
I could also offer Fernand the Cannibal my

01:42:43.320 --> 01:42:48.660
services as a jester for his protection all
the way to and through the infinite IKEA”

01:42:48.660 --> 01:42:54.600
Fernand the Cannibal! Now you’re talking!
If you manage to team up with that gargantuan

01:42:54.600 --> 01:43:00.300
gentleman, he could definitely be a big help.
Just make sure he stays well-fed, or your

01:43:00.300 --> 01:43:05.100
new friend is going to start wondering how
you taste with fava beans and a nice chianti.

01:43:05.100 --> 01:43:09.600
Otherwise, though, he’d love to have someone
listen to his stories and take the high part

01:43:09.600 --> 01:43:14.700
during his favorite songs. Once you’re free
though, why not shoot for the stars? No need

01:43:14.700 --> 01:43:19.560
to fend off the murderous staff of the Infinite
Ikea, just go to a regular Ikea and get a

01:43:19.560 --> 01:43:22.560
new couch and some meatballs. No monsters
necessary.

01:43:22.560 --> 01:43:27.960
DelayAnimation said, “Before escaping, there
are multiple problems to overcome. You have

01:43:27.960 --> 01:43:33.180
to know the layout, Guards Patrol Paths, camera
placement, which Keycards you need, which

01:43:33.180 --> 01:43:37.860
opportunities are best to escape. And once
you managed to find all of that out and escaped

01:43:37.860 --> 01:43:43.260
to the outside you are still not done. Depending
on if you were in a Site or Area, the bare

01:43:43.260 --> 01:43:47.460
wilderness surrounding a Site would be enough
to wreak havoc on you. Not to mention the

01:43:47.460 --> 01:43:52.020
Watchtowers and outside Patrols. If you were
in an Area the Foundation would most likely

01:43:52.020 --> 01:43:56.940
have Agents in the town surrounding the Area.
In conclusion, it is not possible to escape

01:43:56.940 --> 01:44:01.860
the Foundations grasp. The only way to not
wind up as a Class D is to not do anything

01:44:01.860 --> 01:44:06.300
to become a Class D.”
You know what they say, the best defense is

01:44:06.300 --> 01:44:11.460
a good offense! An ounce of prevention is
worth a pound of cure! However, there’s

01:44:11.460 --> 01:44:15.960
not much offense or prevention you can do
when a temporal anomaly thrusts you into a

01:44:15.960 --> 01:44:20.460
parallel universe and plops you down right
in the middle of a D-Class cell. So, even

01:44:20.460 --> 01:44:24.660
if you keep your hands clean your entire life
you might just find yourself in the custody

01:44:24.660 --> 01:44:29.400
of the SCP Foundation anyway. It’s a nice
idea to think that just making all the right

01:44:29.400 --> 01:44:34.440
choices will prevent you from being falsely
imprisoned by a secret organization, but sadly

01:44:34.440 --> 01:44:37.680
life - and wormholes - just don’t work like
that.

01:44:37.680 --> 01:44:43.680
Zagham Shebaz Karim said, “The best thing
to do, is to make allies. First of all, you

01:44:43.680 --> 01:44:48.540
need to get as many D-Class Personnel on your
side as possible. Ask them what duties they

01:44:48.540 --> 01:44:53.460
are on and use your information to help them.
Also, use them to find out what your duties

01:44:53.460 --> 01:44:58.488
are going to be as they will have connections.
You need to stay on the down-low, as if you

01:44:58.488 --> 01:45:02.820
are found out you will be experimented on
or worse. But, you don't only want to make

01:45:02.820 --> 01:45:07.560
allies with the D-Class, armed with the knowledge
from SCP Explained you are able to guess/

01:45:07.560 --> 01:45:13.140
approximate which facility you are at, what
SCP's might be there, and who the staff are.

01:45:13.140 --> 01:45:17.640
With that knowledge, you can approach some
of the kinder staff and build your relationship,

01:45:17.640 --> 01:45:22.200
so in case you get caught when escaping you
have a fall back or someone that can bail

01:45:22.200 --> 01:45:26.940
you out of deadly punishments. You also want
to be resourceful and map out the place to

01:45:26.940 --> 01:45:30.720
identify escape routes and the armory/ weapon
storage facility.

01:45:30.720 --> 01:45:35.580
Next, you want to form better relationships
with some of the SCP's. Before terminations

01:45:35.580 --> 01:45:40.500
roll around, you want to arrange a day that
the D-Class Personnel help some SCP's out

01:45:40.500 --> 01:45:45.060
of containment. Mostly non-deadly ones as
you do not want to make an enemy out of the

01:45:45.060 --> 01:45:49.980
SCP Foundation. But enough to distract the
guarding personnel. Also, with your close

01:45:49.980 --> 01:45:54.540
contact with facility members, you have a
better chance at stealing credentials/ passes

01:45:54.540 --> 01:45:59.280
to allow yourself through doors and escape.
But that isn't all, there actually might be

01:45:59.280 --> 01:46:04.680
some SCP's on site that will make escaping
easier and your goal should not be just your

01:46:04.680 --> 01:46:09.840
own escape, but escaping with as many D-Class
Personnel as possible. You want to equip them

01:46:09.840 --> 01:46:14.940
with passes, all of them should know only
1 specific escape route which they themselves

01:46:14.940 --> 01:46:19.740
or a few others are going, so that even if
they are caught, the others will not be found

01:46:19.740 --> 01:46:24.000
out. And you want a few of them to escape
before everyone else and cause a ruckus outside,

01:46:24.000 --> 01:46:28.800
making it look like the whole group has escaped,
sending guards behind them.

01:46:28.800 --> 01:46:33.240
They will need access to a vehicle, so that
needs to be secured from one of the staff

01:46:33.240 --> 01:46:37.800
which could be captured during the escape
and used for a way out or you could steal

01:46:37.800 --> 01:46:42.540
their keys earlier, depending on the situation.
You yourself and a few others head to the

01:46:42.540 --> 01:46:47.820
armory, to secure weapons, not only to escape
but to protect yourself from SCP's in the

01:46:47.820 --> 01:46:51.960
future. Then you need to secure vehicles for
the group, which you will need to put distance

01:46:51.960 --> 01:46:56.220
between the facility and your group and stay
away from other nearby facilities.

01:46:56.220 --> 01:47:00.360
You need to stop at a certain point and go
to the nearest hospital to check for body

01:47:00.360 --> 01:47:05.820
implants or anything that can be used to track
you, changing clothes and continuing on by

01:47:05.820 --> 01:47:10.860
foot. At this point, your group can choose
to separate, live collectively, and stay alert

01:47:10.860 --> 01:47:17.100
of the Foundation or join another organization”
This is probably the longest response we received,

01:47:17.100 --> 01:47:22.020
and we just had to include it to applaud the
dedication and thought at play here, as well

01:47:22.020 --> 01:47:26.460
as the selflessness! You’re not just planning
for your own rescue, no, that’s too small-time

01:47:26.460 --> 01:47:31.440
for Zagham. You’re planning to liberate
every D-Class you can, and potentially start

01:47:31.440 --> 01:47:35.340
your own brand-new society together. Make
sure you have a nice talk with them about

01:47:35.340 --> 01:47:40.020
the rules for your new community, such as
“absolutely no stabbing your neighbors,”

01:47:40.020 --> 01:47:43.260
and enjoy all the great, trauma-free barbecues
that follow.

01:47:43.260 --> 01:47:48.300
Plot Armor said, “I would either attempt
to cause a containment break or steal an anomaly

01:47:48.300 --> 01:47:52.740
like SCP-268. Still, first&nbsp;
I would draw thaumaturgic&nbsp;

01:47:52.740 --> 01:47:55.260
symbols all over my body and clothes like

01:47:55.260 --> 01:48:00.600
Kalphastian isle which will make me resistant
to physical damage that will help me survive

01:48:00.600 --> 01:48:04.380
containment breach.”
Bringing thaumatology into the equation is

01:48:04.380 --> 01:48:08.820
a brilliant move. You’d likely be the only
D-Class on site with any knowledge of that

01:48:08.820 --> 01:48:13.440
work, or the symbols. You’ll have to act
fast though, and work your magic before the

01:48:13.440 --> 01:48:17.040
Foundation figures out what you’re up to.
If you don’t, they’ll likely wipe your

01:48:17.040 --> 01:48:21.840
memory with amnestics or just terminate you
on the spot. The trickiest part of this plan

01:48:21.840 --> 01:48:27.000
is probably finding something to write with,
you can’t exactly ask to borrow a pen. But

01:48:27.000 --> 01:48:32.100
between those and the hat, you could probably
slip out unnoticed and unscathed!

01:48:32.100 --> 01:48:36.780
Jacob Oddie said, “I'd just do my best to
find Mr. Peanut so I could die relatively

01:48:36.780 --> 01:48:41.160
quickly/painlessly”
Why would that kill you? Are you allergic?

01:48:41.160 --> 01:48:46.500
…Just kidding, we know you’re talking
about SCP-173. As bleak of an answer as this

01:48:46.500 --> 01:48:51.120
is, we have to commend your honesty, and the
self-awareness it takes to look at the dangers

01:48:51.120 --> 01:48:56.220
of trying to escape the SCP Foundation and
simply saying: No, I’d rather not.

01:48:56.220 --> 01:49:00.780
Just another person On The Internet said,
“Well, taken with someone who has a very

01:49:00.780 --> 01:49:06.780
short attention span and who is a procrastinator
with ADHD, I could request a test with Miss

01:49:06.780 --> 01:49:11.460
J to see how it would affect someone like
me. Then I could escape and live the rest

01:49:11.460 --> 01:49:16.500
of my life in peace and solidarity.”
Miss J did help a previous D-Class leave the

01:49:16.500 --> 01:49:21.000
Foundation behind and start a new life, so
why not you too? We’re sure you’ll be

01:49:21.000 --> 01:49:25.860
a model pupil, and wish you luck in whatever
discipline you decide to pursue! Don’t forget

01:49:25.860 --> 01:49:31.620
your number 2 pencil.
Milo Knight said, “SCP-6113 is a pretty

01:49:31.620 --> 01:49:36.120
surefire way to get out. Assuming you meet
the criteria you can suddenly have a different

01:49:36.120 --> 01:49:40.980
name and appearance, and you won't really
be on any Foundation records. Use that to

01:49:40.980 --> 01:49:43.740
plead your case. Just pray&nbsp;
you don't get terminated.”&nbsp;

01:49:43.740 --> 01:49:47.460
This would be a pretty drastic choice, and

01:49:47.460 --> 01:49:51.000
you’d probably have to double down on the
whole “new life” thing since none of your

01:49:51.000 --> 01:49:55.800
loved ones will recognize you - or you can
just tell them you got extreme reconstructive

01:49:55.800 --> 01:50:00.240
surgery, I guess. But it’s better to be
alive in a new body than dead in your usual

01:50:00.240 --> 01:50:04.980
one! As long as you’re able to avoid detection
following your transformation, and make sure

01:50:04.980 --> 01:50:08.640
they don’t catch you on the security tapes,
this might just work.

01:50:08.640 --> 01:50:15.600
Zadanoire 0203 said, “I mean... you can’t.
Yes you’ll survive until the monthly “termination”

01:50:15.600 --> 01:50:20.580
but with how fortified these sites are, unless
you can get lucky with a containment breach

01:50:20.580 --> 01:50:25.200
or are able to gain access to the sewer system,
you’re not escaping. and that’s not even

01:50:25.200 --> 01:50:29.880
taking into account your spontaneous being
here and having great knowledge on the foundation

01:50:29.880 --> 01:50:34.500
that you’d only really know if you were
a being above their perception. So on top

01:50:34.500 --> 01:50:39.060
of that, there’s also the potential of you
being terminated for knowing too much, or

01:50:39.060 --> 01:50:43.200
being contained as a new scp.”
Hey now, we didn’t say “reply with the

01:50:43.200 --> 01:50:47.220
best way to bum us out!”
Really though, we see all of you hyper-realistic

01:50:47.220 --> 01:50:52.200
commenters and we get it, really! But in a
world filled with killer statues and friendly

01:50:52.200 --> 01:50:57.300
slime monsters, anything is possible. Sure,
most of the D-Class that wind up behind Foundation

01:50:57.300 --> 01:51:02.700
walls wind up dead, but you could be the one
in a million who makes it out. Dare to dream!

01:51:02.700 --> 01:51:08.460
Matt’s Magic said, “Easy, I simply say
"whoever shoots me or harms me is a rotten

01:51:08.460 --> 01:51:13.260
egg" and then walk out unharmed. Due to the
fact the guards are in fact people, not eggs”

01:51:13.260 --> 01:51:18.840
That’s it. You’ve cracked the code. Unless
the SCP Foundation starts hiring eggs as guards

01:51:18.840 --> 01:51:23.760
or creates some sort of unholy human-egg hybrid
to staff their halls - we’re looking at

01:51:23.760 --> 01:51:28.380
you, Humans, Refuted - their days of keeping
D-Class prisoners are over.

01:51:46.980 --> 01:51:52.020
The clank of heavy metal bars echoing through
the building usually kept Javi up at night.

01:51:52.020 --> 01:51:55.980
But he never thought that he would ever come
to miss that sound one day.

01:51:55.980 --> 01:52:01.560
As he tossed and turned in his uncomfortable
bunk, he couldn’t help that the noise reminded

01:52:01.560 --> 01:52:06.360
of how confined he was… and how trapped
he felt within.

01:52:06.360 --> 01:52:11.880
It had been a mistake, an honest and completely
unpredictable mistake.

01:52:11.880 --> 01:52:16.800
On the nights that sleep evaded him, he’d
always replay the events over and over again.

01:52:16.800 --> 01:52:22.320
His daughter, Kali, calling him out of the
blue, her distorted, uncontrollable sobs playing

01:52:22.320 --> 01:52:24.000
into his ear over the phone.

01:52:24.000 --> 01:52:29.580
Javi remembered jumping into his car and flooring
the accelerator, rain drumming against the

01:52:29.580 --> 01:52:32.640
windshield
as he drove all the way across town to her

01:52:32.640 --> 01:52:34.560
apartment in the early hours of the morning.

01:52:34.560 --> 01:52:39.540
And then there was the scene that awaited
him as he stepped through the door,

01:52:39.540 --> 01:52:45.300
the mess that had been of her kitchen, covering
the floor, smeared up the walls, even dripping

01:52:45.300 --> 01:52:46.740
off the ceiling.

01:52:46.740 --> 01:52:51.600
It all stayed there as Javi debated with his
daughter about what they should do next.

01:52:51.600 --> 01:52:56.820
She was crying, begging for her dad’s help,
suggesting they should clean the place from

01:52:56.820 --> 01:53:00.360
top to bottom
and hide any evidence scattered around the

01:53:00.360 --> 01:53:00.600
apartment.

01:53:00.600 --> 01:53:05.820
Laying in his cell all those months later,
he sometimes wondered why he said no to that

01:53:05.820 --> 01:53:06.180
idea.

01:53:06.180 --> 01:53:11.100
Instead, they came up with a story, a falsified
version of events.

01:53:11.100 --> 01:53:17.280
Javi made Kali swear to stick to it, no matter
what, and through heavy sobs she agreed.

01:53:17.280 --> 01:53:22.020
Then, after they’d been over every detail
and had it perfect, Javi got back in his car

01:53:22.020 --> 01:53:26.520
and drove to the nearest police precinct to
turn himself in.

01:53:26.520 --> 01:53:31.020
All while taking the fall, pleading guilty
in court and being sentenced to prison,

01:53:31.020 --> 01:53:35.040
he reminded himself over and over that he
was doing it for Kali.

01:53:35.040 --> 01:53:40.920
No matter what she had done, whatever accidents
had happened, she was still his kid, and he’d

01:53:40.920 --> 01:53:42.300
do anything to protect her.

01:53:42.300 --> 01:53:45.300
But that was part of what made it sting so
bad.

01:53:45.300 --> 01:53:48.660
Not being locked up, but not getting to see
her.

01:53:48.660 --> 01:53:53.160
Despite making request after request, Javi
was denied visitation rights,

01:53:53.160 --> 01:53:57.360
when seeing his daughter and knowing she was
alright were the only things that would’ve

01:53:57.360 --> 01:53:59.100
made this struggle easier.

01:53:59.100 --> 01:54:03.480
It was after his twentieth request that a
guard informed Javi he was being moved.

01:54:03.480 --> 01:54:07.620
At first, he assumed ‘moved’ meant to
another part of the prison, maybe with more

01:54:07.620 --> 01:54:10.200
lax security and the right to see Kali.

01:54:10.200 --> 01:54:12.540
But they didn’t mean moved.

01:54:12.540 --> 01:54:14.580
They meant transferred…

01:54:14.580 --> 01:54:19.920
into the care of an organization Javi came
to know as the SCP Foundation.

01:54:19.920 --> 01:54:21.420
It was different here.

01:54:21.420 --> 01:54:26.160
The kind of people he was locked up with as
a member of ‘D Class personnel’ were some

01:54:26.160 --> 01:54:28.200
of the most heinous criminals on the planet.

01:54:28.200 --> 01:54:33.120
But there was something else going on, something
weirder.

01:54:33.120 --> 01:54:36.780
Javi would overhear whispers and rumors from
the other D Classes;

01:54:36.780 --> 01:54:40.440
they’d talk of experiments conducted by
the Foundation…

01:54:40.440 --> 01:54:43.140
with them as the lab rats.

01:54:43.140 --> 01:54:48.480
“D-14164,” the researcher greeted him
as he was pulled into a side room, flanked

01:54:48.480 --> 01:54:50.640
on all sides by armed Foundation guards.

01:54:50.640 --> 01:54:53.220
“Yours is quite the interesting case.

01:54:53.220 --> 01:54:58.140
No priors, no criminal affiliations of any
kind, hardly any family to testify to your

01:54:58.140 --> 01:54:58.620
innocence.

01:54:58.620 --> 01:55:00.000
And yet, what you did…

01:55:00.000 --> 01:55:02.580
now, that takes some guts.

01:55:02.580 --> 01:55:04.020
If you’ll pardon the pun.”

01:55:04.020 --> 01:55:07.500
“So, it’s all true then,” Javi replied
solemnly.

01:55:07.500 --> 01:55:10.140
“You’re here to say you want to experiment
on me.”

01:55:10.140 --> 01:55:15.540
“I wouldn’t call it an ‘experiment’
per se,” the researcher smiled smugly.

01:55:15.540 --> 01:55:17.700
“Let’s use the word… ‘expedition’.

01:55:17.700 --> 01:55:19.800
Has a nicer ring to it, don’t you think?”

01:55:19.800 --> 01:55:22.080
“An expedition to where?” he asked.

01:55:22.080 --> 01:55:24.960
“I’m afraid I can’t tell you that.”

01:55:24.960 --> 01:55:27.540
“Then I won’t go,” Javi shrugged.

01:55:27.540 --> 01:55:30.060
“Listen closely, D-14164.

01:55:30.060 --> 01:55:36.780
The SCP Foundation has… we’ll say a considerable
influence across the globe.

01:55:36.780 --> 01:55:42.120
What we’re asking you to agree to is involvement
in possibly one of the most dangerous operations

01:55:42.120 --> 01:55:43.260
we have ever conducted.

01:55:43.260 --> 01:55:47.040
Now, as unappealing as that might sound, allow
me to sweeten the deal.

01:55:47.040 --> 01:55:51.900
Should you say yes, agree to take part, and
follow our instructions, then you might find

01:55:51.900 --> 01:55:57.720
that the Foundation’s influence may be rather
beneficial to you… should you survive.”

01:55:57.720 --> 01:56:02.700
The researcher paused to push a document across
the table, close enough for Javi to read.

01:56:02.700 --> 01:56:06.720
“A clean record, that one nasty mistake
wiped clean for good.

01:56:06.720 --> 01:56:10.020
On top of that, an immediate release and a
new identity.

01:56:10.020 --> 01:56:13.260
You’d have a second chance, Mr. Garrett.

01:56:13.260 --> 01:56:15.900
Both you and your daughter would.”

01:56:15.900 --> 01:56:17.460
“And what if I say no?”

01:56:17.460 --> 01:56:18.840
Javi asked bluntly.

01:56:18.840 --> 01:56:22.200
“Well, that’s your prerogative,” the
researcher replied.

01:56:22.200 --> 01:56:25.800
“And we won’t be able to detain you after
releasing evidence of your innocence.

01:56:25.800 --> 01:56:27.720
So, you’ll be free no matter what.

01:56:27.720 --> 01:56:32.280
Oh, although, your daughter will, of course,
be remanded into custody, given that she is

01:56:32.280 --> 01:56:32.640
actually guilty.

01:56:32.640 --> 01:56:38.160
Maybe, once we get her move to D Class, she
might be more willing to cooperate than her

01:56:38.160 --> 01:56:39.420
dear old dad?”

01:56:39.420 --> 01:56:42.540
Under the table, Javi’s hands were shaking
with rage.

01:56:42.540 --> 01:56:47.280
He clenched his fist so furiously that the
cuffs felt tighter around his wrists.

01:56:47.280 --> 01:56:51.000
The hate in his eyes was palpable as he glared
at the researcher.

01:56:51.000 --> 01:56:55.560
It was bad enough that they had backed him
into a corner, put him in a situation wherein

01:56:55.560 --> 01:56:58.260
saying yes or no would be equally catastrophic.

01:56:58.260 --> 01:57:03.480
But what felt worse was that they knew, they
knew what Kali had done, and they were using

01:57:03.480 --> 01:57:04.860
that as a threat.

01:57:04.860 --> 01:57:09.180
Taking a deep breath, Javi steadied himself
before finally speaking up again.

01:57:09.180 --> 01:57:11.400
“Just tell me what you want me to do.”

01:57:11.400 --> 01:57:16.020
Instantly, he felt like he’d been thrown
headfirst into a commotion he could barely

01:57:16.020 --> 01:57:16.740
keep up with.

01:57:16.740 --> 01:57:21.720
No sooner had he said that, Javi had been
led away by a team of Foundation guards while

01:57:21.720 --> 01:57:25.500
the researcher followed, rattling off vague
information about the ‘expedition’.

01:57:25.500 --> 01:57:30.540
Guided by the armed escort into another room,
Javi was flocked by more Foundation staff,

01:57:30.540 --> 01:57:35.040
strapping electronic equipment over the outside
of his orange prisoner garb.

01:57:35.040 --> 01:57:40.440
One piece of it was to monitor his heart rate,
another was a portable camera to record his

01:57:40.440 --> 01:57:43.440
surroundings,
although those were among the few details

01:57:43.440 --> 01:57:46.140
that he was actually able to catch during
all the activity.

01:57:46.140 --> 01:57:50.460
Javi couldn’t help noticing how nobody had
even mentioned just where he was meant to

01:57:50.460 --> 01:57:54.180
be going on this expedition,
just that the Foundation wanted to keep a

01:57:54.180 --> 01:57:56.040
close eye on him while he was there.

01:57:56.040 --> 01:58:00.000
“You still haven’t told me exactly where
it is I’m meant to be going,” he finally

01:58:00.000 --> 01:58:00.540
spoke up.

01:58:00.540 --> 01:58:01.800
“Or how I’m getting there.”

01:58:01.800 --> 01:58:04.920
“All in good time,” the head researcher
replied.

01:58:04.920 --> 01:58:09.060
“So far, the only detail you need to be
aware of is this: it’s dangerous.

01:58:09.060 --> 01:58:11.580
A particularly hostile environment.”

01:58:11.580 --> 01:58:13.740
“So, like a warzone?”

01:58:13.740 --> 01:58:14.580
Javi asked.

01:58:14.580 --> 01:58:19.320
The researcher smiled, but to themselves,
as if there was something funny about the

01:58:19.320 --> 01:58:21.960
question and the lack of understanding it
conveyed.

01:58:21.960 --> 01:58:26.640
Javi’s attention was quickly turned to another
D Class, wearing a similar D Class uniform

01:58:26.640 --> 01:58:27.180
to him.

01:58:27.180 --> 01:58:31.740
The staff began prepping the second convict
in the same room, although without nearly

01:58:31.740 --> 01:58:33.300
as much gear as they’d given Javi.

01:58:33.300 --> 01:58:36.180
“I thought I was the only one going on this
trip,” he stated.

01:58:36.180 --> 01:58:40.620
“Oh, don’t worry, that’s just the lure,”
the researcher explained, though it only raised

01:58:40.620 --> 01:58:41.040
more questions.

01:58:41.040 --> 01:58:43.020
“They won’t be accompanying you.”

01:58:43.020 --> 01:58:47.340
It was then Javi was led out of the prep room,
and walked through the Foundation facility.

01:58:47.340 --> 01:58:50.460
The place was a hive of activity, as well
as noise.

01:58:50.460 --> 01:58:55.080
There were alien shrieks and wails, monstrous
sounds emanating from deep in the building,

01:58:55.080 --> 01:59:00.420
muffled only slightly underneath thick layers
of concrete and heavy steel doors.

01:59:00.420 --> 01:59:04.260
On top of that, there were&nbsp;
screams, too, distinctly&nbsp;

01:59:04.260 --> 01:59:07.140
human sounds of pain and fear.

01:59:07.140 --> 01:59:11.940
Javi tried to ignore it as he was instructed
to keep walking, feeling almost nostalgic

01:59:11.940 --> 01:59:14.700
for the clank of metal bars back at his old
cell.

01:59:14.700 --> 01:59:19.620
He had been anticipating being taken out of
the facility, towards maybe a helipad or runway

01:59:19.620 --> 01:59:23.820
where an aircraft would be waiting to fly
him out to his undisclosed destination.

01:59:23.820 --> 01:59:28.500
In fact, even a dock with a moored boat would
have been more within the realm of what he

01:59:28.500 --> 01:59:29.040
was expected.

01:59:29.040 --> 01:59:33.660
But instead, he was taken deeper and deeper
into the Foundation’s sprawling complex,

01:59:33.660 --> 01:59:38.820
passing testing areas and containment levels,
through winding corridors until...

01:59:38.820 --> 01:59:43.920
Javi was told to stop in front of a heavy
door that held a containment area beyond.

01:59:43.920 --> 01:59:48.480
He couldn’t help noticing that the door
itself looked brand new like it had only just

01:59:48.480 --> 01:59:49.380
been installed.

01:59:49.380 --> 01:59:54.240
Meanwhile, the frame surrounding it had traces
of discoloring and damage, almost as if something

01:59:54.240 --> 01:59:58.560
corrosive had melted away the previous door,
hence the replacement.

01:59:58.560 --> 01:59:59.880
“This is it?”

01:59:59.880 --> 02:00:05.100
Javi asked, confused but still trying to nudge
more information out of the Foundation staff.

02:00:05.100 --> 02:00:08.640
“You want me to do an ‘expedition’ within
your own building?”

02:00:08.640 --> 02:00:15.360
“Yes and no,” the head researcher replied,
still giving next to no useful details.

02:00:15.360 --> 02:00:16.560
“Now, this is important.

02:00:16.560 --> 02:00:20.340
Once you get to your destination, we need
you to figure out how to get back to us.

02:00:20.340 --> 02:00:24.660
You do that, and you’ll have your freedom,
your daughter, your second chance.”

02:00:24.660 --> 02:00:27.900
“Surely to get back I can just open up this
door again?”

02:00:27.900 --> 02:00:29.100
the prisoner said.

02:00:29.100 --> 02:00:31.740
That garnered a slight&nbsp;
chuckle from the researcher,&nbsp;

02:00:31.740 --> 02:00:34.440
again seeming to find Javi’s lack of understanding

02:00:34.440 --> 02:00:35.220
amusing.

02:00:35.220 --> 02:00:38.400
“Oh, D-14164.

02:00:38.400 --> 02:00:40.500
If only it was that simple.”

02:00:40.500 --> 02:00:45.780
The door opened with a heaving, metallic creak,
and Javi was unceremoniously pushed inside

02:00:45.780 --> 02:00:46.860
by the guards.

02:00:46.860 --> 02:00:50.940
Staggering inside, catching himself before
he lost his balance and fell over,

02:00:50.940 --> 02:00:56.040
he almost turned back to start slamming his
fists against the now-sealed entrance.

02:00:56.040 --> 02:01:00.960
He would have, were it not for the sight of
something oozing out of the wall directly

02:01:00.960 --> 02:01:01.800
in front of him.

02:01:01.800 --> 02:01:07.140
Javi stepped closer to inspect in, but withdrew
his hand as he noticed thick, black droplets

02:01:07.140 --> 02:01:08.220
leaking onto the floor.

02:01:08.220 --> 02:01:14.040
The substance hissed as it made contact with
the ground, like it was burning through, corroding

02:01:14.040 --> 02:01:15.840
whatever it came into contact with.

02:01:15.840 --> 02:01:19.620
Keeping himself from touching it, Javi leaned
in to get a closer look…

02:01:19.620 --> 02:01:24.900
and found the grotesque face of SCP-106 looking
back at him.

02:01:24.900 --> 02:01:29.640
A spindly, decrepit hand reached out of the
gooey puddle and grabbed the orange material

02:01:29.640 --> 02:01:32.820
of Javi’s jumpsuit,
pulling at the garment with enough strength

02:01:32.820 --> 02:01:34.500
to tug his entire body forward.

02:01:34.500 --> 02:01:39.540
In a blind panic, Javi tried to swipe the
Old Man’s rotting arm away, feeling an intense

02:01:39.540 --> 02:01:43.080
burning,
almost like a chemical scalding his skin as

02:01:43.080 --> 02:01:48.360
the same black ooze burned through his clothing
and started to singe his chest.

02:01:48.360 --> 02:01:53.400
Another hand shot forward out of the puddle
on the wall, a clawed thumb puncturing Javi’s

02:01:53.400 --> 02:01:53.580
side.

02:01:53.580 --> 02:01:57.660
He screamed in pain as he felt the blistering
digit pierce into him,

02:01:57.660 --> 02:02:03.240
while the rest of the fingers pulled him closer
still to the wall, using the hole in his side

02:02:03.240 --> 02:02:04.500
like a handle.

02:02:04.500 --> 02:02:09.900
The stench of his own burning flesh mixed
with the air of rotting decay that SCP-106

02:02:09.900 --> 02:02:11.340
brought with it everywhere.

02:02:11.340 --> 02:02:15.840
The decaying face of the Old Man peered through
the black slime on the wall,

02:02:15.840 --> 02:02:22.140
its mouth locked in a sadistic, sinister grin
as Javi found himself pulled inside.

02:02:22.140 --> 02:02:25.140
It almost felt like he had fallen asleep in
the bathtub,

02:02:25.140 --> 02:02:29.820
and woken up gasping and spluttering, just
having narrowly avoided drowning.

02:02:29.820 --> 02:02:35.340
Except now he was waking up in an entirely
different space, the transition there having

02:02:35.340 --> 02:02:37.860
done little to dull the pain of his injuries.

02:02:37.860 --> 02:02:42.720
Sitting up as he arrived in his new dark and
desolate surroundings, Javi gripped his side

02:02:42.720 --> 02:02:44.640
and winced at the searing pain.

02:02:44.640 --> 02:02:49.680
Then, the sudden smell of burning plastic
crawled up his nostrils and down the back

02:02:49.680 --> 02:02:50.460
of his throat.

02:02:50.460 --> 02:02:53.640
Most of the equipment the Foundation had strapped
onto him was melting;

02:02:53.640 --> 02:02:58.740
the heart rate monitor and camera corroding,
covered in more of that black sludge.

02:02:58.740 --> 02:03:02.880
Standing up, gritting his teeth through the
pain of his wounds, Javi quickly removed the

02:03:02.880 --> 02:03:04.560
devices as carefully as he could.

02:03:04.560 --> 02:03:09.300
He was far beyond caring about the Foundation’s
‘expedition’, right now, he just wanted

02:03:09.300 --> 02:03:13.080
to get back out of… wherever the hell this
place was.

02:03:13.080 --> 02:03:17.460
Checking over his shoulder, the slick, corrosive
puddle he’d been pulled through was already

02:03:17.460 --> 02:03:17.820
gone.

02:03:17.820 --> 02:03:23.190
At any rate, Javi didn’t particularly like
the idea of going back that way, so was relieved

02:03:23.190 --> 02:03:27.120
– although he’d soon find out that relief
was perhaps misplaced.

02:03:27.120 --> 02:03:31.800
He had barely a few moments to examine his
surroundings, not that there was much he could

02:03:31.800 --> 02:03:36.480
see besides endless dark corridors…
and the withered figure of the Old Man.

02:03:36.480 --> 02:03:41.880
It had reappeared, looking like it was far
off in the distance before, in a split second,

02:03:41.880 --> 02:03:45.960
the gap between SCP-106 and Javi had closed.

02:03:45.960 --> 02:03:51.600
The creature tilted its bald, skeletal head
as it looked at him, and he got a full, unobscured

02:03:51.600 --> 02:03:56.340
glimpse at its sunken eyes and face locked
in a perpetual lipless grin.

02:03:56.340 --> 02:04:01.380
The rotting, wrinkly flesh of the Old Man’s
body was oozing more and more of that dark,

02:04:01.380 --> 02:04:04.860
disgusting mucus,
droplets of it landing on Javi’s clothing

02:04:04.860 --> 02:04:06.360
and burning holes right through.

02:04:06.360 --> 02:04:11.100
One hand still applying pressure to his side,
Javi turned and tried to run as far and fast

02:04:11.100 --> 02:04:12.360
as his body was able.

02:04:12.360 --> 02:04:16.920
If getting away was his sole objective, he
wanted out as quick as possible,

02:04:16.920 --> 02:04:21.480
anything to get away from the nightmarish
creature he had now found himself trapped

02:04:21.480 --> 02:04:21.660
with.

02:04:21.660 --> 02:04:23.940
Running didn’t seem to work though.

02:04:23.940 --> 02:04:26.220
He was sure he had made it at least a few
feet,

02:04:26.220 --> 02:04:30.600
the Old Man was still standing behind him
but looked to be at more of a distance.

02:04:30.600 --> 02:04:36.060
That was, until Javi noticed his legs; they
were running backwards.

02:04:36.060 --> 02:04:42.720
Each step he had taken away from SCP-106 was
being reversed, rewound like on an old VHS

02:04:42.720 --> 02:04:45.840
tape,
bringing him back closer and closer to the

02:04:45.840 --> 02:04:46.800
creature’s clutches.

02:04:46.800 --> 02:04:51.480
Its claws pushed painfully through Javi’s
back as it reversed time right back to the

02:04:51.480 --> 02:04:53.160
point where he had turned to run away.

02:04:53.160 --> 02:04:58.320
As the Old Man reached through him, more of
that burning, acidic pain starting eating

02:04:58.320 --> 02:05:03.300
away at its captive D Class prey,
with Javi letting out a pained scream.

02:05:03.300 --> 02:05:07.920
It wasn’t until several minutes in that
he realized something that hurt almost just

02:05:07.920 --> 02:05:12.420
as bad:
SCP-106 could have killed him in an instant.

02:05:12.420 --> 02:05:13.740
That much was clear.

02:05:13.740 --> 02:05:15.180
But it hadn’t.

02:05:15.180 --> 02:05:17.160
It had chosen not to.

02:05:17.160 --> 02:05:22.680
Instead, it was torturing him, prolonging
the pain Javi was experiencing…

02:05:22.680 --> 02:05:25.260
because it wanted him to suffer.

02:05:25.260 --> 02:05:31.440
With all its strength, SCP-106 hurled him
several feet, until Javi’s burning, corroding

02:05:31.440 --> 02:05:35.940
body collided with a hard surface, a wall,
with a loud crack.

02:05:35.940 --> 02:05:40.140
The Old Man then manipulated the time within
his pocket dimension, causing Javi to fly

02:05:40.140 --> 02:05:43.620
backward through the air,
back into the creature’s corrosive grip.

02:05:43.620 --> 02:05:48.480
It allowed time to keep winding back, undoing
all the damage it had done so far.

02:05:48.480 --> 02:05:53.340
As much as Javi was relieved to be rid of
the searing pain he was in, a sick worry deep

02:05:53.340 --> 02:05:57.180
in the pit of his stomach told him that this
wasn’t an act of mercy;

02:05:57.180 --> 02:06:01.320
the Old Man just wanted to make him suffer
all over again.

02:06:01.320 --> 02:06:08.220
He lost track of how long it went on for,
SCP-106 taking sick glee in hurting his captive,

02:06:08.220 --> 02:06:12.420
only to reverse the time within the pocket
dimension and do it all again.

02:06:12.420 --> 02:06:18.120
Each time felt just like the first, made worse
by the fact that Javi never had a chance to

02:06:18.120 --> 02:06:20.820
get used to the pain, to become numb to it.

02:06:20.820 --> 02:06:26.580
Instead, every moment the torture started
was just like the first time, in fact, it

02:06:26.580 --> 02:06:27.420
technically was.

02:06:27.420 --> 02:06:32.880
Screaming in agony, Javi had no clue if he
had been there for hours, days, months, or

02:06:32.880 --> 02:06:33.540
even years.

02:06:33.540 --> 02:06:35.880
Had any time passed at all?

02:06:35.880 --> 02:06:38.460
How many times had the Old Man done this to
him,

02:06:38.460 --> 02:06:43.740
only to reverse time and do it all over again,
letting the pain begin anew?

02:06:43.740 --> 02:06:46.980
Every few tries, the Old Man would release
Javi for a moment.

02:06:46.980 --> 02:06:51.360
But once again, it was never done out of any
sense of mercy or kindness.

02:06:51.360 --> 02:06:53.340
It was so it could hunt him.

02:06:53.340 --> 02:06:59.400
Still, Javi ran every time, as far and fast
as he could, searching every corner of the

02:06:59.400 --> 02:07:01.740
dark, desolate place for some kind of way
out.

02:07:01.740 --> 02:07:07.080
A portal home, another of those corrosive
black puddles, a big door with ‘Exit’

02:07:07.080 --> 02:07:08.460
written on it in big red letters.

02:07:08.460 --> 02:07:16.980
But instead, all he found was SCP-106… or
rather, the Old Man found him.

02:07:16.980 --> 02:07:18.540
It was hunting him again.

02:07:18.540 --> 02:07:21.300
Javi had no idea how many times this made.

02:07:21.300 --> 02:07:24.600
Panting, he slowed to a halt, trying to catch
his breath.

02:07:24.600 --> 02:07:30.240
Reaching out in the dark, he put a hand against
a nearby hard surface, a wall… then felt

02:07:30.240 --> 02:07:31.440
a crack under his hand.

02:07:31.440 --> 02:07:36.600
Examining closer, although he could barely
see it, Javi could feel, with absolute certainty,

02:07:36.600 --> 02:07:39.480
that the wall had been damaged, just a little.

02:07:39.480 --> 02:07:45.180
It was the same one, the exact same wall he
had been thrown against, who knew how long

02:07:45.180 --> 02:07:45.480
ago.

02:07:45.480 --> 02:07:51.000
But the point was, it was still cracked from
where his body had hit it, even after the

02:07:51.000 --> 02:07:56.880
Old Man had reversed time to hurt Javi over
and over again, the damage had stayed.

02:07:56.880 --> 02:08:01.500
He had no way of knowing if it would work,
or how long it would take, but it might have

02:08:01.500 --> 02:08:03.180
been a way out.

02:08:03.180 --> 02:08:07.260
Desperately, Javi slammed his fist against
the wall, feeling pain shooting through his

02:08:07.260 --> 02:08:11.280
fingers and up into his arm as it collided
with the solid surface.

02:08:11.280 --> 02:08:12.660
Nothing changed.

02:08:12.660 --> 02:08:14.820
Winding back the other arm, he hit it again.

02:08:14.820 --> 02:08:15.900
Still nothing.

02:08:15.900 --> 02:08:20.580
Javi could hear the Old Man shuffling closer
behind him, could sense his hands reaching

02:08:20.580 --> 02:08:22.140
out closer to torture him again.

02:08:22.140 --> 02:08:27.600
He let it happen, striking one last blow against
the wall before it finally reached him, and

02:08:27.600 --> 02:08:33.660
the pain began once more,
only to be reversed so SCP-106 could do it

02:08:33.660 --> 02:08:33.840
all again.

02:08:33.840 --> 02:08:36.180
But now, he had a goal.

02:08:36.180 --> 02:08:40.740
Each time the creature rewound time and undid
all the damage to its captive’s body,

02:08:40.740 --> 02:08:46.260
Javi went looking for that same wall, feeling
across every surface until he reached it again.

02:08:46.260 --> 02:08:51.780
The damage was still there, only a tiny crack
for now, but time – despite being under

02:08:51.780 --> 02:08:55.800
the Old Man’s control in this pocket dimension
– was on Javi’s side.

02:08:55.800 --> 02:09:00.840
Whenever he made it back to the wall, he started
to punch at it again, pounding one fist against

02:09:00.840 --> 02:09:01.800
it after the other.

02:09:01.800 --> 02:09:06.900
He didn’t relent, despite the pain it caused
him, he told himself it would be worth it

02:09:06.900 --> 02:09:08.040
if it meant he made it out.

02:09:08.040 --> 02:09:12.660
Even the times when the bones in his hands
broke, and he was flimsily striking at the

02:09:12.660 --> 02:09:15.720
cracked wall with shattered fingers, he didn’t
stop.

02:09:15.720 --> 02:09:17.280
It would be worth it.

02:09:17.280 --> 02:09:20.160
It had to be, no matter how long it took.

02:09:20.160 --> 02:09:23.580
He would gradually chip away at the wall,
more and more.

02:09:23.580 --> 02:09:29.100
Every time SCP-106 would find Javi, and when
it did, it would hurt him.

02:09:29.100 --> 02:09:33.480
The pain he’d put himself through trying
to break down the wall helped numb some of

02:09:33.480 --> 02:09:35.640
the torture before the Old Man and reset&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:35.640 --> 02:09:36.780
everything
again.

02:09:36.780 --> 02:09:42.300
Then, the hunt began again, and Javi went
back to making his gradual dent in the wall.

02:09:42.300 --> 02:09:47.280
The whole time, he remembered he was doing
it for Kali, for his freedom, and a fresh

02:09:47.280 --> 02:09:47.580
start.

02:09:47.580 --> 02:09:50.640
Until, eventually, it stopped.

02:09:50.640 --> 02:09:54.600
Javi had no idea how many times he’d struck
the wall, but it had been enough to start

02:09:54.600 --> 02:09:57.720
tunneling through it slightly, both hands
bloody and broken again.

02:09:57.720 --> 02:10:00.180
But this time, the Old Man didn’t appear.

02:10:00.180 --> 02:10:01.440
It had stopped.

02:10:01.440 --> 02:10:05.520
Screaming in rage, Javi started throwing himself
at the indented wall, trying desperately to

02:10:05.520 --> 02:10:10.200
chip away more,
completely unaware that SCP-106 had left him

02:10:10.200 --> 02:10:12.120
alone in its pocket dimension.

02:10:12.120 --> 02:10:16.680
Back in their facility, the Foundation had
lost contact with Javi’s recording devices

02:10:16.680 --> 02:10:20.340
over three hours ago,
at which point they had initiated their Recall

02:10:20.340 --> 02:10:23.340
Protocol,
injuring the D Class they had chosen as a

02:10:23.340 --> 02:10:23.640
lure.

02:10:23.640 --> 02:10:28.200
Sure enough, the Old Man had reappeared from
his pocket dimension, following the pained

02:10:28.200 --> 02:10:30.780
screams to come and finish the lure off.

02:10:30.780 --> 02:10:35.160
There was no sign of Javi, and with a sigh,
the head researcher simply noted down:

02:10:35.160 --> 02:10:38.640
‘SCP-106 Pocket Dimension Escape Test.

02:10:38.640 --> 02:10:41.040
Result: test inconclusive.’

02:10:41.040 --> 02:10:48.600
I made it? I actually made it? I’m still alive,&nbsp;
by some miracle – or maybe by sheer luck – I made&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:48.600 --> 02:10:53.400
it to the end of my sentence. Over three months&nbsp;
of the most horrific work I’ve ever had to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:53.400 --> 02:10:58.740
with no other alternative. But it’s paid off,&nbsp;
because any minute now, they’ll be coming into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:58.740 --> 02:11:03.300
dorm to let me go. I can walk out of here with my&nbsp;
head up. I can’t wait to get out of these orange&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:03.300 --> 02:11:09.360
overalls that I’ve had to live in since I arrived.
It seems like so much longer, in all honesty. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:09.360 --> 02:11:12.780
only way I’ve been able to keep track of how&nbsp;
many days I’ve been stuck here is by putting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:12.780 --> 02:11:18.660
tally marks on the wall next to my dormitory&nbsp;
bed. A hundred, I’ve been a member of D-Class&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:18.660 --> 02:11:23.700
personnel for the SCP Foundation for the last&nbsp;
one hundred days. And here’s how I survived…&nbsp;

02:11:23.700 --> 02:11:28.440
They didn’t waste any time early on; I’ll give&nbsp;
the Foundation that. I’d barely been out of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:28.440 --> 02:11:33.120
sentencing hearing for a few days. Put on trial&nbsp;
and convicted for a crime I didn’t even commit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:33.120 --> 02:11:37.140
There had been a string of murders, and someone&nbsp;
who had either been involved or was responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:37.140 --> 02:11:42.240
laid the blame on me. But whoever they were, they&nbsp;
had powerful friends. The cops were in on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:42.240 --> 02:11:46.860
planting evidence to frame me and make it look&nbsp;
like I was guilty when I was really innocent.&nbsp;

02:11:46.860 --> 02:11:52.380
That’s what landed me in prison, serving a&nbsp;
sentence of twenty-five years to life behind bars.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:52.380 --> 02:11:57.720
And it was during my first week of incarceration&nbsp;
that he showed up. The recruitment specialist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:57.720 --> 02:12:02.580
a clean-cut, blunt, Agent Smith type came&nbsp;
to visit me. I hadn’t even been in prison&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:02.580 --> 02:12:06.240
long enough to have visitor privileges, but&nbsp;
the shady agent seemed to know which strings&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:06.240 --> 02:12:11.820
to pull in order to ask me an important question:
“What would you do to get out of here?” he’d said.&nbsp;

02:12:11.820 --> 02:12:16.200
To which my answer was “Anything.”
That was my first mistake.&nbsp;

02:12:16.200 --> 02:12:20.700
Immediately, the next day I received a letter&nbsp;
detailing more about what exactly Agent Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:20.700 --> 02:12:25.740
was offering. It was folded and hidden in the&nbsp;
spine of a book that a guard handed me during&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:25.740 --> 02:12:30.720
reading hour. Unfolding the message, it all&nbsp;
felt like an old-school covert spy tactic.&nbsp;

02:12:30.720 --> 02:12:36.240
“To Mr Emil Carker,” the letter read. “We&nbsp;
understand that you recently received a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:36.240 --> 02:12:41.820
criminal conviction. There are two options&nbsp;
currently before you, as detailed below. One:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:41.820 --> 02:12:46.680
you can serve out the remainder of your sentence&nbsp;
in prison. Or two: you can be released into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:46.680 --> 02:12:50.940
care of our organization. While with us,&nbsp;
you will be helping to further scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:50.940 --> 02:12:56.400
advancements through hard work. We have devised&nbsp;
a system that allows convicts such as yourself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:56.400 --> 02:13:02.220
perform a vital role within our organization, in&nbsp;
return for a reduction in their prison sentences.&nbsp;

02:13:02.220 --> 02:13:06.780
“We have no interest in or intention of&nbsp;
determining any guilt for the crimes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:06.780 --> 02:13:12.780
which you are currently convicted. We merely seek&nbsp;
to present this opportunity to you. We ask that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:12.780 --> 02:13:17.160
you destroy this letter once you have finished&nbsp;
reading. Should you be interested in our offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:17.160 --> 02:13:23.940
then please recite the phrase ‘it’s a yes’ to&nbsp;
a prison guard with the badge number: 47890.”&nbsp;

02:13:23.940 --> 02:13:28.440
Naturally, as cryptic as an offer like that&nbsp;
was, I didn’t need much time to mull it over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:28.440 --> 02:13:32.160
I was trapped in prison for something I&nbsp;
didn’t do, with no way to appeal for my&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:32.160 --> 02:13:37.440
freedom or prove my innocence. So, taking up a&nbsp;
mysterious job opportunity from a shadowy group&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:37.440 --> 02:13:42.300
seemed like a much better alternative at the&nbsp;
time. After all, I could either work off my&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:42.300 --> 02:13:47.040
sentence through employment, or rot away in a&nbsp;
cell for the next two and half decades at minimum.&nbsp;

02:13:47.040 --> 02:13:52.080
All I had to do next was find the right guard.&nbsp;
I tried not to make it obvious that I was eyeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:52.080 --> 02:13:56.280
every prison guard’s badge number as they&nbsp;
stood around, keeping a close eye on me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:56.280 --> 02:14:02.280
and the other inmates. But it was while out&nbsp;
in the yard that I saw him: number 47890.&nbsp;

02:14:02.280 --> 02:14:06.900
I had gone to bed with a knot in my stomach.&nbsp;
Hours earlier, I had done exactly as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:06.900 --> 02:14:10.440
letter said and approached the guard, making&nbsp;
sure I was close enough for him to hear me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:10.440 --> 02:14:14.400
but trying to make it clear that I wasn’t&nbsp;
looking tor start any trouble either.&nbsp;

02:14:14.400 --> 02:14:20.100
“It’s a yes,” I told him.
In response, 47890 had furrowed his brow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:20.100 --> 02:14:24.960
scrunched his mustache, seemingly in disgust.
“Back up, inmate,” he commanded sharply.&nbsp;

02:14:24.960 --> 02:14:29.220
I backed away with my hands raised, confused as&nbsp;
to what had just happened. I spent the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:29.220 --> 02:14:33.780
rest of the day thinking about it; maybe the&nbsp;
note had been a prank, some kind of initiation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:33.780 --> 02:14:38.040
seeing as I was the newest prisoner. But that&nbsp;
didn’t explain the agent who had shown up&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:38.040 --> 02:14:42.900
before. What was going on? The questions that were&nbsp;
spinning around in my head eventually wore me out.&nbsp;

02:14:42.900 --> 02:14:46.440
And while I slept, that’s when&nbsp;
someone put a bag over my head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:46.440 --> 02:14:51.720
drugged me, and snuck me out of prison.
When I woke up, I was somewhere new, a room filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:51.720 --> 02:14:55.980
with other convicts. Although not many of them&nbsp;
seemed to have come from the same prison I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:55.980 --> 02:15:00.600
been sentenced to; plenty were sporting different&nbsp;
jumpsuits, but their gruff demeanors told me all&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:00.600 --> 02:15:05.040
I needed to about them. I was surrounded by&nbsp;
a more violent breed of criminal, some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:05.040 --> 02:15:09.900
worst of the absolute worst. The atmosphere in&nbsp;
this pen full of murderers and monsters was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:09.900 --> 02:15:14.280
tense that it felt like the slightest accidental&nbsp;
bump could explode into a full-blown fight.&nbsp;

02:15:14.280 --> 02:15:19.320
Suddenly, a hatch in the ceiling opened, and a&nbsp;
pile of clothes came thumping down from above.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:19.320 --> 02:15:24.540
It was a mass of orange, enough matching overalls&nbsp;
for every inmate in the room. Of course, I held&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:24.540 --> 02:15:28.740
back from the initial clamor some of the others&nbsp;
made, grabbing their new prison garb, snatching&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:28.740 --> 02:15:33.780
orange overalls from each other, and arguing. When&nbsp;
I eventually got mine, I noticed an unfamiliar&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:33.780 --> 02:15:38.460
logo emblazoned on the front of the uniform.
After being made to wait a whole day in the pen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:38.460 --> 02:15:42.600
the other prisoners and I were filed out of&nbsp;
the room by heavily armed security guards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:42.600 --> 02:15:46.680
We were all directed towards a hall, nobody&nbsp;
was daring enough to challenge the officers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:46.680 --> 02:15:50.940
even the more violent among the prisoners.&nbsp;
I spotted that the guard’s uniforms bore the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:50.940 --> 02:15:55.380
same insignia as the overalls we’d been made to&nbsp;
wear. It must’ve been the logo of the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:55.380 --> 02:15:59.940
organization that had offered me employment.
We were given an orientation talk led by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:59.940 --> 02:16:04.620
someone who introduced themselves as a Junior&nbsp;
Assistant Researcher. They explained that our&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:04.620 --> 02:16:09.060
current location was highly classified, as was&nbsp;
the true identity of the group that had arranged&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:09.060 --> 02:16:15.120
for us to be released. All we were told was that&nbsp;
this was an unspecified form of research facility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.120 --> 02:16:20.280
and that we had to cooperate with the facility’s&nbsp;
staff if we wanted to secure our release. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:20.280 --> 02:16:23.760
seemed straightforward enough, although&nbsp;
the Junior Assistant Researcher seemed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:23.760 --> 02:16:27.900
make a lot of jokes about us dying during&nbsp;
these tests. I think they hoped it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:27.900 --> 02:16:32.460
alleviate some of the tension… it didn’t.
After sitting through the orientation talk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:32.460 --> 02:16:37.080
I still had more questions than answers. The&nbsp;
one thing I had learned was that being tattooed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:37.859 --> 02:16:42.780
hurt. Exiting the hall, the other prisoners and&nbsp;
I had been directed to get a designation number&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:42.780 --> 02:16:47.219
tattooed on our wrist and across our chest.&nbsp;
When someone had asked why the chest, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:47.220 --> 02:16:52.080
Researcher conducting orientation had answered:
“Well, in the event of an explosion, it's most&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:52.080 --> 02:16:57.180
likely that it'll be the largest intact chunk of&nbsp;
meat left.” This was another of their ‘jokes’.&nbsp;

02:16:57.180 --> 02:17:02.460
Still feeling sore as my tattoo healed, I read&nbsp;
the number now permanently inked onto my body:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:04.560 --> 02:17:09.480
D-2152. ‘D-Class Personnel’ that’s what they&nbsp;
called us now. We were directed into a dormitory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:09.479 --> 02:17:13.919
a lot less cramped than my old cell. But&nbsp;
still, being surrounded by violent criminals&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:13.920 --> 02:17:18.240
when I knew I didn’t belong there, it&nbsp;
felt no less isolating. One of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:18.240 --> 02:17:23.219
who took the bunk next to mine, introduced himself&nbsp;
to me as ‘Shiv’. Worried he might try to kill me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:23.220 --> 02:17:28.560
I did my best to be friendly towards him.
Shiv and I didn’t exactly bond, but more&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:28.560 --> 02:17:33.540
sprung up conversations because there was little&nbsp;
else to do. I was still worried about some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:33.540 --> 02:17:38.580
flippant comments made about us all dying,&nbsp;
until he pointed out something far stranger.&nbsp;

02:17:38.580 --> 02:17:42.120
“Don’t you wonder why they’re&nbsp;
making us wait?” he asked.&nbsp;

02:17:42.120 --> 02:17:46.859
And the moment he mentioned it, I did start&nbsp;
to wonder about that. It seemed odd that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:46.859 --> 02:17:50.040
organization had wasted little time&nbsp;
in getting us all here. It had only&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:50.040 --> 02:17:54.000
been a week ago that I entered my prison&nbsp;
cell for the first time, now I was here.&nbsp;

02:17:54.000 --> 02:17:58.800
“I think it’s their way of telling us,” Shiv&nbsp;
went on. “That we aren’t here to be helpful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:58.800 --> 02:18:02.100
to be a workforce for them. They’ve&nbsp;
brought us all here to die.”&nbsp;

02:18:02.100 --> 02:18:07.440
That comment made me look around, and actually&nbsp;
acknowledge the people I was trapped with. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:07.439 --> 02:18:12.179
were some of the worst criminals imaginable,&nbsp;
irredeemable killers. Would this organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:12.180 --> 02:18:15.960
really let those people back out into the&nbsp;
world after helping out with a few tests?&nbsp;

02:18:15.960 --> 02:18:20.040
I’d soon find out, because Day 8&nbsp;
was the last day before I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:20.040 --> 02:18:25.380
myself working directly for the SCP Foundation.
I picked up the name from a few places around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:25.380 --> 02:18:30.900
facility; nametags, lab coats, and security&nbsp;
uniforms, always with the same logo. Me and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:30.899 --> 02:18:35.519
the other D-Class personnel were woken up early in&nbsp;
the morning by a bell and issued with assignments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:35.520 --> 02:18:40.321
From the very first day the work started, I&nbsp;
noticed that not all the others came back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:40.320 --> 02:18:45.299
dorm at the end of the day. Shiv was one of those.
My tasks mostly consisted of cleaning up with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:45.300 --> 02:18:50.460
mop, hosing down empty testing chambers that had&nbsp;
a worrying amount of blood sprayed over the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:50.460 --> 02:18:55.500
and floors. The regiments were strict, and I made&nbsp;
sure to do exactly what was asked of me to avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:55.500 --> 02:19:01.200
causing any trouble. It felt a lot like being back&nbsp;
in prison, but with the only added caveat of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:01.200 --> 02:19:06.000
able to move around the facility, even if it&nbsp;
was to go and wipe up the Foundation’s mess.&nbsp;

02:19:06.000 --> 02:19:11.399
Although the other classes of personnel didn’t&nbsp;
seem to take kindly to my help, making snide&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:11.399 --> 02:19:15.540
comments as they passed me in the hallways. We had&nbsp;
been told not to address the other staff unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:15.540 --> 02:19:20.340
spoken to, which was frustrating. I certainly&nbsp;
had a few things I would’ve liked to say to them.&nbsp;

02:19:20.340 --> 02:19:25.020
After two and a half weeks without so much as a&nbsp;
hint of any trouble, I was given my first testing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:25.020 --> 02:19:29.041
assignment that wasn’t just cleaning up. You&nbsp;
would think that would be exciting, but having&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:29.040 --> 02:19:34.380
seen some of the carnage left behind after other&nbsp;
tests, I was anything but eager to take part.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:34.380 --> 02:19:39.359
Luckily for me, and pretty surprisingly,&nbsp;
my first test was pretty straightforward.&nbsp;

02:19:39.359 --> 02:19:43.380
I was shown to a room containing a&nbsp;
white bowl decorated with light blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:43.380 --> 02:19:49.020
flowers that the researchers referred to&nbsp;
as SCP-348. I had received a nasty splinter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:49.020 --> 02:19:53.281
from my mop’s handle the day before, and was&nbsp;
instructed to sit down and eat from the bowl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:53.280 --> 02:19:58.859
which was filled with soup. Naturally, I eagerly&nbsp;
ate it all, despite how bitter it tasted. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:58.859 --> 02:20:03.420
better than the food from the D-Class canteen at&nbsp;
least. But when I finished, I noticed a message&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:03.420 --> 02:20:09.540
had appeared at the bottom of SCP-348. It read:
“I don’t believe you were framed. Goodbye, son.”&nbsp;

02:20:09.540 --> 02:20:14.100
After that, it was as if the floodgates had&nbsp;
been opened, and every day I was asked to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:14.100 --> 02:20:19.380
another test with a new SCP. Most were harmless&nbsp;
anomalous creatures or objects, like one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:19.380 --> 02:20:25.140
bigger experiments I was involved in, which&nbsp;
focused on SCP-999, this friendly gelatinous&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:25.140 --> 02:20:30.480
orange blob. Electrodes were hooked up to me&nbsp;
while I just sat calmly in a room with SCP-999.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:30.479 --> 02:20:36.240
Being around it made me feel great, the best I had&nbsp;
felt since arriving and joining D-Class. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:36.240 --> 02:20:42.479
I did hear one of the researchers making the&nbsp;
comment that SCP-999 was ‘not quite ready yet’.&nbsp;

02:20:43.680 --> 02:20:48.120
Even so, the Tickle Monster had given me a new&nbsp;
positive outlook on my role at the Foundation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:48.120 --> 02:20:53.399
It was odd work, but I could find a rhythm to&nbsp;
it that would hopefully make the time pass by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:53.399 --> 02:20:57.960
a little quicker. And if it meant getting out, it&nbsp;
was worth all the dirty looks from other classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:57.960 --> 02:21:03.359
of personnel. This was an outlook I didn’t want&nbsp;
to lose. After a month, we were told to take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:03.359 --> 02:21:08.160
pill that the guards handed out to us, something&nbsp;
called an amnestic that would make us all forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:08.160 --> 02:21:13.380
the previous month. But, wanting to hang on to my&nbsp;
newfound optimism, I secretly flushed mine down&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:13.380 --> 02:21:18.660
a toilet. In hindsight, I should have taken it.
My next big test quickly brought me back down a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:18.660 --> 02:21:25.620
peg. I was handed a katana, SCP-572, and instantly&nbsp;
felt like I was unstoppable. The sword made me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:25.620 --> 02:21:29.880
believe I was a powerful warrior, as if I could&nbsp;
maybe fight my way out of the Foundation facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:29.880 --> 02:21:35.220
with ease. Unfortunately, the moment I tried to&nbsp;
use it didn’t lead to freedom. I had an accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:35.220 --> 02:21:40.380
broke my arm and suffered a series of internal&nbsp;
fractures, all while the research team mocked me.&nbsp;

02:21:40.380 --> 02:21:46.740
Embarrassed, I felt like a total laughing stock.
Things only got worse from there. I had barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:46.740 --> 02:21:50.460
set foot outside of the infirmary before a&nbsp;
different team of personnel told me I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:50.460 --> 02:21:54.420
going to be assisting them with an important&nbsp;
task. They dragged me to an old wooden door&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:54.420 --> 02:21:59.399
after briefly explaining what was going on;&nbsp;
although I struggled to keep up. Beyond the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:59.399 --> 02:22:05.460
door – which they called SCP-2317 – was a&nbsp;
vast salt pan. Following their instructions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:05.460 --> 02:22:11.280
I acted as an assistant to the rest of the team as&nbsp;
they performed a strange ritual. I had to scatter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:11.280 --> 02:22:16.679
a mix of holy water and chicken blood around&nbsp;
a circle of seven stone pillars, then recite:&nbsp;

02:22:16.680 --> 02:22:22.920
“Blood for the Old Gods, Water for the New King.”
It was an unusual practice, to say the least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:22.920 --> 02:22:26.819
but apparently, I was helping keep a&nbsp;
powerful, vengeful demigod imprisoned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:26.819 --> 02:22:31.920
That did little to soften the news that I&nbsp;
heard when we returned through SCP-2317.&nbsp;

02:22:31.920 --> 02:22:36.060
While I was gone, one of the Foundation’s&nbsp;
security team had been infected by something&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:36.060 --> 02:22:42.000
known as SCP-2193, a phenomenon that makes&nbsp;
people believe that every month a large group&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:42.000 --> 02:22:48.120
of D-Class are to be ‘terminated’. When I heard,&nbsp;
I actually considered taking my amnestic for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:48.120 --> 02:22:53.040
month to forget just how many had been killed…
It had been a whole two months since I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:53.040 --> 02:22:57.180
actively started work as part of D-Class&nbsp;
Personnel, and there weren’t many of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:57.180 --> 02:23:02.281
fellow prisoners left. I was sent for a mandatory&nbsp;
psychological evaluation; the Foundation wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:02.280 --> 02:23:07.380
to know if I would still be able to perform tests&nbsp;
for them. I doubted it came from a place of actual&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:07.380 --> 02:23:12.120
concern, it’s not like they cared all that much&nbsp;
if the things I had seen were taking a toll on me.&nbsp;

02:23:12.120 --> 02:23:17.160
Sitting across from the researcher performing my&nbsp;
evaluation, I did my best impression of a normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:17.160 --> 02:23:21.960
person. I had to pretend like none of it was&nbsp;
getting to me, all the horrors and the close brush&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:21.960 --> 02:23:26.640
I’d almost had with death by Monthly Termination.&nbsp;
I wouldn’t have lied about it normally, But I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:26.640 --> 02:23:31.560
been told I had a month left before I was due to&nbsp;
be released. Apparently, not many D-Class made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:31.560 --> 02:23:36.360
that far, given the dangerous nature of testing.&nbsp;
All I could think about was how good it would&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:36.359 --> 02:23:41.280
feel to eat real food again; I started to daydream&nbsp;
about getting a cheeseburger on my first day out.&nbsp;

02:23:41.280 --> 02:23:46.500
Almost instantly, my hopes for freedom were&nbsp;
dashed the very next day. I was told I’d be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:46.500 --> 02:23:51.540
going on a longer assignment, this time it would&nbsp;
be an expedition into an anomalous location called&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:51.540 --> 02:23:58.560
SCP-432. The Foundation’s researchers outfitted&nbsp;
me with a flashlight, plus extra batteries. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:58.560 --> 02:24:03.060
headset and microphone linked to their control&nbsp;
center were placed over my ear, along with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:03.060 --> 02:24:07.320
camera unit mounted on my shoulder; they said&nbsp;
it would wirelessly transmit back to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:07.319 --> 02:24:12.120
and they’d see what I saw. I was also given a&nbsp;
couple of bottles of water, some energy bars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:12.120 --> 02:24:17.040
and a few sticks of luminous marker chalk.
But when they had said ‘anomalous location’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:17.040 --> 02:24:21.300
the last thing I’d been expecting was&nbsp;
to climb into a rusty metal cabinet.&nbsp;

02:24:21.300 --> 02:24:26.940
The next two weeks were like living inside a&nbsp;
nightmare. Inside the cabinet was a huge maze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:26.939 --> 02:24:33.179
a literal labyrinth all made out of the same&nbsp;
rusted steel of the exterior of SCP-432. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:33.180 --> 02:24:37.620
so dark, even with the use of my flashlight and&nbsp;
the few lightbulbs affixed to some of the walls&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:37.620 --> 02:24:42.479
within the Cabinet Maze. Before long, I ended&nbsp;
up losing my bearings, lost with no way out.&nbsp;

02:24:42.479 --> 02:24:46.200
But being trapped in the maze and the&nbsp;
dark wasn’t even the worst parts. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:46.200 --> 02:24:50.220
was something else in the labyrinth, some&nbsp;
kind of creature lurking through the metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:50.220 --> 02:24:55.020
corridors. My flashlight eventually ran&nbsp;
out of power, so I never actually saw it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:24:55.020 --> 02:24:59.760
but every time I tried to sleep, I’d just lay&nbsp;
awake, listening to its growls. I couldn’t help&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:00.300 --> 02:25:04.860
what kind of horrible thing was out there, and&nbsp;
might be looming over me in the dark. Even when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:04.859 --> 02:25:10.740
I opened my eyes, I still wouldn’t get to see it.
Eventually, another D-Class was sent into SCP-432.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:10.740 --> 02:25:15.120
By some miracle, he was able to not only find&nbsp;
me, but guide me back to the entrance of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:15.120 --> 02:25:20.819
Cabinet Maze. The moment I was free, I yelled at&nbsp;
the research team, demanding they let me go. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:20.819 --> 02:25:26.700
didn’t just mean let out of SCP-432, I meant free&nbsp;
from D-Class, from the Foundation, from all of it.&nbsp;

02:25:26.700 --> 02:25:30.960
I was sent for another psyche evaluation&nbsp;
and deemed to be fine, just suffering from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:30.960 --> 02:25:35.760
heightened stress. When they asked me if I’d been&nbsp;
taking my amnestics, I lied and told them yes.&nbsp;

02:25:35.760 --> 02:25:40.560
Ten days before I was set to be released and they&nbsp;
couldn’t help but assign me to one last test.&nbsp;

02:25:40.560 --> 02:25:46.680
“Don’t worry,” a researcher sarcastically assured&nbsp;
me. “This one will be nice and easy on you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:46.680 --> 02:25:51.180
you’ll just have to jump into a paddling&nbsp;
pool. You can do that, can’t you, D-2152?”&nbsp;

02:25:51.180 --> 02:25:56.940
The paddling pool in question was SCP-120.&nbsp;
Every time a different glow emanated from it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:25:56.939 --> 02:26:02.160
I was instructed to jump in. The exact second I&nbsp;
did, I found myself transported somewhere new.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:02.160 --> 02:26:07.019
I got The Himalayas on my first go, Greenland&nbsp;
and the Sahara Desert on the next two tries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:07.020 --> 02:26:10.920
Every one of the locations I ended up&nbsp;
at had some kind of Foundation facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:10.920 --> 02:26:16.800
established nearby. They’d pick me up, and I’d&nbsp;
be taken back to SCP-120 to continue testing.&nbsp;

02:26:16.800 --> 02:26:21.060
It felt like I was being deliberately,&nbsp;
intentionally tortured. The Foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:21.060 --> 02:26:25.081
was giving me these momentary glimpses&nbsp;
of freedom every time I was teleported by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:25.080 --> 02:26:29.640
SCP-120. It was a carrot on the end of a stick&nbsp;
that they were keeping just out of my reach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:29.640 --> 02:26:33.720
and I hated it. It made me realize that&nbsp;
I had never been there to work for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:33.720 --> 02:26:39.720
being part of D-Class Personnel wasn’t a&nbsp;
job. All I was to them was a human lab rat.&nbsp;

02:26:39.720 --> 02:26:44.820
Now it’s my last day. They’ve promised me that by&nbsp;
the end of today, I won’t be here anymore. I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:44.819 --> 02:26:49.679
want to be out, done with D-Class for good. The&nbsp;
Foundation’s researchers have given me one last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:49.680 --> 02:26:54.840
simple test to do before I go. They said if I&nbsp;
do, then I’ll be gone. All they want me to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:54.840 --> 02:26:59.819
is wear this amulet for someone called Doctor&nbsp;
Bright. How hard can that be? And they said&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:26:59.819 --> 02:27:04.679
